// Physics articles, part 2.3: particle physics, antimatter and radioactivity.
ADDART({id:"particles",t:"Particle Physics",c:"Science",i:"⚛️",w:"Particle physics",r:["atom","quantum","antimatter","relat","radioact","chaostheory"],
p:[["The smallest pieces","Particle physics studies the tiniest known building blocks of matter and the forces between them."],["The Standard Model","A theory called the Standard Model describes 17 fundamental particles and three of the four forces of nature."],["Giant machines","Scientists study particles by smashing them together in huge machines called particle accelerators."]],
l:"Particle physics is the branch of physics that studies the fundamental particles that make up matter and radiation, and the forces through which they interact. Everything around us is made of atoms, which are made of protons, neutrons and electrons; protons and neutrons are themselves made of smaller particles called quarks. The Standard Model of particle physics, developed in the 1960s and 1970s, describes these particles with remarkable accuracy, and its last missing piece, the Higgs boson, was discovered in 2012.",
s:[["Inside the atom","In 1897 J. J. Thomson discovered the electron. In 1911 Ernest Rutherford showed that atoms have a tiny, dense nucleus, and he later identified the proton; James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932. In the 1960s, experiments revealed that protons and neutrons are made of three quarks each, held together by particles called gluons."],["Matter particles","The Standard Model has 12 matter particles. Six are quarks, with names such as up, down and strange; two up quarks and a down quark make a proton. Six are leptons, including the electron and three kinds of ghostly neutrinos, which pass through almost everything; trillions of them stream through your body every second."],["Force particles","Forces are carried by particles called bosons. Photons carry the electromagnetic force, which holds atoms together and produces light. Gluons carry the strong force, which binds quarks. W and Z bosons carry the weak force, responsible for some kinds of radioactivity. Gravity is not included in the Standard Model."],["The Higgs boson","In 1964 Peter Higgs and others proposed an invisible field filling all of space that gives some particles their mass. Its sign would be a particle, the Higgs boson. On 4 July 2012, scientists at CERN announced they had found it at the Large Hadron Collider. Peter Higgs and François Englert won the 2013 Nobel Prize in Physics."]],
k:[["Fundamental particles","17 in the Standard Model"],["Matter particles","6 quarks and 6 leptons"],["Forces described","Electromagnetic, strong, weak (not gravity)"],["Largest accelerator","Large Hadron Collider, 27 km ring"],["Higgs boson found","2012"]],
d:[["1897","J. J. Thomson discovers the electron"],["1911","Rutherford discovers the atomic nucleus"],["1932","Chadwick discovers the neutron"],["1964","Quarks are proposed; the Higgs mechanism is proposed"],["1995","The top quark is discovered"],["2012","The Higgs boson is discovered at CERN"]],
s2:[["The Large Hadron Collider","The Large Hadron Collider at CERN, near Geneva on the border of Switzerland and France, is the world’s largest and most powerful particle accelerator. Protons race in opposite directions around a 27-kilometer underground ring at nearly the speed of light and collide up to a billion times a second. Giant detectors record the particles that fly out."],["CERN","CERN, the European Organization for Nuclear Research, was founded in 1954 to rebuild European science after World War II and to bring countries together. Today it has more than 20 member states and scientists from over 100 countries work there. The World Wide Web was invented at CERN by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989 to help physicists share information."],["Neutrinos","Neutrinos are among the most common particles in the universe but are extremely hard to detect, since they rarely interact with anything. Huge detectors, such as Super-Kamiokande in Japan, a tank holding 50,000 metric tons of ultra-pure water deep underground, and IceCube, buried in the ice at the South Pole, catch a few of them. Experiments showed that neutrinos have a tiny mass, winning the 2015 Nobel Prize."],["Particles from space","Cosmic rays, high-energy particles from space, constantly hit Earth’s atmosphere and create showers of new particles. Before accelerators, scientists discovered new particles such as the positron and the muon in cosmic rays, using detectors on mountaintops and in balloons."],["What the Standard Model cannot explain","The Standard Model leaves big questions unanswered. It does not include gravity, cannot explain dark matter or dark energy, and does not say why there is more matter than antimatter in the universe. Physicists are searching for new particles and theories, such as supersymmetry or string theory, that might go beyond it."],["Uses of particle physics","Particle physics has led to many practical tools. Accelerators are used to treat cancer with beams of protons or X-rays, to make medical isotopes and to study materials. PET scanners in hospitals use antimatter. Detector technology has improved medical imaging, airport security and computing."]],
sp:["Up, down, charm and strange","The physicist Murray Gell-Mann named quarks after a word in James Joyce’s novel Finnegans Wake: Three quarks for Muster Mark! The six kinds, called flavors, have playful names: up, down, charm, strange, top and bottom. Quarks also have a property called color, unrelated to real colors, which describes how they interact through the strong force."],
g:[["Quark","A fundamental particle that makes up protons and neutrons"],["Lepton","A family of fundamental particles that includes the electron and neutrinos"],["Boson","A particle that carries a force"],["Particle accelerator","A machine that speeds up particles to high energies"]],
f:["Protons are made of three quarks.","The Large Hadron Collider has a 27-kilometer ring.","The World Wide Web was invented at CERN."],
fun:"Trillions of neutrinos from the Sun pass through your body every second without you noticing."});

ADDART({id:"antimatter",t:"Antimatter",c:"Science",i:"🔄",w:"Antimatter",r:["particles","bigbang","quantum","energyphys"],
p:[["A mirror of matter","Antimatter is made of particles that have the same mass as ordinary particles but opposite charge."],["Mutual destruction","When matter and antimatter meet, they destroy each other in a flash of energy."],["A cosmic puzzle","The Big Bang should have made equal amounts of both, yet our universe is made almost entirely of matter."]],
l:"Antimatter is matter made of antiparticles, partners of ordinary particles with the same mass but opposite electric charge and other properties. The antiparticle of the electron is the positron, and of the proton, the antiproton. When a particle meets its antiparticle, both are destroyed, or annihilated, and their mass is turned completely into energy. Antimatter is made naturally in cosmic rays and radioactive decay, and in tiny amounts in laboratories, and it is used in medical PET scanners.",
s:[["A prediction from equations","In 1928 the British physicist Paul Dirac wrote an equation combining quantum mechanics and relativity to describe the electron. It had two solutions, one of which seemed to describe an electron with positive charge. Dirac boldly suggested that such antiparticles really exist."],["The positron","In 1932 Carl Anderson, studying cosmic rays in the United States, photographed the track of a particle with the mass of an electron but curving the opposite way in a magnetic field. It was the positron, the first antiparticle discovered. Dirac and Anderson both won Nobel Prizes for this work."],["Annihilation","When matter and antimatter meet, their entire mass turns into energy, according to Einstein’s E = mc². An electron and positron usually turn into two gamma rays flying in opposite directions. Because all the mass is converted, annihilation releases far more energy per kilogram than any chemical or nuclear reaction."],["Making antimatter","Physicists make antiprotons by smashing protons into targets at high energy. In 1995 scientists at CERN created the first atoms of antihydrogen, made of an antiproton and a positron. Since 2010, experiments have trapped antihydrogen atoms for minutes using magnetic fields, and in 2023 showed that antimatter falls downward under gravity, like ordinary matter."]],
k:[["First predicted","1928 by Paul Dirac"],["First discovered","Positron, 1932, by Carl Anderson"],["First anti-atoms","Antihydrogen, 1995, at CERN"],["Annihilation","Turns all mass into energy"],["Medical use","PET scanners"]],
d:[["1928","Dirac predicts antimatter"],["1932","Anderson discovers the positron"],["1955","The antiproton is discovered"],["1995","First antihydrogen atoms are made"],["2010","Antihydrogen atoms are trapped"],["2023","Experiment shows antimatter falls under gravity"]],
s2:[["PET scans","Positron emission tomography, or PET, is a medical imaging method that uses antimatter. Patients receive a substance containing a radioactive isotope that gives off positrons. When the positrons meet electrons in the body, they annihilate, producing gamma rays that a scanner detects, revealing activity in organs such as the brain and the growth of cancers."],["Where is all the antimatter?","Physicists believe the Big Bang should have created matter and antimatter in equal amounts, which would then have annihilated, leaving only light. Yet the universe is full of matter. Something must have favored matter slightly, perhaps by about one extra particle in a billion. Explaining this imbalance is one of the greatest unsolved problems in physics."],["Antimatter in nature","Antimatter is produced in tiny amounts all the time. Cosmic rays striking the atmosphere create it, and some radioactive substances, such as potassium-40 found in bananas and in our bodies, occasionally give off positrons. Thunderstorms have been found to produce bursts of positrons, detected by satellites."],["Is antimatter fuel possible?","Antimatter would be the most powerful fuel imaginable: a gram annihilating with a gram of matter would release about as much energy as a large nuclear bomb. But making it is extremely difficult and costly. All the antimatter ever made at CERN amounts to only billionths of a gram, far too little to power anything."],["Storing antimatter","Because antimatter is destroyed when it touches ordinary matter, it cannot be kept in a normal container. Scientists hold charged antiparticles in a vacuum using electric and magnetic fields in devices called Penning traps. In 2024 CERN researchers tested a portable trap by driving it around the laboratory site on a truck, carrying ordinary protons, as a step toward moving antiprotons to other laboratories."],["Testing nature’s symmetry","Physicists compare hydrogen and antihydrogen atoms with extreme precision, measuring the light they absorb and how they fall. Any tiny difference could reveal new physics and help explain why the universe contains more matter than antimatter. So far, they behave identically."]],
sp:["Star Trek and science","Science fiction often uses antimatter to power starships, as in Star Trek’s warp drive. While the physics of annihilation is real, the engineering is far beyond us. To fuel even a short trip, a spacecraft would need metric tons of antimatter, but humanity has made less than a millionth of a gram in total."],
g:[["Antiparticle","A particle with the same mass as another but opposite charge"],["Positron","The antiparticle of the electron"],["Annihilation","The destruction of a particle and its antiparticle, releasing energy"],["Antihydrogen","An atom made of an antiproton and a positron"]],
f:["The positron was the first antiparticle discovered, in 1932.","PET scanners in hospitals use antimatter.","Antimatter falls downward under gravity, as an experiment showed in 2023."],
fun:"Bananas give off a tiny amount of antimatter, because of the radioactive potassium they contain."});

ADDART({id:"radioact",t:"Radioactivity",c:"Science",i:"☢️",w:"Radioactive decay",r:["curie","atom","nuclear","particles","fusion","chienshiungwu"],
p:[["Unstable atoms","Radioactivity happens when the nucleus of an unstable atom breaks down and gives off energy as radiation."],["Discovered in 1896","Henri Becquerel discovered radioactivity, and Marie and Pierre Curie studied it and found new radioactive elements."],["Useful and dangerous","Radiation is used in medicine, energy and dating ancient objects, but large doses are harmful to living things."]],
l:"Radioactivity, or radioactive decay, is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by giving off radiation. Some elements, such as uranium and radium, are naturally radioactive, and radioactive forms of common elements exist too. Radiation can be alpha particles, beta particles or gamma rays. Discovered in 1896, radioactivity revealed that atoms have an inner structure and led to nuclear power, cancer treatments, radiocarbon dating and nuclear weapons.",
s:[["Discovery","In 1896 the French physicist Henri Becquerel found that uranium salts darkened a photographic plate wrapped in black paper, even without sunlight. Marie Curie named the effect radioactivity. With Pierre Curie, she discovered two new radioactive elements, polonium and radium, in 1898. The three shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Kinds of radiation","Alpha particles, made of two protons and two neutrons, are stopped by a sheet of paper or skin. Beta particles are fast electrons or positrons and can be stopped by a few millimeters of aluminum. Gamma rays are high-energy light that needs thick lead or concrete to block. Each kind affects living tissue differently."],["Half-life","Each radioactive substance decays at its own rate, measured by its half-life: the time it takes for half of the atoms in a sample to decay. Some half-lives are fractions of a second; carbon-14’s is about 5,730 years; uranium-238’s is about 4.5 billion years, roughly the age of Earth."],["Background radiation","We are all exposed to low levels of natural radiation every day, from rocks, soil, the air, cosmic rays and even our own bodies, which contain radioactive potassium and carbon. A radioactive gas called radon, which seeps from the ground in some areas, is the largest natural source for most people."]],
k:[["Discovered","1896 by Henri Becquerel"],["Main types","Alpha, beta, gamma"],["Carbon-14 half-life","About 5,730 years"],["Unit of dose","Sievert (Sv)"],["Warning symbol","The trefoil, introduced in 1946"]],
d:[["1895","Wilhelm Röntgen discovers X-rays"],["1896","Becquerel discovers radioactivity"],["1898","The Curies discover polonium and radium"],["1902","Rutherford and Soddy explain radioactive decay"],["1949","Willard Libby develops radiocarbon dating"],["1986","Chernobyl nuclear disaster"]],
s2:[["Radiocarbon dating","Living things take in carbon, including a small amount of radioactive carbon-14. When they die, they stop taking it in, and the carbon-14 slowly decays. By measuring how much remains, scientists can tell how long ago a plant or animal died, up to about 50,000 years. Willard Libby developed the method in 1949 and won the 1960 Nobel Prize in Chemistry."],["Medicine","Radiation is used to diagnose and treat disease. Radioactive tracers help doctors see how organs work, and radiotherapy uses beams of radiation to destroy cancer cells. Radiation is also used to sterilize medical equipment and to kill bacteria in some foods."],["Dangers","High doses of radiation damage living cells and DNA, causing burns, radiation sickness and an increased risk of cancer. Marie Curie died in 1934 of a blood disorder probably caused by years of exposure; her notebooks are still radioactive and are kept in lead-lined boxes. Today workers wear badges to measure their exposure."],["Nuclear accidents","In 1986 an explosion at the Chernobyl nuclear power plant in Soviet Ukraine released large amounts of radioactive material over Europe, and the area around it is still restricted. In 2011 a tsunami caused meltdowns at the Fukushima Daiichi plant in Japan. These disasters led to stricter safety rules worldwide."],["Energy from decay","Heat from radioactive decay deep inside Earth helps keep its core hot and drives volcanoes and plate movements. Spacecraft such as the Voyager probes and the Curiosity and Perseverance rovers on Mars use generators that turn the heat of decaying plutonium into electricity."],["Measuring radiation","Geiger counters detect radiation by the clicks it causes in a gas-filled tube. The effect of radiation on the body is measured in sieverts. A typical person receives about 2 to 3 millisieverts a year from natural sources; a chest X-ray gives about 0.1 millisieverts."]],
sp:["The radium craze","After radium was discovered, people were fascinated by its glow. In the early 1900s, it was added to drinks, toothpaste, cosmetics and watch dials that glowed in the dark. Many young women who painted radium watch dials, licking their brushes to make a fine point, later suffered terrible illnesses. Their court cases in the 1920s led to new safety laws for workers."],
g:[["Radioactive decay","The breakdown of an unstable atomic nucleus"],["Half-life","The time for half of a radioactive sample to decay"],["Isotope","A form of an element with a different number of neutrons"],["Sievert","A unit measuring the effect of radiation on the body"]],
f:["Radioactivity was discovered in 1896.","Carbon-14 dating works for objects up to about 50,000 years old.","Marie Curie’s notebooks are still radioactive."],
fun:"Marie Curie’s notebooks are still so radioactive that they are kept in lead-lined boxes."});

