// Physics articles, part 2.4: nuclear fusion and superconductivity.
ADDART({id:"fusion",t:"Nuclear Fusion",c:"Science",i:"☀️",w:"Nuclear fusion",r:["sun","stars","nuclear","particles","energyphys","kilonovae","nucleosynthesis"],
p:[["The power of the stars","Nuclear fusion is the process that powers the Sun and other stars, joining light atoms into heavier ones."],["Enormous energy","Fusion releases huge amounts of energy from small amounts of fuel, without producing greenhouse gases."],["A goal on Earth","Scientists around the world are working to build fusion power plants, but none yet produces electricity for the grid."]],
l:"Nuclear fusion is a reaction in which two light atomic nuclei combine to form a heavier nucleus, releasing a great deal of energy. Fusion powers the Sun and all stars, where hydrogen is fused into helium at temperatures of millions of degrees. On Earth, scientists are trying to harness fusion to generate clean electricity, using heavy forms of hydrogen as fuel. It is extremely difficult, because the fuel must be heated to over 100 million degrees and held together long enough for fusion to happen.",
s:[["How fusion works","Atomic nuclei are positively charged, so they repel each other. To fuse, they must collide at very high speeds, which requires extremely high temperatures. When light nuclei fuse, the new nucleus weighs slightly less than the original ones; the missing mass becomes energy, following Einstein’s E = mc²."],["Fusion in stars","In the Sun’s core, at about 15 million degrees Celsius and enormous pressure, hydrogen nuclei fuse into helium. Every second, the Sun turns about 600 million metric tons of hydrogen into helium, converting around 4 million metric tons of mass into energy. Larger stars fuse heavier elements, up to iron, in their cores."],["Fusion versus fission","Today’s nuclear power stations use fission, the splitting of heavy atoms such as uranium. Fusion joins light atoms instead. Fusion fuel is abundant, it produces no long-lasting high-level radioactive waste, and a fusion reactor cannot run out of control the way a fission reactor can, since the reaction stops if conditions are disturbed."],["Holding a star in a box","No material can touch fuel at 100 million degrees. Tokamaks, doughnut-shaped machines first developed in the Soviet Union in the 1950s and 1960s, hold the hot, electrically charged gas, called plasma, with powerful magnetic fields. Another method, inertial confinement, uses lasers to crush tiny fuel pellets."]],
k:[["Fuel","Deuterium and tritium (heavy hydrogen)"],["Temperature needed on Earth","Over 100 million °C"],["Main machine types","Tokamaks, stellarators, laser fusion"],["Largest project","ITER, in France"],["Ignition first achieved","December 2022, National Ignition Facility, USA"]],
d:[["1920","Arthur Eddington suggests stars are powered by fusing hydrogen"],["1939","Hans Bethe explains the fusion reactions in stars"],["1952","First hydrogen bomb tested"],["1968","Soviet tokamak achieves record results"],["1997","JET in the United Kingdom sets a fusion power record"],["2022","National Ignition Facility achieves ignition"]],
s2:[["Ignition","On 5 December 2022, the National Ignition Facility in California used 192 lasers to compress a tiny capsule of fuel, producing more fusion energy than the laser energy delivered to it, for the first time. This was called ignition. However, the lasers themselves used far more electricity than was produced, so practical power is still far off."],["ITER","ITER, being built in southern France, is a huge international tokamak backed by the European Union, China, India, Japan, South Korea, Russia and the United States. It aims to produce ten times more fusion power than the heating power put into the plasma. Its schedule has slipped; research operations are now planned to begin in the 2030s."],["Stellarators and private companies","Stellarators are twisted cousins of tokamaks; Germany’s Wendelstein 7-X has set records for long plasma pulses. Dozens of private companies, in the United States, the United Kingdom, China, Japan and elsewhere, are racing to build smaller, cheaper fusion machines, some aiming to deliver electricity in the 2030s."],["Records in Asia and Europe","China’s EAST tokamak and South Korea’s KSTAR have held hot plasmas for record lengths of time, and in 2025 EAST sustained a plasma for over 1,000 seconds. In 2023, the Joint European Torus, JET, in the United Kingdom, set a record for total fusion energy in its final experiments before closing."],["Fuel from the sea","Deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen, can be extracted from seawater; about one in every 6,400 hydrogen atoms in water is deuterium. Tritium is rare but can be made inside a reactor from lithium. The deuterium in a liter of seawater could, in principle, provide as much energy through fusion as burning hundreds of liters of gasoline."],["Fusion bombs","Uncontrolled fusion is the source of the enormous power of hydrogen bombs, first tested in 1952. They use a fission explosion to trigger fusion. The goal of fusion energy research is the opposite: a slow, controlled, safe release of energy for peaceful use."]],
sp:["Always 30 years away","Fusion researchers sometimes joke that practical fusion power has been 30 years away for the last 70 years. The challenges are huge, but progress has been real: experiments have reached the temperatures of stars, held plasmas for minutes and, in 2022, released more fusion energy than the lasers delivered. Whether fusion will power homes by the 2040s or later remains an open question."],
g:[["Fusion","Joining light atomic nuclei to form a heavier one"],["Fission","Splitting a heavy atomic nucleus"],["Plasma","A hot gas of charged particles, the fourth state of matter"],["Tokamak","A doughnut-shaped machine that holds plasma with magnetic fields"]],
f:["The Sun converts about 4 million metric tons of mass into energy every second.","Fusion ignition was first achieved in 2022.","Deuterium for fusion can be extracted from seawater."],
fun:"Every second, the Sun converts about 4 million metric tons of matter into energy."});

ADDART({id:"superconduct",t:"Superconductivity",c:"Science",i:"🧲",w:"Superconductivity",r:["magnet","elec","quantum","thermo","absolutezero"],
p:[["Zero resistance","Superconductors are materials that conduct electricity with no resistance at all when cooled below a certain temperature."],["Floating magnets","They push away magnetic fields, which can make magnets float above them."],["Used every day","Superconducting magnets are used in hospital MRI scanners, particle accelerators and some high-speed trains."]],
l:"Superconductivity is a state in which certain materials, when cooled below a critical temperature, carry electric current with absolutely no resistance and expel magnetic fields. Discovered in 1911, it allows electricity to flow forever in a loop without losing energy. Superconductors are used to make the powerful magnets in MRI scanners, particle accelerators and fusion experiments, and in some maglev trains. Scientists are searching for materials that superconduct at room temperature, which could transform technology.",
s:[["Discovery","In 1911 the Dutch physicist Heike Kamerlingh Onnes, who had learned to liquefy helium, cooled mercury to about 4 degrees above absolute zero and found that its electrical resistance suddenly vanished. He won the 1913 Nobel Prize in Physics. Many other metals were later found to superconduct at very low temperatures."],["Why resistance matters","In ordinary wires, electrons bump into atoms as they flow, losing energy as heat; this is resistance. In a superconductor, electrons pair up and move together without scattering. A current set flowing in a superconducting loop can keep circulating for years without a power source."],["The Meissner effect","In 1933 Walther Meissner and Robert Ochsenfeld found that superconductors push magnetic fields out of their interior. Because of this, a small magnet placed above a cooled superconductor floats in midair, a striking demonstration often shown in science classes."],["How it works","In 1957 John Bardeen, Leon Cooper and Robert Schrieffer explained superconductivity in metals with the BCS theory: vibrations in the material bind electrons into pairs, called Cooper pairs, which move through the metal without resistance. The theory won the 1972 Nobel Prize in Physics, making Bardeen the only person to win the physics prize twice."]],
k:[["Discovered","1911 by Heike Kamerlingh Onnes"],["Key properties","Zero resistance, expulsion of magnetic fields"],["Classic theory","BCS theory (1957)"],["High-temperature superconductors","Discovered 1986"],["Main uses","MRI scanners, particle accelerators, maglev trains"]],
d:[["1911","Kamerlingh Onnes discovers superconductivity"],["1933","The Meissner effect is discovered"],["1957","BCS theory explains superconductivity"],["1986","Bednorz and Müller discover high-temperature superconductors"],["1987","Superconductors working above the boiling point of liquid nitrogen are found"],["2023","Claims of a room-temperature superconductor, LK-99, are not confirmed"]],
s2:[["High-temperature superconductors","In 1986 Georg Bednorz and Alex Müller in Switzerland discovered that certain ceramic materials made of copper oxides superconduct at much higher temperatures. Within a year, scientists found materials that work above minus 196 degrees Celsius, the temperature of liquid nitrogen, which is far cheaper than liquid helium. Bednorz and Müller won the 1987 Nobel Prize."],["MRI scanners","Magnetic resonance imaging scanners in hospitals use superconducting coils cooled by liquid helium to create very strong, steady magnetic fields. These let doctors see detailed pictures of soft tissues such as the brain, muscles and organs without using harmful radiation. MRI is the most common everyday use of superconductivity."],["Big science","The Large Hadron Collider uses more than 1,200 superconducting magnets, cooled to 1.9 degrees above absolute zero, to steer protons around its ring. The ITER fusion experiment uses giant superconducting magnets to hold its plasma. Superconducting circuits are also at the heart of many quantum computers."],["Maglev trains","Some magnetic levitation trains use superconducting magnets to float above the track and travel without wheels touching the rails. Japan’s superconducting maglev set a world speed record of 603 kilometers per hour in 2015, and a line between Tokyo and Nagoya is under construction."],["The search for room temperature","A superconductor that worked at room temperature and normal pressure could make power lines without losses, cheaper MRI scanners and better electronics. Some hydrogen-rich materials superconduct near room temperature, but only when squeezed at enormous pressures. In 2023 claims about a material called LK-99 went viral online, but other labs found it was not a superconductor."],["Power cables","Superconducting cables can carry several times more electricity than copper cables of the same size, with almost no loss. They are being tested in cities, including in Germany, South Korea, Japan, China and the United States, to deliver power in crowded areas where there is little room for new lines."]],
sp:["A floating magnet","One of the most famous physics demonstrations is a small magnet hovering above a dark disk of high-temperature superconductor cooled with liquid nitrogen, wisps of cold vapor drifting around it. Because the superconductor expels and pins magnetic fields, the magnet stays locked in place, and it can even be spun or pushed along a track like a tiny maglev train."],
g:[["Superconductor","A material that conducts electricity with zero resistance when cold enough"],["Resistance","Opposition to the flow of electric current"],["Critical temperature","The temperature below which a material becomes superconducting"],["Meissner effect","The expulsion of magnetic fields from a superconductor"]],
f:["Superconductivity was discovered in 1911.","MRI scanners use superconducting magnets.","John Bardeen is the only person to win the Nobel Prize in Physics twice."],
fun:"A current in a superconducting loop can flow for years without any power source."});

;
