// Artificial intelligence articles, part 1: the history of AI, algorithms and machine learning.
ADDART({id:"aihistory",t:"The History of Artificial Intelligence",c:"Technology",i:"🧠",w:"History of artificial intelligence",r:["ai","comp","mlearn","neural","llm"],
p:[["Can machines think?","In 1950 Alan Turing asked whether machines could think and proposed a test to find out."],["Booms and winters","AI research has gone through periods of excitement and funding, followed by disappointment, called AI winters."],["The deep learning era","Since the 2010s, neural networks trained on huge amounts of data have transformed AI."]],
l:"The history of artificial intelligence stretches from ancient myths of mechanical servants to today’s systems that can write, draw and hold conversations. As a field of science, AI began in the 1950s, when researchers set out to build machines that could reason, learn and solve problems. Progress came in waves, with bursts of optimism followed by AI winters when results fell short. Since about 2012, advances in neural networks, computing power and data have produced dramatic breakthroughs.",
s:[["Early dreams","Ancient Greek myths told of Talos, a giant bronze guardian, and many cultures imagined artificial beings. In the 1800s Ada Lovelace wrote that Charles Babbage’s Analytical Engine could work on things other than numbers, such as music, though she doubted it could originate ideas. The word robot came from Karel Čapek’s 1920 play R.U.R."],["Alan Turing","The British mathematician Alan Turing laid the foundations of computer science in 1936 with his idea of a universal machine. In 1950 he published Computing Machinery and Intelligence, proposing the imitation game, now called the Turing test: if a person chatting by text cannot tell a machine from a human, the machine might be said to think."],["The birth of AI","The term artificial intelligence was coined by John McCarthy for a summer workshop at Dartmouth College in the United States in 1956, organized with Marvin Minsky, Claude Shannon and Nathaniel Rochester. Early programs proved math theorems, played checkers and solved puzzles, and researchers predicted that human-level AI was only decades away."],["AI winters","Early AI struggled with real-world problems such as understanding language and recognizing objects, which turned out to be far harder than playing games. Funding was cut in the 1970s and again in the late 1980s, periods known as AI winters. In between, expert systems, programs encoding the rules of human specialists, were briefly popular in business."]],
k:[["Term coined","1956, by John McCarthy"],["Turing test proposed","1950"],["AI winters","Mid-1970s and late 1980s to early 1990s"],["Deep learning breakthrough","2012 (AlexNet)"],["Key pioneers","Turing, McCarthy, Minsky, Hinton, LeCun, Bengio, Fei-Fei Li"]],
d:[["1950","Turing proposes the imitation game"],["1956","Dartmouth workshop names artificial intelligence"],["1966","ELIZA chatbot is created"],["1997","IBM’s Deep Blue defeats chess champion Garry Kasparov"],["2016","DeepMind’s AlphaGo defeats Go champion Lee Sedol"],["2022","ChatGPT is released to the public"]],
s2:[["ELIZA","In 1966 Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT created ELIZA, a simple program that imitated a therapist by turning a person’s sentences back into questions. Although it understood nothing, many users felt it understood them. Weizenbaum was troubled by this and became a critic of trusting computers too much."],["Machines beat champions","In 1997 IBM’s Deep Blue defeated the world chess champion Garry Kasparov in a six-game match. In 2011 IBM’s Watson won the television quiz show Jeopardy!. In 2016 DeepMind’s AlphaGo beat Lee Sedol, one of the world’s best players of Go, a game long thought too complex for computers."],["The deep learning revolution","In 2012 a neural network called AlexNet, built by Alex Krizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever and Geoffrey Hinton, won an image-recognition contest by a huge margin, using graphics chips to train on the ImageNet database of over a million labeled images created by Fei-Fei Li’s team. This sparked a boom in deep learning across speech, vision and language."],["Language models","In 2017 Google researchers introduced the transformer, a new neural network design. It led to large language models, trained on vast amounts of text. OpenAI’s ChatGPT, released in November 2022, gained 100 million users within about two months, and similar chatbots from many companies and countries followed."],["Science and medicine","AI has begun to change science. In 2020 DeepMind’s AlphaFold predicted the 3D shapes of proteins with remarkable accuracy, solving a problem biologists had worked on for 50 years. In 2024 Demis Hassabis and John Jumper shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it, and Geoffrey Hinton and John Hopfield won the Nobel Prize in Physics for foundational work on neural networks."],["Debates about the future","As AI grows more capable, people debate its effects on jobs, creativity, privacy, fairness and safety. Some researchers believe AI could one day match or exceed human abilities in most tasks; others are skeptical. Governments have begun to create laws and safety institutes to guide its development."]],
sp:["The Turing test","Turing imagined a judge exchanging typed messages with two hidden players, one human and one machine. If the judge could not reliably tell which was which, the machine would pass. Turing predicted that by about the year 2000, computers would fool judges at least some of the time. Modern chatbots can often do so, but many scientists argue that passing the test does not prove a machine truly understands."],
g:[["Artificial intelligence","Computer systems that perform tasks normally requiring human intelligence"],["AI winter","A period of reduced funding and interest in AI research"],["Expert system","A program that uses rules from human experts to solve problems"],["Deep learning","Machine learning using neural networks with many layers"]],
f:["The term artificial intelligence was coined in 1956.","Deep Blue beat the world chess champion in 1997.","Two 2024 Nobel Prizes recognized work related to AI."],
fun:"ELIZA, a chatbot from 1966, understood nothing, yet many users believed it understood them."});

ADDART({id:"algorithm",t:"Algorithms",c:"Technology",i:"🧮",w:"Algorithm",r:["comp","alg","mlearn","internet","goldage","graphtheory","sorting","databases","softwarebugs"],
p:[["Step-by-step instructions","An algorithm is a precise set of steps for solving a problem or completing a task."],["Everywhere in computing","Every computer program is built from algorithms, from searching the web to recommending videos."],["An ancient idea","The word comes from the name of al-Khwarizmi, a mathematician in 9th-century Baghdad."]],
l:"An algorithm is a finite list of clear, step-by-step instructions for solving a problem or carrying out a task. A cooking recipe, the method for long division and directions to a friend’s house are all algorithms. In computing, algorithms tell computers exactly what to do, and they power everything from calculators and maps to search engines, social media feeds and artificial intelligence. Designing fast, correct algorithms is a central part of computer science.",
s:[["Origins","The word algorithm comes from the Latinized name of Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi, a Persian mathematician who worked at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad around 820. His books explained how to calculate with Hindu-Arabic numerals and solve equations. Much earlier, around 300 BCE, Euclid described an algorithm for finding the greatest common divisor of two numbers that is still used today."],["What makes an algorithm","A good algorithm has clear inputs and outputs, each step is precise and unambiguous, and it finishes after a finite number of steps. It must also be correct, giving the right answer for every valid input. Algorithms can be written in everyday language, in diagrams called flowcharts or in programming languages."],["Searching and sorting","Two of the most common jobs are searching and sorting. Linear search checks items one by one. Binary search, which works on sorted lists, checks the middle item and throws away half the list each time, so it can find one name among a million in about 20 steps. Sorting algorithms, such as merge sort and quicksort, put data in order."],["Measuring speed","Computer scientists compare algorithms by how their running time grows as the input gets bigger. An algorithm that checks every pair of items in a list becomes slow very quickly as the list grows, while a smarter one may barely slow down. Choosing the right algorithm can make the difference between an answer in a second and one that would take years."]],
k:[["Named after","Al-Khwarizmi (c. 780–850)"],["Oldest known in use","Euclid’s algorithm, c. 300 BCE"],["Key tasks","Searching, sorting, finding routes, compressing data"],["Famous search engine method","PageRank (1998)"],["Important question","P versus NP, unsolved"]],
d:[["c. 300 BCE","Euclid describes his algorithm for common divisors"],["c. 820","Al-Khwarizmi writes on calculation and algebra"],["1843","Ada Lovelace publishes an algorithm for Babbage’s engine"],["1936","Alan Turing defines computation with the Turing machine"],["1959","Edsger Dijkstra publishes his shortest-path algorithm"],["1998","Google’s PageRank algorithm is described"]],
s2:[["Ada Lovelace","In 1843 Ada Lovelace published notes on Charles Babbage’s planned Analytical Engine, including a step-by-step method for calculating a series of numbers called Bernoulli numbers. It is often described as the first published computer program, written for a machine that was never completed."],["Finding the shortest route","In 1956 the Dutch computer scientist Edsger Dijkstra designed, reportedly in about 20 minutes at a café, an algorithm for finding the shortest path between two points in a network, published in 1959. Variants of it help map apps find routes and help data travel across the internet."],["Ranking the web","In 1998 Larry Page and Sergey Brin described PageRank, an algorithm that ranked web pages by how many other important pages linked to them. It helped make Google’s search engine more useful than its rivals. Modern search engines use many algorithms together."],["Recommendation algorithms","Social media apps, video sites and online shops use algorithms that learn from what people click, watch and buy to recommend more content. They can help people find things they enjoy, but critics worry they can spread misinformation, encourage endless scrolling or show people only views they already agree with."],["Fairness","Algorithms are written and trained by people and can reflect human biases. If an algorithm used for hiring, lending or policing learns from biased past data, it may treat some groups unfairly. Researchers and governments are working on ways to test algorithms for fairness and make their decisions explainable."],["Unsolved problems","Some problems seem to have no fast algorithm. The famous P versus NP question asks whether every problem whose answer can be checked quickly can also be solved quickly. It is one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems, each with a reward of one million US dollars for a solution."]],
sp:["An algorithm for every day","People follow algorithms all the time without calling them that. Tying shoelaces, brushing teeth, folding a paper airplane and solving a Rubik’s Cube all follow step-by-step methods. Teaching computers to do things means breaking tasks down into steps so precise that a machine, which cannot guess what we mean, can follow them exactly."],
g:[["Algorithm","A precise, step-by-step method for solving a problem"],["Binary search","A fast search that repeatedly halves a sorted list"],["Flowchart","A diagram showing the steps of a process"],["Program","Instructions written in a language a computer can run"]],
f:["The word algorithm comes from the name of al-Khwarizmi.","Binary search can find one item among a million in about 20 steps.","Euclid’s algorithm is more than 2,000 years old."],
fun:"Edsger Dijkstra is said to have invented his famous shortest-route algorithm in about 20 minutes at a café."});

ADDART({id:"mlearn",t:"Machine Learning",c:"Technology",i:"📈",w:"Machine learning",r:["ai","neural","algorithm","stats","llm"],
p:[["Learning from data","Machine learning is a kind of AI in which computers learn patterns from examples instead of being given exact rules."],["Everyday uses","It powers spam filters, voice assistants, translation apps, product recommendations and fraud detection."],["Three main types","The main kinds are supervised learning, unsupervised learning and reinforcement learning."]],
l:"Machine learning is a branch of artificial intelligence in which computer systems improve at a task by learning from data, rather than following instructions written step by step by a programmer. A machine learning model is trained on many examples, such as photos labeled cat or dog, and adjusts itself until it can make accurate predictions on new examples it has never seen. Most modern AI, from face recognition to chatbots, is built using machine learning.",
s:[["Rules versus learning","Writing exact rules for some tasks is almost impossible. How would you describe in rules every way a handwritten 7 can look? Machine learning instead shows the computer thousands of examples and lets it discover the patterns itself. The term was popularized by Arthur Samuel of IBM, whose checkers program learned to play better than he did in the 1950s."],["Supervised learning","In supervised learning, the model learns from examples that come with the right answers, called labels. Shown thousands of emails marked spam or not spam, it learns to sort new emails. It is used to recognize speech, detect diseases in scans and predict house prices."],["Unsupervised learning","In unsupervised learning, the data has no labels, and the model looks for structure on its own, such as grouping customers with similar shopping habits or finding unusual patterns that might reveal fraud. It can reveal patterns people had not noticed."],["Reinforcement learning","In reinforcement learning, a program learns by trial and error, receiving rewards for good actions and penalties for bad ones. It has learned to play video games and board games at superhuman levels and to control robots. AlphaGo used it to master the game of Go by playing millions of games against itself."]],
k:[["Main types","Supervised, unsupervised, reinforcement"],["Term popularized","1959, by Arthur Samuel"],["Needs","Data, algorithms and computing power"],["Common tools","Neural networks, decision trees, regression"],["Key risk","Learning bias from biased data"]],
d:[["1952","Arthur Samuel begins his self-improving checkers program"],["1958","Frank Rosenblatt builds the perceptron"],["1986","Backpropagation for training neural networks is popularized"],["2009","ImageNet database is released"],["2012","Deep learning wins the ImageNet contest"],["2016","AlphaGo defeats Lee Sedol"]],
s2:[["Training and testing","To check that a model really works, data is split into a training set, used to learn, and a test set, kept aside. If a model does very well on training data but poorly on new data, it has overfitted, memorizing the examples instead of learning general patterns, like a student who memorizes answers without understanding."],["Data is key","Machine learning needs large amounts of good data. If the data is too small, unrepresentative or biased, the model will be too. A face-recognition system trained mainly on photos of one group of people, for example, may work worse for others. Collecting and checking data is often the hardest part of a project."],["Everyday machine learning","Machine learning recommends songs and videos, suggests the next word as you type, translates languages, filters spam, recognizes faces to unlock phones, detects credit card fraud and predicts traffic. In science, it helps find new medicines, forecast weather and analyze telescope images."],["Weather forecasting","In the 2020s, machine learning weather models trained on decades of past weather began to rival or beat traditional forecasting systems on many measures, producing forecasts in minutes rather than hours on supercomputers. Weather services now use them alongside physics-based models."],["Explainability","Many powerful models, especially large neural networks, are hard to interpret: they give an answer but not a clear reason. In areas such as medicine, law and lending, people need to understand decisions. The field of explainable AI develops ways to show which features led a model to its output."],["Learning in medicine","Machine learning systems can analyze medical images such as X-rays, eye scans and skin photos, sometimes as accurately as specialists for specific tasks. They are used as aids to doctors, not replacements, and must be carefully tested on patients from many backgrounds before use."]],
sp:["Teaching a computer to see cats","In 2012 researchers at Google trained a huge neural network on 10 million unlabeled frames from YouTube videos. Without being told what a cat was, the network developed a detector that responded strongly to cat faces, simply because cats appeared so often. The experiment became famous as an example of a machine learning to recognize things on its own."],
g:[["Machine learning","AI that learns patterns from data"],["Model","The program produced by training, which makes predictions"],["Training data","Examples used to teach a model"],["Overfitting","When a model memorizes examples instead of learning general patterns"]],
f:["Arthur Samuel’s checkers program learned to beat him.","Most modern AI is built using machine learning.","Biased data can produce biased models."],
fun:"A Google neural network learned to recognize cats in 2012 just by watching YouTube videos."});

;
/* data/ai-2.js */
// Artificial intelligence articles, part 2: neural networks, large language models and computer vision.
ADDART({id:"neural",t:"Neural Networks",c:"Technology",i:"🕸️",w:"Neural network (machine learning)",r:["mlearn","brain","ai","llm","compvision"],
p:[["Inspired by the brain","Artificial neural networks are computer systems loosely inspired by the networks of nerve cells in the brain."],["Layers of neurons","They are made of layers of simple units that pass numbers to one another and adjust their connections as they learn."],["Deep learning","Networks with many layers, called deep neural networks, power most of today’s advanced AI."]],
l:"An artificial neural network is a computing system made of many simple connected units, called artificial neurons, arranged in layers. Each connection has a strength, or weight, that is adjusted during training so the network learns to turn inputs, such as the pixels of an image, into useful outputs, such as the word cat. Neural networks were first proposed in the 1940s and 1950s, but only became powerful in the 2010s, with large datasets and fast computer chips. They now drive image recognition, speech recognition, translation and chatbots.",
s:[["Artificial neurons","An artificial neuron receives numbers from other neurons, multiplies each by a weight, adds them up and passes the result through a simple function that decides how strongly to fire. A single neuron is very simple, but millions or billions connected together can represent extremely complex patterns."],["Layers","A network has an input layer, which receives data, one or more hidden layers that transform it, and an output layer, which gives the answer. In an image network, early layers might detect edges, middle layers shapes such as eyes or wheels, and later layers whole objects. Deep networks may have dozens or hundreds of layers."],["How networks learn","Training starts with random weights. The network makes a guess, its error is measured, and a method called backpropagation works backward through the layers, nudging every weight slightly in the direction that reduces the error. Repeated over millions of examples, these small adjustments add up to a network that performs well."],["A long history","In 1943 Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts described a mathematical model of neurons. In 1958 Frank Rosenblatt built the perceptron, a machine that could learn to recognize simple shapes. Interest faded when its limits were shown, but in the 1980s researchers including Geoffrey Hinton, David Rumelhart and Ronald Williams popularized backpropagation, reviving the field."]],
k:[["First model","McCulloch and Pitts, 1943"],["Training method","Backpropagation and gradient descent"],["Hardware","Graphics processing units (GPUs) and AI chips"],["Famous types","Convolutional networks, recurrent networks, transformers"],["Turing Award","2018: Hinton, LeCun and Bengio"]],
d:[["1943","McCulloch and Pitts model artificial neurons"],["1958","Rosenblatt builds the perceptron"],["1986","Backpropagation paper by Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams"],["1989","Yann LeCun trains networks to read handwritten digits"],["2012","AlexNet triggers the deep learning boom"],["2017","The transformer design is introduced"]],
s2:[["Convolutional networks","Convolutional neural networks, developed by Yann LeCun and others, scan an image with small filters to detect patterns wherever they appear. In the 1990s LeCun’s networks read handwritten numbers on bank checks. Today convolutional networks help recognize faces, read medical scans and guide self-driving cars."],["Recurrent networks and transformers","Language and speech come in sequences. Recurrent neural networks process sequences one step at a time, remembering what came before. In 2017 the transformer design replaced this with a mechanism called attention, which lets the network look at all words in a sentence at once and weigh how they relate. Transformers now power most language and many image models."],["Why GPUs matter","Training a neural network means doing an enormous number of multiplications. Graphics processing units, first designed for video games, can do thousands of these at once. Using GPUs, and later specialized AI chips, made it possible to train much bigger networks, and AI training now takes place in huge data centers."],["The godfathers of deep learning","Geoffrey Hinton, Yann LeCun and Yoshua Bengio kept working on neural networks when many others had given up. In 2018 they shared the Turing Award, often called the Nobel Prize of computing. In 2024 Hinton shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with John Hopfield for foundational discoveries that enabled machine learning with artificial neural networks."],["Brains and networks","Artificial neural networks are only loosely like the brain. The human brain has about 86 billion neurons and trillions of connections, uses about 20 watts of power and learns from relatively few examples. Artificial networks need vast amounts of data and energy. Neuroscientists and AI researchers continue to learn from each other."],["Black boxes","It is hard to know exactly why a neural network makes a particular decision, because its knowledge is spread across millions or billions of weights. Researchers in the field of interpretability are developing tools to look inside networks and understand the features and concepts they have learned."]],
sp:["Reading the mail","One of the first successful uses of neural networks was reading handwritten numbers. In the late 1980s and 1990s, Yann LeCun and colleagues at Bell Labs trained a convolutional network called LeNet to recognize digits. By the late 1990s, systems based on it were reading a large share of the handwritten checks processed in the United States, years before the deep learning boom."],
g:[["Artificial neuron","A simple unit that combines inputs and passes on a signal"],["Weight","A number that sets the strength of a connection"],["Backpropagation","A method for adjusting weights to reduce errors"],["GPU","A graphics processing unit, a chip that does many calculations at once"]],
f:["The first mathematical model of a neuron was published in 1943.","Graphics chips made deep learning practical.","The human brain uses about 20 watts of power."],
fun:"The human brain runs on about 20 watts, roughly the power of a dim light bulb."});

ADDART({id:"llm",t:"Large Language Models",c:"Technology",i:"💬",w:"Large language model",r:["ai","neural","mlearn","aisafety","langevo"],
p:[["Models of language","Large language models are AI systems trained on enormous amounts of text to understand and generate language."],["Chatbots","They power chatbots and assistants that can answer questions, write, translate, summarize and help with computer code."],["Powerful but imperfect","They can be very useful, but they can also make mistakes and state false information confidently."]],
l:"A large language model, or LLM, is a type of artificial intelligence trained on huge collections of text, such as books, articles and websites, to predict and generate language. Built on the transformer neural network design, LLMs learn patterns in grammar, facts and reasoning from their training data. Since the release of ChatGPT in 2022, chatbots based on LLMs, made by companies and research groups in many countries, have been used by hundreds of millions of people for writing, learning, research and programming.",
s:[["Predicting the next word","At its core, a language model is trained to predict the next piece of text, called a token, given the words before it. By practicing this on trillions of words, the model learns a great deal about language and the world. When it answers a question, it generates its reply one token at a time."],["Transformers","LLMs are based on the transformer, introduced by researchers at Google in a 2017 paper titled Attention Is All You Need. Its attention mechanism lets the model consider how every word in a passage relates to every other, capturing meaning across long stretches of text. Models have grown from millions to hundreds of billions of adjustable weights."],["From model to assistant","After the first stage of training on text, models are fine-tuned to follow instructions and be helpful. A common method, reinforcement learning from human feedback, has people rate different answers so the model learns which are better. Developers also train models to refuse harmful requests and to follow guidelines."],["What they can do","LLMs can answer questions, explain ideas, draft essays and emails, summarize documents, translate between many languages, solve math problems and write computer code. Some can also understand images and audio. Newer systems, sometimes called agents, can use tools such as web search and carry out multi-step tasks."]],
k:[["Based on","The transformer (2017)"],["Trained on","Very large collections of text"],["Basic task","Predicting the next token"],["Widespread public use","Since late 2022"],["Known problem","Hallucination, stating false information"]],
d:[["2017","Transformer paper is published"],["2018","Google’s BERT and OpenAI’s first GPT are released"],["2020","GPT-3 shows strong abilities from scale"],["2022","ChatGPT is released"],["2023","Many companies release rival chatbots"],["2024","EU adopts the AI Act, including rules for general-purpose models"]],
s2:[["Hallucinations","Because LLMs generate text that sounds likely, they sometimes produce statements that are false, invent quotations or cite sources that do not exist. These errors are called hallucinations. Users are advised to check important facts, and developers are working on ways to make models more accurate and to have them admit uncertainty."],["Many models","Large language models are built by companies and research groups around the world, including in the United States, China, Europe and elsewhere. Some, such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT, Google’s Gemini and Anthropic’s Claude, are offered as services; others are released openly so anyone can download and adapt them."],["Learning and schools","LLMs can act as tutors, explaining topics step by step and answering follow-up questions. They also raise concerns about cheating and over-reliance. Schools and universities are developing rules on when AI tools may be used and teaching students how to use them responsibly and check their output."],["Energy and computing","Training the largest models requires thousands of specialized chips running for weeks or months in data centers that use large amounts of electricity and water for cooling. Researchers are working on more efficient models and chips, and companies are investing in new power sources."],["Copyright and data","LLMs learn from text written by people, raising questions about copyright, credit and payment for authors, artists and news organizations whose work was used in training. Several court cases and new laws are addressing these issues, and the debate continues."],["Many languages","Most training text has been in English and a few other widely used languages, so models often work less well in languages with less online text. Projects around the world are gathering text and building models for languages such as Swahili, Tagalog, Bengali, Yoruba and Indigenous languages."]],
sp:["What is a token?","Language models do not read whole words but tokens, pieces of text that may be a word, part of a word or a punctuation mark. A common English word might be one token, while a rare or long word may be split into several. On average, an English word is about one and a third tokens. Models have a limit, called a context window, on how many tokens they can consider at once."],
g:[["Large language model","An AI system trained on huge amounts of text to understand and generate language"],["Token","A small piece of text processed by a language model"],["Hallucination","A false statement produced confidently by an AI model"],["Fine-tuning","Further training of a model for a particular purpose"]],
f:["Large language models generate text one token at a time.","The transformer was introduced in 2017.","LLMs can state false information confidently, so facts should be checked."],
fun:"A language model reads text in pieces called tokens; an average English word is about one and a third tokens."});

ADDART({id:"compvision",t:"Computer Vision",c:"Technology",i:"👁️",w:"Computer vision",r:["neural","mlearn","camera","eye","robot"],
p:[["Teaching computers to see","Computer vision is the field of AI that helps computers understand images and videos."],["All around us","It unlocks phones by face, reads text in photos, sorts packages and helps cars detect pedestrians."],["From pixels to meaning","A computer sees an image as a grid of numbers and must learn to find objects, faces and actions in them."]],
l:"Computer vision is a field of artificial intelligence that enables computers to interpret and understand visual information from the world, such as photographs, videos and medical scans. To a computer, an image is just a grid of numbers representing the color of each pixel. Computer vision systems learn to turn these numbers into meaning: recognizing objects, reading text, tracking movement and measuring shapes. Since the 2010s, deep neural networks have made computer vision far more accurate.",
s:[["Seeing is hard","Humans recognize a friend’s face in an instant, but this is extremely hard for computers. The same object can look very different depending on light, angle, distance and what is hiding part of it. Early researchers in the 1960s thought teaching computers to see might be a summer project; it took decades."],["Main tasks","Image classification decides what is in a picture. Object detection finds and outlines several objects, such as cars, people and traffic lights. Segmentation labels every pixel, separating sky from road. Other tasks include recognizing faces, reading text, estimating depth and tracking moving objects in video."],["How it works today","Modern systems use convolutional neural networks and, increasingly, vision transformers, trained on millions of labeled images. Early layers detect edges and colors, later layers detect shapes and parts, and the final layers recognize whole objects. ImageNet, a database of over 14 million labeled images, was key to their development."],["Everyday uses","Computer vision unlocks smartphones by face, suggests tags in photo albums, reads car license plates, scans barcodes and checks products on factory lines for defects. Farmers use drones with cameras to monitor crops, and translation apps read signs and menus in other languages through a phone camera."]],
k:[["Key database","ImageNet, over 14 million images"],["Main tasks","Classification, detection, segmentation, tracking"],["Main tools","Convolutional networks, vision transformers"],["Breakthrough","2012, AlexNet"],["Concerns","Privacy and surveillance, bias"]],
d:[["1966","MIT’s Summer Vision Project begins"],["1989","Neural networks read handwritten digits"],["2001","Viola–Jones method enables fast face detection"],["2009","ImageNet is released"],["2012","AlexNet wins the ImageNet contest"],["2015","Computers surpass human error rates on the ImageNet test"]],
s2:[["Medical imaging","Computer vision systems help doctors analyze X-rays, CT scans, MRI images, eye scans and skin photos, spotting signs of disease such as tumors, fractures and diabetic eye disease. In some tests they match specialists on narrow tasks. They are used to assist doctors and must be checked carefully in real clinics."],["Self-driving cars","Autonomous vehicles use cameras, often together with radar and laser sensors called lidar, to see lanes, signs, other vehicles and pedestrians. Robotaxi services now operate in some cities in the United States and China, but handling every situation safely, such as bad weather and unusual events, remains challenging."],["Faces and privacy","Face recognition can find a person in photos or video by comparing their face with stored images. It is used to unlock phones and at some airport gates, but its use for public surveillance raises serious privacy concerns. Studies have found some systems less accurate for certain groups, and some cities and countries have restricted its use."],["Seeing the planet","Satellites take images of the whole Earth every day. Computer vision helps analyze them to track deforestation, monitor floods and wildfires, count buildings after disasters, map crops and even find illegal fishing boats. It helps scientists and aid groups respond quickly."],["Images that fool machines","Researchers have shown that tiny changes to an image, invisible to people, can make a vision system misidentify it completely, for example seeing a panda as a gibbon. Stickers placed on road signs have fooled some systems. Making computer vision robust against such tricks is important for safety."],["Generating images","Computer vision now works in reverse too: AI image generators create pictures from text descriptions. They are trained on huge collections of images and captions. They raise questions about copyright, about artists’ livelihoods and about fake images, sometimes called deepfakes, that could mislead people."]],
sp:["The summer project","In 1966, at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Seymour Papert launched the Summer Vision Project, hoping students could build a significant part of a visual system in a single summer. The goal turned out to be far harder than expected. Researchers often tell the story to show how difficult it is to teach machines what humans do effortlessly."],
g:[["Pixel","A tiny dot that is the smallest part of a digital image"],["Object detection","Finding and locating objects in an image"],["Segmentation","Labeling each pixel of an image by what it shows"],["Lidar","A sensor that measures distance using laser light"]],
f:["To a computer, an image is a grid of numbers.","ImageNet contains over 14 million labeled images.","Tiny invisible changes can fool computer vision systems."],
fun:"In 1966 researchers thought teaching a computer to see could be done in a single summer."});

;
/* data/ai-3.js */
// Artificial intelligence and computing articles, part 3: AI safety and ethics, and quantum computing.
ADDART({id:"aisafety",t:"AI Safety and Ethics",c:"Technology",i:"⚖️",w:"AI safety",r:["ai","llm","hrights","law","algorithm"],
p:[["Using AI responsibly","AI ethics asks how artificial intelligence should be designed and used fairly, honestly and safely."],["Real risks today","Concerns include bias, privacy, misinformation, job changes and misuse of AI tools."],["Looking ahead","AI safety researchers also study how to keep increasingly powerful AI systems reliable and under human control."]],
l:"AI safety and ethics is the study of how to make sure artificial intelligence benefits people and avoids causing harm. Ethics covers questions of fairness, privacy, transparency, responsibility and the effects of AI on work and society. Safety research focuses on making AI systems behave reliably and as intended, resisting misuse and remaining under meaningful human control as they become more capable. Governments, companies, researchers and international bodies are all developing rules and practices for responsible AI.",
s:[["Bias and fairness","AI systems learn from data, and data can reflect past discrimination. Systems used to screen job applicants, assess loans or predict crime have sometimes treated people unfairly based on race, gender or other characteristics. Researchers test systems for bias, use more representative data and design methods to reduce unfair outcomes."],["Privacy and surveillance","AI can analyze huge amounts of personal data, from photos and location to shopping habits. Face recognition can identify people in crowds. This raises concerns about privacy and surveillance by companies and governments. Laws such as the European Union’s General Data Protection Regulation give people some control over their data."],["Misinformation and deepfakes","AI can generate realistic text, images, audio and video, including deepfakes that show people saying or doing things they never did. These can be used to spread false information, commit fraud or harass people. Tools to detect AI-generated content and to label it, such as digital watermarks, are being developed."],["Alignment","Alignment research aims to make AI systems pursue the goals their designers and users actually intend, rather than finding shortcuts or behaving in unexpected ways. As AI systems become more capable and act more independently, researchers study how to test them, understand their inner workings and make sure they follow human values and instructions."]],
k:[["Main concerns","Bias, privacy, misinformation, safety, jobs, misuse"],["Key EU law","The AI Act (adopted 2024)"],["International summits","Bletchley Park 2023, Seoul 2024, Paris 2025"],["Research areas","Alignment, interpretability, robustness, evaluation"],["UNESCO guidance","Recommendation on the Ethics of AI (2021)"]],
d:[["1942","Isaac Asimov publishes his Three Laws of Robotics in a story"],["2018","EU’s data protection law (GDPR) takes effect"],["2019","OECD adopts principles on AI"],["2021","UNESCO adopts its Recommendation on the Ethics of AI"],["2023","First international AI Safety Summit at Bletchley Park"],["2024","EU adopts the AI Act"]],
s2:[["Asimov’s laws","In 1942 the science fiction writer Isaac Asimov introduced the Three Laws of Robotics: a robot must not harm a human, must obey humans unless that causes harm, and must protect itself unless that conflicts with the first two laws. His stories showed how such simple rules could lead to unexpected problems, a theme that still shapes discussions of AI safety."],["Laws and rules","The European Union’s AI Act, adopted in 2024, sorts AI uses by risk, banning some, such as social scoring by governments, and setting strict rules for high-risk uses such as in hiring and medicine. China, the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada, Brazil, India and many other countries have issued their own rules or guidelines, and approaches differ."],["International cooperation","The first international AI Safety Summit was held at Bletchley Park in the United Kingdom in 2023, where the codebreakers of World War II worked. Further summits followed in Seoul in 2024 and Paris in 2025. Several countries have set up AI safety institutes to test advanced models, and the United Nations has created an advisory body on AI."],["Jobs and the economy","AI can automate tasks in many jobs, from customer service and translation to programming and design, while creating new jobs and raising productivity. Economists disagree about how quickly and how much work will change. Many experts call for training and education to help workers adapt."],["Debates about long-term risk","Some prominent researchers warn that very advanced AI could pose serious risks if it becomes difficult to control, and have called for such risks to be taken as seriously as pandemics. Others argue that these concerns are exaggerated or distract from present harms such as bias and misinformation. The debate is active among scientists, companies and governments."],["Responsible development","AI developers publish safety policies, test models for dangerous capabilities before release, invite outside researchers to probe for weaknesses, a practice called red-teaming, and build in safeguards against misuse. Critics say voluntary measures are not enough and that independent oversight is needed."]],
sp:["The Three Laws of Robotics","Isaac Asimov’s laws first appeared in his 1942 short story Runaround. First: a robot may not injure a human being or, through inaction, allow a human being to come to harm. Second: a robot must obey orders given by humans, except where they conflict with the First Law. Third: a robot must protect its own existence, as long as this does not conflict with the first two laws. Real AI cannot simply be programmed with such rules, but they started a lasting conversation."],
g:[["AI ethics","The study of right and wrong in designing and using AI"],["Bias","Unfair favoring of some people or outcomes over others"],["Deepfake","A realistic fake image, video or audio made with AI"],["Alignment","Making AI systems pursue the goals humans intend"]],
f:["Isaac Asimov wrote the Three Laws of Robotics in 1942.","The EU adopted the AI Act in 2024.","The first AI Safety Summit was held at Bletchley Park in 2023."],
fun:"The first international AI Safety Summit was held at Bletchley Park, where Alan Turing helped break wartime codes."});

ADDART({id:"quantumcomp",t:"Quantum Computing",c:"Technology",i:"🔮",w:"Quantum computing",r:["quantum","comp","chip","superconduct","crypto"],
p:[["A new kind of computer","Quantum computers use the strange rules of quantum physics to process information in new ways."],["Qubits","Instead of bits that are 0 or 1, they use qubits, which can be in a combination of both at once."],["Still experimental","Today’s quantum computers are small and error-prone, but they could one day solve problems beyond ordinary computers."]],
l:"A quantum computer is a machine that uses principles of quantum mechanics, such as superposition and entanglement, to perform calculations. Ordinary computers store information as bits, each either 0 or 1. Quantum computers use quantum bits, or qubits, which can exist in combinations of 0 and 1. For certain problems, such as simulating molecules or breaking some codes, a large, reliable quantum computer could be vastly faster than any ordinary computer. Building one is extremely difficult, and current machines are still experimental.",
s:[["Bits and qubits","A classical bit is like a light switch, either off or on. A qubit can be in a superposition, a blend of 0 and 1 at the same time, until it is measured, when it settles into one value. Many qubits together can represent an enormous number of possibilities at once, which a quantum algorithm can use to find answers."],["Entanglement","Qubits can be entangled, meaning their states are linked so that measuring one immediately tells you about the other, no matter how far apart they are. Einstein called this spooky action at a distance. Entanglement lets quantum computers process information in ways ordinary computers cannot efficiently copy."],["How they are built","Qubits can be made in several ways: tiny superconducting circuits cooled to near absolute zero, single charged atoms called ions held in electric fields, neutral atoms trapped by lasers, particles of light, or special defects in crystals. Each approach has strengths and weaknesses, and companies and universities around the world are pursuing them."],["The problem of errors","Qubits are fragile: heat, vibration and stray signals can destroy their delicate quantum states in a fraction of a second, a problem called decoherence. Quantum error correction combines many physical qubits into one more reliable logical qubit. Recent experiments have shown error correction improving as more qubits are added, an important milestone."]],
k:[["Basic unit","The qubit"],["Key effects","Superposition, entanglement, interference"],["Proposed by","Richard Feynman and others, early 1980s"],["Famous algorithms","Shor’s algorithm (factoring), Grover’s algorithm (search)"],["Main challenge","Errors and decoherence"]],
d:[["1981","Richard Feynman suggests quantum computers to simulate nature"],["1985","David Deutsch describes a universal quantum computer"],["1994","Peter Shor publishes his factoring algorithm"],["1998","First small quantum computers using a few qubits"],["2019","Google claims quantum advantage on a specialized task"],["2024","Experiments show error correction improving with more qubits"]],
s2:[["Feynman’s idea","In 1981 the physicist Richard Feynman pointed out that simulating quantum systems, such as molecules, on ordinary computers becomes impossibly hard as they grow larger, because the number of possibilities explodes. He suggested building computers that work by quantum rules themselves to simulate nature."],["Shor’s algorithm","In 1994 Peter Shor showed that a large quantum computer could factor huge numbers into primes exponentially faster than known methods on ordinary computers. Much internet security relies on factoring being hard, so this could break widely used encryption. Governments have begun switching to new post-quantum encryption that quantum computers are not expected to crack; the United States published standards for it in 2024."],["Quantum advantage","In 2019 Google reported that its 53-qubit Sycamore processor performed a specialized calculation in about 200 seconds that it estimated would take the best supercomputer thousands of years, a claim others disputed. Teams in China and elsewhere have reported similar demonstrations. These tasks have no practical use yet but show quantum effects at work."],["What they could do","Researchers hope that future quantum computers could simulate molecules and materials to help design new medicines, batteries, fertilizers and solar cells, solve certain optimization problems and advance physics research. For most everyday tasks, such as browsing the web or word processing, ordinary computers will remain better."],["Quantum computers around the world","Companies such as IBM, Google, Quantinuum, IonQ and others, along with research programs in China, Europe, Japan, Canada, Australia and India, are building quantum computers. Some can be used over the internet. Machines have grown from a few qubits to hundreds or more, though most are still noisy."],["The quantum year","The United Nations declared 2025 the International Year of Quantum Science and Technology, marking 100 years since the development of quantum mechanics by Werner Heisenberg, Erwin Schrödinger and others. It celebrated how quantum physics underlies technologies from lasers and computer chips to MRI scanners and, in the future, quantum computers."]],
sp:["Colder than space","Many quantum computers use superconducting qubits that must be cooled to about one hundredth of a degree above absolute zero, colder than outer space. They sit inside dilution refrigerators, which look like golden chandeliers made of layered plates and coiled tubes, hanging from the ceiling. Only the bottom stage, holding a small chip, reaches the coldest temperature."],
g:[["Qubit","A quantum bit that can be in a combination of 0 and 1"],["Superposition","Being in a combination of states at the same time"],["Entanglement","A link between quantum particles so that their states are connected"],["Decoherence","The loss of quantum behavior caused by disturbances"]],
f:["Qubits can be in a combination of 0 and 1 at once.","Shor’s algorithm could break much of today’s encryption on a large quantum computer.","2025 was the International Year of Quantum Science and Technology."],
fun:"Many quantum computers run colder than outer space."});

;
/* data/living-1.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, living part 1: giant pandas, cheetahs, camels and hippos.
ADDART({id:"panda",t:"Giant Pandas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐼",w:"Giant panda",r:["bear","bamboo","chinacountry","eco"],
p:[["A bamboo-eating bear","The giant panda is a black-and-white bear that lives in the mountain forests of central China and eats almost nothing but bamboo."],["Back from the brink","Conservation work has helped panda numbers grow, and in 2016 the IUCN changed its status from Endangered to Vulnerable."],["A symbol of conservation","The panda is one of the world’s best-known animals and the logo of the conservation group WWF."]],
l:"The giant panda is a bear native to the mountains of central China, in the provinces of Sichuan, Shaanxi and Gansu. Famous for its black-and-white coat, it feeds almost entirely on bamboo, eating for up to 14 hours a day. Habitat loss once pushed it close to extinction, but protected reserves, bamboo corridors and breeding programs have helped the wild population rise to around 1,900 adults. China lends pandas to zoos around the world as a sign of friendship, a practice known as panda diplomacy.",
s:[["Built for bamboo","Pandas belong to the order of meat-eating mammals and have the short gut of a carnivore, yet bamboo makes up about 99 percent of their diet. Because bamboo gives little energy and is hard to digest, an adult may eat 12 to 38 kilograms a day. Pandas save energy by moving slowly and resting a lot."],["The panda’s thumb","Pandas grip bamboo with a special enlarged wrist bone that works like a thumb, letting them hold stems while they strip off leaves with their teeth. Their strong jaws and broad, flat back teeth crush tough stalks."],["Life in the mountains","Pandas live alone in cool, damp bamboo forests between about 1,200 and 3,400 meters high. They do not hibernate, but move to lower slopes in winter. They communicate with scent marks and calls such as bleats, honks and barks."],["Tiny cubs","A newborn panda weighs only about 100 to 200 grams, about 1/900 of its mother’s weight, and is pink, blind and nearly hairless. Mothers often give birth to twins in captivity but usually raise only one in the wild. Cubs stay with their mothers for about two years."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Ailuropoda melanoleuca"],["Range","Mountains of central China"],["Diet","About 99% bamboo"],["Wild population","About 1,900 adults (2014 survey)"],["IUCN status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["1869","French missionary Armand David describes the giant panda for Western science"],["1961","WWF adopts the panda as its logo"],["1963","China opens its first panda reserve, Wolong"],["2016","Giant panda moved from Endangered to Vulnerable"],["2021","China creates Giant Panda National Park"]],
s2:[["Why black and white?","Scientists think the panda’s coloring helps it hide: the white parts blend with snow and the black parts with shadows in the forest. The dark eye patches and ears may help pandas recognize each other and signal to rivals."],["Threats","Farming, logging and roads broke panda forests into small, isolated patches, making it hard for pandas to find mates and fresh bamboo. Bamboo also flowers and dies off all at once every few decades, which once caused pandas to starve when they could not move to new areas."],["Saving pandas","China has created more than 60 panda reserves and in 2021 combined many into the Giant Panda National Park, larger than the island of Puerto Rico. Corridors of planted forest link isolated groups. Breeding centers, such as Chengdu’s, have raised hundreds of pandas, and some have been released into the wild."],["Panda diplomacy","China has long given or lent pandas to other countries as goodwill gifts. In 1972, after US President Richard Nixon visited China, two pandas named Ling-Ling and Hsing-Hsing were sent to the National Zoo in Washington. Today pandas are loaned to zoos in many countries, and cubs born abroad usually return to China."],["Pandas’ relatives","Genetic studies show that the giant panda is a true bear, related to brown bears and polar bears. The red panda, a small, reddish animal of the Himalayas with a ringed tail, shares the name and a similar false thumb but is not closely related; it belongs to its own family."],["An umbrella species","Protecting pandas also protects their whole forest home, including golden snub-nosed monkeys, takins, red pandas and many rare birds and plants. Animals like this, whose protection helps many others, are called umbrella species."]],
sp:["Eating all day","A wild panda spends up to 14 hours a day eating and most of the rest sleeping. Because it digests only about a fifth of what it eats, it produces a great deal of droppings, sometimes more than 100 a day. Scientists collect these droppings to study pandas’ diet, health and DNA without disturbing them."],
g:[["Carnivore","An animal that belongs to the meat-eating order, though some, like pandas, eat plants"],["Reserve","A protected area for wildlife"],["Umbrella species","A species whose protection also protects many others"],["Corridor","A strip of habitat linking separate areas"]],
f:["Giant pandas eat bamboo for up to 14 hours a day.","A newborn panda weighs about as much as a small apple.","The red panda is not closely related to the giant panda."],
fun:"A newborn giant panda is about 900 times smaller than its mother."});

ADDART({id:"cheetah",t:"Cheetahs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐆",w:"Cheetah",r:["lion","tiger","kenyacountry","eco","antelopes"],
p:[["The fastest land animal","The cheetah can sprint at up to about 100 kilometers per hour, faster than any other land animal."],["Built for speed","Its light body, long legs, flexible spine and long tail make it a perfect sprinter."],["In danger","Only about 7,000 cheetahs remain in the wild, mostly in eastern and southern Africa."]],
l:"The cheetah is a large wild cat of the African savannas and grasslands, famous as the fastest land animal. In short bursts it can reach speeds of about 100 kilometers per hour, accelerating faster than most sports cars. Cheetahs hunt by day, sprinting after gazelles and other antelopes. Once found across much of Africa and southwestern Asia, they have disappeared from most of their former range, and about 7,000 remain in the wild. A tiny population of Asiatic cheetahs survives in Iran.",
s:[["Speed machine","A cheetah’s spine bends like a spring, stretching its stride to about 7 meters. Large nostrils, lungs and heart take in extra oxygen. Its claws do not fully retract, gripping the ground like running spikes, and its long tail works like a rudder for sharp turns. A chase usually lasts less than a minute before the cat overheats and tires."],["Hunting","Cheetahs rely on sharp eyesight to spot prey, often from a termite mound or tree. They creep close, then sprint, trip the prey with a paw and seize its throat. Because they are lighter than lions and hyenas, they often lose their kills to these stronger predators and must eat quickly."],["Not like other big cats","Cheetahs cannot roar; instead they chirp, purr, hiss and growl. Their small, rounded heads and black tear marks running from their eyes to their mouths help reduce glare from the Sun. Unlike lions and leopards, they hunt mostly in daylight to avoid competition."],["Family life","Females live alone and raise their cubs, usually three to five, by themselves. Cubs have a fluffy gray mantle of fur along their backs that may help disguise them as honey badgers, fierce animals that predators avoid. Brothers often stay together for life in small groups called coalitions."]],
k:[["Top speed","About 100 km/h in short bursts"],["Scientific name","Acinonyx jubatus"],["Wild population","About 7,000"],["Range","Mainly eastern and southern Africa; a few in Iran"],["IUCN status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BCE","Cheetahs are tamed for hunting in ancient Egypt and Asia"],["1500s","Mughal emperor Akbar is said to keep about 1,000 hunting cheetahs"],["1952","Cheetahs are declared extinct in India"],["2001","Iran launches a program to save the Asiatic cheetah"],["2022","Cheetahs from Namibia are released in Kuno National Park, India"]],
s2:[["Cheetahs and people","For thousands of years, rulers in Egypt, Persia and India kept tamed cheetahs to hunt antelopes, bringing them to the field hooded like falcons. The cheetah’s name comes from a Hindi word, chita, meaning spotted. In the 1500s the Mughal emperor Akbar was said to have kept about a thousand."],["Threats","Cheetahs need huge areas to hunt, but farms and towns have broken up their habitat. They are killed by farmers protecting livestock, struck by cars, and cubs are captured illegally for the pet trade. Because cheetahs have low genetic diversity, they may also be more vulnerable to disease."],["Helping farmers and cheetahs","In Namibia, home to one of the largest wild populations, conservation groups give farmers trained guard dogs, such as Anatolian shepherds, to protect their herds. Fewer livestock are lost, so fewer cheetahs are shot. Most of Namibia’s cheetahs live on farmland rather than in parks."],["Asiatic cheetahs","The Asiatic cheetah once roamed from the Arabian Peninsula to India. Today fewer than 30 are thought to survive, all in the deserts of central Iran, making it one of the rarest big cats on Earth."],["Return to India","Cheetahs died out in India by the mid-1900s. In 2022 and 2023, cheetahs from Namibia and South Africa were moved to Kuno National Park in central India in the first intercontinental move of a large carnivore. Several cubs have been born, though some adults and cubs died, and the project is closely watched."],["Fast but fragile","Cheetahs are built for speed rather than strength. Their light bones and small teeth leave them poorly armed, and fewer than half of their chases succeed. Cub survival in the wild is low, especially in places with many lions and hyenas."]],
sp:["Faster than a car","A cheetah can go from standing still to about 95 kilometers per hour in roughly three seconds, as fast as many high-performance cars. During a sprint its feet touch the ground only briefly, and for parts of each stride all four feet are in the air. Scientists have studied cheetah movement to design faster, more agile running robots."],
g:[["Savanna","Tropical grassland with scattered trees"],["Coalition","A small group of male cheetahs, often brothers, that live together"],["Genetic diversity","Variety in the genes of a population"],["Predator","An animal that hunts other animals"]],
f:["The cheetah is the fastest land animal.","Cheetahs cannot roar, but they can purr and chirp.","Fewer than 30 Asiatic cheetahs may survive, all in Iran."],
fun:"Cheetah cubs’ fluffy fur may help them look like fierce honey badgers."});

ADDART({id:"camel",t:"Camels",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐫",w:"Camel",r:["desert","sahara","saudicountry","mali"],
p:[["Ships of the desert","Camels are large mammals adapted to deserts, able to travel long distances with little water."],["One hump or two","Dromedaries have one hump and live mainly in Africa and Arabia; Bactrian camels have two and live in Central Asia."],["Partners of people","For thousands of years camels have carried goods and people and provided milk, meat and wool."]],
l:"Camels are large, long-legged mammals famous for their humps and their ability to survive in hot and cold deserts. The dromedary, or Arabian camel, has one hump and is found in North Africa, the Middle East and South Asia, as well as feral herds in Australia. The Bactrian camel has two humps and lives in Central Asia; a separate wild Bactrian species survives in small numbers in China and Mongolia. Domesticated thousands of years ago, camels made possible the great caravan routes across the Sahara and Arabia.",
s:[["The humps","A camel’s hump does not store water, as many people believe, but fat, up to about 35 kilograms. Storing fat in one place helps the rest of the body lose heat, and the fat can be broken down for energy and water when food is scarce. A hungry camel’s hump shrinks and flops over."],["Saving water","Camels can go many days without drinking in hot weather and much longer in cool weather. They lose very little water in urine and droppings, and their body temperature can rise by several degrees during the day before they start to sweat. When they do drink, they can gulp more than 100 liters in a few minutes."],["Desert equipment","Camels have long eyelashes and slit nostrils they can close against blowing sand, and thick pads on their knees and chests for kneeling on hot ground. Their broad, padded feet spread out to walk on sand without sinking, and tough mouths let them eat thorny desert plants."],["Two kinds","Dromedaries make up about 90 percent of the world’s camels. Bactrian camels have thick, shaggy coats to survive freezing winters in the Gobi Desert and the steppes. The wild Bactrian camel, a separate species, is critically endangered, with only about 1,000 left."]],
k:[["Species","Dromedary, Bactrian, wild Bactrian"],["Hump contains","Fat, not water"],["Water in one drink","More than 100 liters"],["Domesticated","About 3,000 to 4,000 years ago"],["Relatives","Llamas, alpacas, vicuñas, guanacos"]],
d:[["c. 2000–1000 BCE","Camels are domesticated in Arabia and Central Asia"],["c. 300 CE","Camel caravans begin crossing the Sahara regularly"],["1324","Mansa Musa’s camel caravan travels to Mecca"],["1840","Camels are first imported into Australia"],["2002","Wild Bactrian camel listed as critically endangered"]],
s2:[["Caravans and trade","Camels transformed trade in Africa and Asia. Caravans of hundreds or thousands of camels carried salt, gold, cloth and spices across the Sahara, linking West Africa with the Mediterranean, and along the Silk Road between China and the Middle East. A strong camel can carry about 200 kilograms."],["Milk, meat and wool","In many desert regions, camels are still valued for milk, which is rich in vitamin C, and for meat, leather and wool. Camel milk is sold in shops in the Gulf countries and is being studied as a food for dry regions where cattle struggle."],["Camel races and beauty contests","Camel racing is a popular sport in the Arabian Peninsula, where robot jockeys now ride the camels instead of children. Camel beauty contests judge features such as the shape of the lips, hump and neck, with prizes worth millions of dollars."],["Camels in Australia","In the 1800s camels were brought to Australia with handlers from Afghanistan, India and Pakistan to help explore and supply the dry interior. When trucks and railways replaced them, many were released. Today Australia has hundreds of thousands of wild dromedaries, the largest feral herd in the world."],["Family of camels","Camels belong to the camelid family, which first evolved in North America about 45 million years ago. Some crossed to Asia, becoming camels, while others moved to South America, becoming llamas, alpacas, vicuñas and guanacos. Camelids died out in North America about 10,000 years ago."],["Spitting and grumbling","When annoyed, camels may bring up partly digested food from their stomachs and spray it, a habit that gave them a reputation for spitting. They also groan, bellow and grumble. Despite this, they are often patient and calm working animals."]],
sp:["Arctic camels","Fossils found in the Canadian Arctic show that giant camels lived there about 3.5 million years ago, when the region was warmer and forested but still had long, freezing winters. Some scientists think that features such as humps of fat and wide feet first helped camels survive cold, snowy winters, and only later helped them in hot deserts."],
g:[["Dromedary","The one-humped camel"],["Bactrian camel","The two-humped camel of Central Asia"],["Caravan","A group of travelers and animals crossing a desert together"],["Feral","Once domesticated, now living wild"]],
f:["A camel’s hump stores fat, not water.","A thirsty camel can drink over 100 liters in minutes.","Australia has the largest herd of wild camels in the world."],
fun:"A camel’s hump stores fat, not water."});

ADDART({id:"hippo",t:"Hippopotamuses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦛",w:"Hippopotamus",r:["whale","elephant","nile","eco"],
p:[["River horse","The name hippopotamus means river horse in Greek, but hippos are more closely related to whales."],["Huge and heavy","Adult hippos can weigh more than 1,500 kilograms, making them among the largest land animals."],["Dangerous","Hippos are aggressive and responsible for many deaths of people in Africa each year."]],
l:"The common hippopotamus is a huge, semi-aquatic mammal of sub-Saharan Africa. Hippos spend the day resting in rivers and lakes to keep cool, and come out at night to graze on grass, walking several kilometers. They can weigh more than 1,500 kilograms, with males sometimes much heavier. Despite their round shape, they can run fast on land and are very territorial, making them one of Africa’s most dangerous animals. A much smaller relative, the pygmy hippopotamus, lives in the forests of West Africa.",
s:[["Life in the water","Hippos cannot really swim; they walk or push off the bottom of rivers and lakes. They can hold their breath for about five minutes. Their eyes, ears and nostrils sit on top of the head, so they can see, hear and breathe while almost completely underwater, and they close their ears and nostrils when they dive."],["Natural sunscreen","Hippo skin is thin and dries out quickly in the sun. Glands in the skin release an oily red liquid, once called blood sweat, which scientists found contains red and orange pigments that block sunlight and slow the growth of bacteria."],["Grazers at night","At night hippos leave the water and follow paths to grassy feeding areas, eating about 35 kilograms of grass. Their droppings in rivers bring nutrients from land into the water, feeding fish and other animals, though too much can harm water quality in drought."],["Jaws and tusks","A hippo can open its mouth almost 150 degrees, showing tusk-like teeth up to 50 centimeters long. Males use them in fierce fights over territory. Yawning is a threat display, warning rivals and intruders to keep away."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Hippopotamus amphibius"],["Weight","Usually 1,300–1,800 kg; large males over 3,000 kg"],["Closest living relatives","Whales and dolphins"],["Range","Sub-Saharan Africa"],["IUCN status","Vulnerable"]],
d:[["c. 3500 BCE","Hippos appear in ancient Egyptian art"],["1850","Obaysch becomes the first hippo seen in Europe since Roman times, at London Zoo"],["1990s","Genetic studies show hippos are close relatives of whales"],["2006","Common hippo listed as Vulnerable"],["2023","Colombia announces plans to control its runaway hippo population"]],
s2:[["Cousins of whales","Genetic and fossil evidence shows that the closest living relatives of hippos are whales and dolphins. They share a common ancestor that lived more than 50 million years ago. Like whales, hippos give birth and nurse their young underwater."],["Dangerous neighbors","Hippos are aggressive, especially mothers with calves and males defending their stretch of river. They may overturn boats and attack people who come too close, and they are blamed for hundreds of human deaths a year in Africa, though reliable numbers are hard to find."],["Threats","Hippos are hunted illegally for meat and for their ivory teeth, and they lose habitat when rivers are dammed, drained or farmed. Droughts can crowd them into shrinking pools. Their numbers have fallen in many countries, though they remain common in parts of East and southern Africa."],["The pygmy hippo","The pygmy hippopotamus, which lives in the swampy forests of West Africa, especially Liberia, is about a tenth the weight of its larger cousin. It is shy, solitary and endangered, with only a few thousand left in the wild. A pygmy hippo calf named Moo Deng, born in a zoo in Thailand in 2024, became famous worldwide online."],["Colombia’s hippos","In the 1980s the drug lord Pablo Escobar brought four hippos to his private zoo in Colombia. After his death they escaped into nearby rivers and multiplied, with more than 150 today. With no natural predators, they threaten local wildlife and people, and Colombia has begun sterilizing and relocating them."],["Hippos in history","Ancient Egyptians feared and honored hippos. Taweret, a goddess of childbirth, had the head of a hippo. Small blue faience hippo figures were placed in tombs. Hippos once lived along the whole Nile and even in Europe during warm periods of the Ice Age."]],
sp:["Moo Deng","In July 2024, a pygmy hippo calf named Moo Deng, meaning bouncy pork in Thai, was born at Khao Kheow Open Zoo in Thailand. Videos of her playful and sometimes grumpy behavior spread around the world online, drawing huge crowds to the zoo. Her fame helped draw attention to the endangered pygmy hippopotamus in the wild."],
g:[["Semi-aquatic","Living partly in water and partly on land"],["Territorial","Defending an area against others"],["Pygmy","A smaller form of an animal"],["Graze","To feed on growing grass"]],
f:["Hippos are more closely related to whales than to horses.","A hippo can hold its breath for about five minutes.","Hippo skin makes its own red sunscreen."],
fun:"Hippos make their own reddish sunscreen."});

;
/* data/living-2.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, living part 2: rhinoceroses, crows, seahorses and crustaceans.
ADDART({id:"rhino",t:"Rhinoceroses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Rhinoceros",r:["elephant","indonesiacountry","southafricacountry","eco","paraceratherium","arsinoitherium"],
p:[["Horned giants","Rhinoceroses are large, thick-skinned plant-eaters with one or two horns on their snouts."],["Africa and Asia","There are five living species: two in Africa and three in Asia."],["Hunted for horns","Poaching for their horns has pushed several species close to extinction."]],
l:"Rhinoceroses, or rhinos, are among the largest land mammals, with thick skin and one or two horns made of keratin, the same material as fingernails. There are five living species: the white and black rhinos of Africa, and the greater one-horned, Javan and Sumatran rhinos of Asia. Once widespread, rhinos have been hunted for their horns, which are wrongly believed in some places to have medicinal value. Thanks to protection, some populations have recovered, but the Javan and Sumatran rhinos are critically endangered.",
s:[["Five species","The white rhino is the largest, weighing up to about 3,500 kilograms, with a wide mouth for grazing grass. The black rhino, smaller and with a pointed lip for browsing shrubs, is more aggressive. The greater one-horned rhino of India and Nepal has skin that looks like armor plates. The Javan and Sumatran rhinos are the rarest."],["Horns","A rhino’s horn is not bone but a mass of tightly packed keratin fibers that keeps growing throughout its life. Rhinos use their horns to defend themselves, dig for water, break branches and fight rivals. The horn has no proven medicinal power."],["Senses and skin","Rhinos have poor eyesight but a keen sense of smell and hearing. Their skin, up to about 5 centimeters thick in places, is sensitive to sunburn and insect bites, so they wallow in mud, which acts as sunscreen and insect repellent."],["Social life","Most rhinos live alone, though white rhinos sometimes form small groups. They mark territories with dung piles and urine. Females give birth to a single calf after a pregnancy of about 15 to 16 months, and the calf stays with its mother for two to three years."]],
k:[["Species","White, black, greater one-horned, Javan, Sumatran"],["Largest","White rhino, up to about 3,500 kg"],["Horn made of","Keratin"],["Rarest","Javan rhino, about 50 left"],["Main threat","Poaching for horns"]],
d:[["1970s","Rhino numbers crash due to poaching"],["1977","International trade in rhino horn is banned"],["2008–2015","Poaching surges in South Africa"],["2011","Western black rhino declared extinct"],["2018","Sudan, the last male northern white rhino, dies"],["2024","First pregnancy using a lab-made rhino embryo is reported"]],
s2:[["Poaching","Rhino horn is sold illegally at very high prices, mainly in parts of Asia, for traditional remedies or as a status symbol. Poaching surged after 2008, especially in South Africa, which has most of the world’s rhinos. Rangers, sniffer dogs, tracking collars and stricter laws have helped reduce poaching in some areas."],["The southern white rhino","By about 1900, fewer than 100 southern white rhinos survived, in what is now South Africa. Careful protection in reserves such as Hluhluwe-iMfolozi brought them back to many thousands, one of the great conservation successes. But poaching has since reduced their numbers again."],["The last northern white rhinos","The northern white rhino is functionally extinct: only two females, Najin and Fatu, survive in Kenya. Sudan, the last male, died in 2018. Scientists have made embryos using eggs from the females and stored sperm, hoping to carry a pregnancy using a southern white rhino surrogate mother."],["Rhinos of Asia","The greater one-horned rhino has recovered from fewer than 200 in the early 1900s to more than 4,000, thanks to protection in India’s Kaziranga National Park and Nepal’s Chitwan. The Javan rhino survives only in Ujung Kulon National Park in Indonesia, and the hairy Sumatran rhino, the smallest, numbers fewer than 50."],["Dehorning","In some reserves, wildlife workers sedate rhinos and saw off their horns, which do not hurt and grow back, to make them less valuable to poachers. Studies in South Africa have found that dehorning can greatly reduce poaching, though it must be repeated every year or two."],["Ancient rhinos","Rhinos have a long history. The woolly rhinoceros roamed Europe and Asia during the Ice Age and appears in cave paintings, such as those at Chauvet in France. Paraceratherium, a hornless rhino relative that lived in Asia about 30 million years ago, was one of the largest land mammals ever, about 5 meters tall at the shoulder."]],
sp:["Dürer’s rhinoceros","In 1515 an Indian rhinoceros arrived in Lisbon, the first seen in Europe since Roman times. The German artist Albrecht Dürer never saw it, but made a woodcut from a sketch and a description. His rhino has armor-like plates and an extra small horn on its back. Although inaccurate, the image was copied in books for nearly 300 years."],
g:[["Keratin","A tough protein found in hair, nails and horns"],["Poaching","Illegal hunting of animals"],["Functionally extinct","Having too few individuals left to survive without help"],["Wallow","To roll or lie in mud or water"]],
f:["Rhino horn is made of keratin, like fingernails.","The southern white rhino recovered from fewer than 100 animals.","Only two northern white rhinos are left, both female."],
fun:"A rhino’s horn is made of the same material as your fingernails."});

ADDART({id:"crow",t:"Crows and Ravens",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐦",w:"Corvidae",r:["birds","parrot","eagle","owl"],
p:[["Clever birds","Crows, ravens, jays and magpies belong to the crow family, among the most intelligent animals on Earth."],["Tool users","Some crows make and use tools to get food, an ability once thought to be almost uniquely human."],["Found worldwide","Members of the crow family live on every continent except Antarctica."]],
l:"Crows and ravens belong to the corvid family, which also includes jays, magpies, rooks and nutcrackers. Corvids are found almost worldwide and are known for their intelligence: they solve puzzles, make tools, remember human faces and plan for the future. The common raven, with a wingspan of more than a meter, is the largest songbird. Corvids often live close to people in cities and farmland, and they appear in the myths and stories of many cultures.",
s:[["Brainy birds","Relative to body size, corvids have brains as large as those of chimpanzees. Their brains pack many neurons into a small space. In experiments, crows and ravens solve problems as well as young children or great apes, for example understanding that dropping stones into a tube of water will raise the water level and bring food within reach."],["Toolmakers","New Caledonian crows, from an island in the Pacific, shape twigs and leaves into hooks and probes to pull insects from holes in wood. They can even combine short pieces into longer tools. Young crows learn by watching adults, and different groups make different styles of tools."],["Remembering faces","Studies have shown that crows remember the faces of people who threatened them and scold them for years afterward. They also seem to pass this information to other crows, including young ones that never saw the original threat."],["Planning ahead","Corvids such as jays hide food in thousands of places and remember where they put it. Some jays rehide food if they notice another bird watching them, suggesting they understand what others might do. Ravens in experiments have saved tools to use later for a reward."]],
k:[["Family","Corvidae"],["Largest songbird","Common raven, wingspan over 1 m"],["Number of species","About 130"],["Famous toolmaker","New Caledonian crow"],["Found","Every continent except Antarctica"]],
d:[["c. 700 BCE","Aesop’s fable The Crow and the Pitcher is told in ancient Greece"],["1600s","Legend says ravens must stay at the Tower of London"],["2002","A New Caledonian crow named Betty bends wire into a hook in a lab"],["2010","Study shows crows remember dangerous human faces"],["2017","Ravens shown to plan for future events"]],
s2:[["Aesop’s fable","In Aesop’s ancient fable The Crow and the Pitcher, a thirsty crow drops pebbles into a jug until the water rises high enough to drink. Modern experiments have shown that rooks, crows and jays really can solve this kind of problem, choosing heavy stones over light ones and solid objects over hollow ones."],["Talking and playing","Some corvids, such as ravens and magpies, can imitate human speech and other sounds. Ravens have been seen sliding down snowy roofs on their backs, apparently for fun, and playing games with sticks and other animals, behavior usually linked with intelligence."],["Crows in cities","Crows thrive in towns and cities, eating scraps and nesting in parks. In Japan, carrion crows have been seen placing walnuts on roads for cars to crack, then waiting for traffic lights to change before picking them up. Their adaptability helps them succeed alongside people."],["Ravens of the Tower","A legend says that if the ravens ever leave the Tower of London, the kingdom will fall. At least six ravens are kept there, looked after by a special guard called the Ravenmaster. The legend probably dates from the 1800s, though it is often said to be older."],["Myths and stories","Ravens and crows appear in stories worldwide. In Norse myth, the god Odin had two ravens, Thought and Memory, that flew over the world bringing him news. Peoples of the Pacific Northwest of North America tell of Raven as a trickster who brought light to the world. In Japan, the three-legged crow Yatagarasu is a guide."],["Magpies and jays","The Eurasian magpie is one of the few animals known to recognize itself in a mirror, a sign of self-awareness. Blue jays, Steller’s jays and the colorful green jay of the Americas are also corvids. The Clark’s nutcracker can remember the locations of thousands of hidden pine seeds for months."]],
sp:["Betty the crow","In 2002, scientists at the University of Oxford gave a New Caledonian crow named Betty a straight piece of wire and a small bucket of food at the bottom of a tube. Betty bent the wire into a hook and used it to lift the bucket out. The experiment amazed scientists and helped show how inventive crows can be."],
g:[["Corvid","A member of the crow family"],["Songbird","A perching bird with a well-developed voice box"],["Cache","To hide food for later"],["Self-awareness","Recognizing oneself as an individual"]],
f:["The common raven is the largest songbird.","New Caledonian crows make hooked tools.","Crows can remember human faces for years."],
fun:"Some crows in Japan use passing cars to crack walnuts, then wait for the traffic lights to collect them safely."});

ADDART({id:"seahorse",t:"Seahorses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐴",w:"Seahorse",r:["reef","shark","octopus","eco","parentalcare"],
p:[["Fish with a horse’s head","Seahorses are small fish with heads shaped like a horse’s, curling tails and armor-like plates instead of scales."],["Fathers give birth","Male seahorses carry the eggs in a pouch and give birth to the babies."],["Slow swimmers","They are among the slowest fish, moving by fluttering a tiny fin on their backs."]],
l:"Seahorses are small marine fish found in shallow tropical and temperate waters around the world, among seagrass, coral reefs and mangroves. There are more than 40 species, ranging from less than 2 centimeters to about 35 centimeters long. They swim upright, anchor themselves with curling tails and suck up tiny shrimp through their tube-like snouts. Uniquely, it is the male seahorse that becomes pregnant and gives birth. Many species are threatened by habitat loss and overfishing.",
s:[["Strange fish","Seahorses are true fish, related to pipefish and sea dragons. Instead of scales, they have bony plates under their skin. They breathe with gills and have a swim bladder to control buoyancy. Their eyes move independently, like a chameleon’s, letting them look for food and danger at the same time."],["Slow but sneaky","A seahorse swims by fanning a small fin on its back up to about 50 times a second, steering with fins on its head. They are poor swimmers and cling to seagrass or coral with their tails in currents. To hunt, they creep up on tiny animals and snap them up with a quick upward flick of the head."],["Male pregnancy","After a courtship dance, which may last days, the female places her eggs into a pouch on the male’s belly. He fertilizes them and carries them for about two to four weeks, supplying oxygen and nutrients. He then gives birth to dozens to more than a thousand tiny babies, which must survive on their own."],["Masters of disguise","Many seahorses can change color to blend with their surroundings, and some grow skin filaments that look like seaweed. The pygmy seahorse, smaller than a fingernail, lives on sea fans and matches their color and bumps so well that it was only discovered when scientists collected a sea fan."]],
k:[["Species","More than 40"],["Size","Under 2 cm to about 35 cm"],["Who gives birth","The male"],["Habitat","Seagrass beds, coral reefs, mangroves"],["Relatives","Pipefish and sea dragons"]],
d:[["c. 350 BCE","Aristotle writes about pipefish carrying young"],["1758","Linnaeus describes the first seahorse species in his system"],["1969","The pygmy seahorse is discovered on a sea fan"],["2004","International trade in seahorses comes under CITES controls"],["2016","The first seahorse genome is sequenced"]],
s2:[["Threats","Millions of seahorses are caught each year, many as accidental catch in shrimp trawling nets, and many are dried for traditional medicine or sold as souvenirs and aquarium pets. Seagrass meadows and coral reefs, their homes, are being damaged by pollution, coastal development and warming seas."],["Protecting seahorses","Since 2004, international trade in all seahorse species has been regulated under CITES, the treaty on trade in endangered species. Marine protected areas and community projects, such as in the Philippines and Vietnam, help protect seahorse habitats and train fishers in sustainable practices."],["Pairs for life?","Many seahorse species are monogamous during a breeding season, and some pairs greet each other every morning with a dance, changing color and swimming side by side. This may help keep their breeding cycles in step. However, scientists have found that not all seahorses stay with one partner."],["Sea dragons","Leafy and weedy sea dragons, relatives of seahorses found off southern Australia, are covered in leaf-like flaps of skin that make them look like drifting seaweed. Like seahorses, the males carry the eggs, but on the underside of their tails rather than in a pouch."],["The seahorse in the brain","The hippocampus, a part of the brain important for memory, is named after the seahorse, whose scientific genus name is Hippocampus, because of its curved shape. The name comes from Greek words meaning horse and sea monster."],["Babies on their own","A newborn seahorse is only a few millimeters long and receives no care from its parents. Most are eaten by other animals or swept away by currents; scientists estimate that fewer than one in a thousand survives to adulthood. Producing many young helps the species survive."]],
sp:["Why do males give birth?","Scientists think male pregnancy may let seahorses have more babies: while the male carries one brood, the female can already be producing the next batch of eggs. The male’s pouch works much like a womb, controlling salt levels, supplying oxygen and even providing some nutrients to the developing young."],
g:[["Brood pouch","A pocket in which a male seahorse carries eggs"],["Monogamous","Having one partner"],["Camouflage","Colors or shapes that help an animal blend in"],["CITES","An international treaty controlling trade in threatened species"]],
f:["Male seahorses give birth.","Seahorses have no scales, but bony plates under their skin.","The brain’s hippocampus is named after the seahorse."],
fun:"Seahorses are fish, even though they swim upright and have no tail fin."});

ADDART({id:"crustacean",t:"Crabs, Shrimp and Other Crustaceans",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦀",w:"Crustacean",r:["insect","spider","reef","fish"],
p:[["Armored animals","Crustaceans are animals with hard outer skeletons and jointed legs, including crabs, lobsters, shrimp and barnacles."],["Mostly in water","Most live in the sea or fresh water, but woodlice and land crabs live on land."],["Hugely important","Tiny crustaceans such as krill and copepods are food for whales, fish and seabirds."]],
l:"Crustaceans are a large group of arthropods, animals with jointed legs and a hard outer skeleton, that includes crabs, lobsters, crayfish, shrimp, krill, barnacles and woodlice. There are more than 60,000 known species, from tiny water fleas to the Japanese spider crab, whose legs can span nearly 4 meters. Most live in oceans and fresh water, breathing through gills. Crustaceans are a vital part of food webs and an important source of food for people around the world.",
s:[["Body plan","Crustaceans have a hard exoskeleton made of chitin, often strengthened with calcium. Their bodies are divided into segments with many pairs of jointed limbs used for walking, swimming, feeding and breathing. Most have two pairs of antennae, which they use to touch and smell, and many have eyes on movable stalks."],["Growing by molting","Because their hard skeleton cannot stretch, crustaceans grow by molting: they shed the old shell, swell up by taking in water, and grow a new, larger shell, which takes time to harden. While soft, they are very vulnerable and often hide."],["Crabs","Crabs have broad, flat bodies with a short tail tucked underneath, and front legs with claws for feeding and defense. Many walk sideways. Hermit crabs protect their soft bodies by living in empty snail shells, moving to bigger shells as they grow. The coconut crab, the largest land invertebrate, can crack coconuts with its claws."],["Krill and copepods","Krill are small shrimp-like crustaceans that swarm in vast numbers in cold oceans. Antarctic krill may be the most abundant wild animal by weight on Earth and are the main food of blue whales, penguins and seals. Copepods, even smaller, are among the most numerous animals in the oceans."]],
k:[["Known species","More than 60,000"],["Largest by leg span","Japanese spider crab, nearly 4 m"],["Largest land invertebrate","Coconut crab"],["Skeleton made of","Chitin"],["Group","Arthropods"]],
d:[["c. 500 million years ago","Early crustaceans appear in the Cambrian seas"],["1758","Linnaeus classifies crabs and lobsters"],["1846–1854","Darwin studies barnacles for about eight years"],["1925","The Discovery Investigations begin studying Antarctic krill and whales"],["2021","UK law recognizes crabs and lobsters as sentient beings"]],
s2:[["Barnacles","Barnacles start life as free-swimming larvae, then glue themselves head-first to rocks, ships or whales and build hard shells around themselves. They kick food into their mouths with feathery legs. Charles Darwin spent about eight years studying barnacles before publishing On the Origin of Species."],["Mantis shrimp","Mantis shrimp, which live in tropical seas, have one of the fastest strikes in the animal kingdom. Their club-like limbs hit with the speed of a small bullet, cracking snail shells and even aquarium glass. They also have extraordinarily complex eyes with up to 16 types of color-detecting cells, compared with our three."],["Land crabs","On Christmas Island in the Indian Ocean, tens of millions of red crabs migrate from the forest to the sea each year to release their eggs. Roads are closed and special bridges and tunnels built to help them cross safely. The migration is timed by the rains and the phases of the Moon."],["Food for people","Shrimp, prawns, crabs, lobsters and crayfish are popular foods worldwide, and shrimp farming is a major industry in Asia and Latin America. Poorly managed shrimp farms have destroyed mangrove forests in some countries, and fishing for crustaceans must be managed to avoid overfishing."],["Do crustaceans feel pain?","Experiments suggest that crabs and lobsters may experience pain: they avoid places where they received electric shocks and tend injured body parts. In 2021 the United Kingdom recognized decapod crustaceans, such as crabs and lobsters, as sentient beings in animal welfare law."],["Woodlice","Woodlice, also called pill bugs or roly-polies, are crustaceans that live on land in damp places under logs and stones. Some can roll into a ball for protection. They help break down dead leaves and wood, returning nutrients to the soil."]],
sp:["The Japanese spider crab","The Japanese spider crab, which lives in deep waters off Japan, has the largest leg span of any arthropod, nearly 4 meters from claw to claw, though its body is only about 40 centimeters across. It may live for up to 100 years. Despite its fearsome appearance, it is a gentle scavenger that eats dead animals and plants on the seafloor."],
g:[["Crustacean","An arthropod with a hard shell, such as a crab or shrimp"],["Exoskeleton","A hard skeleton on the outside of the body"],["Molting","Shedding an old shell to grow"],["Krill","Small shrimp-like crustaceans that swarm in the ocean"]],
f:["Crabs, shrimp and barnacles are all crustaceans.","Antarctic krill are among the most abundant animals on Earth.","Mantis shrimp have up to 16 types of color receptors."],
fun:"Woodlice, the roly-polies found under logs, are crustaceans like crabs."});

;
/* data/living-3.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, living part 3: snails and shellfish, dragonflies, termites and earthworms.
ADDART({id:"mollusc",t:"Snails, Clams and Other Molluscs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐌",w:"Mollusca",r:["octopus","crustacean","reef","worms"],
p:[["Soft-bodied animals","Molluscs are soft-bodied animals, many protected by shells, including snails, slugs, clams, oysters and squid."],["A huge group","With about 85,000 known species, molluscs are one of the largest groups of animals."],["From gardens to the deep sea","They live on land, in fresh water and throughout the oceans."]],
l:"Molluscs are a large and varied group of invertebrates with soft bodies, most of which are protected by a hard shell made of calcium carbonate. The group includes gastropods such as snails and slugs, bivalves such as clams, mussels, oysters and scallops, and cephalopods such as octopuses, squid and cuttlefish. With about 85,000 living species, molluscs range from tiny snails less than a millimeter long to the colossal squid, which can weigh about 500 kilograms. They are important in food webs and as food for people.",
s:[["Body plan","Most molluscs have a muscular foot for moving or digging, a soft body containing the organs, and a fold of tissue called the mantle, which makes the shell. Many have a radula, a tongue-like ribbon covered in tiny teeth, used to scrape up food. Shells grow from the edge as the animal grows, often in beautiful spirals."],["Snails and slugs","Gastropods, meaning stomach-foot, are the largest group of molluscs. Snails carry a coiled shell and can pull inside for protection, while slugs have lost their shells or have only tiny internal ones. Land snails and slugs glide on a layer of slime, which protects them and helps them stick to surfaces, even upside down."],["Clams and oysters","Bivalves have two hinged shells. Most filter food from water, cleaning it as they feed: a single oyster can filter dozens of liters a day. Oysters make pearls when an irritant gets inside the shell and they coat it in layers of nacre, or mother-of-pearl. The giant clam of the Indo-Pacific can weigh over 200 kilograms."],["Cephalopods","Squid, octopuses, cuttlefish and nautiluses are cephalopods, meaning head-foot. They have arms or tentacles, large brains and excellent eyes, and most can change color in an instant. The nautilus still has a coiled external shell, but squid and cuttlefish have small internal shells, and octopuses have almost none."]],
k:[["Known species","About 85,000"],["Main groups","Gastropods, bivalves, cephalopods"],["Shell made of","Calcium carbonate"],["Largest","Colossal squid, about 500 kg"],["Oldest known animal","Ming, an ocean quahog clam, 507 years"]],
d:[["c. 540 million years ago","Early molluscs appear in the oceans"],["c. 1500 BCE","Phoenicians make purple dye from sea snails"],["1758","Linnaeus classifies molluscs"],["2006","Ming the clam is found off Iceland"],["2007","The heaviest colossal squid is caught near Antarctica"]],
s2:[["Purple dye","The Phoenicians of the eastern Mediterranean made a precious purple dye from murex sea snails. Thousands of snails were needed for a single gram of dye, so purple cloth became a symbol of royalty and power. Roman emperors wore Tyrian purple, named after the Phoenician city of Tyre."],["Ming the clam","In 2006 scientists dredged up an ocean quahog clam off Iceland and later counted the growth bands in its shell. It turned out to be 507 years old, having started life around 1499, making it the oldest individual animal whose age has been precisely measured. It was nicknamed Ming, after the Chinese dynasty ruling when it was born."],["Snails and disease","Some freshwater snails carry tiny parasitic worms that cause schistosomiasis, also called snail fever, a disease affecting more than 200 million people, mainly in Africa. Controlling snails and improving water and sanitation help prevent it."],["Pearls","Natural pearls are very rare, so most pearls today are cultured: pearl farmers place a small bead inside an oyster or mussel, which coats it in nacre over months or years. Kokichi Mikimoto in Japan pioneered cultured pearls in the 1890s. The South Sea pearls of Australia, Indonesia and the Philippines are among the largest."],["Molluscs in danger","Many land snails, especially on islands such as Hawaii and Tahiti, have become extinct because of habitat loss and introduced predators. Ocean acidification, caused by the ocean absorbing carbon dioxide, makes it harder for shellfish such as oysters and some snails to build their shells."],["Snail speed","A garden snail moves at about 1 millimeter per second, or around 50 meters per hour at top speed. Its slime is so useful that scientists study it for medical glues and skin care products. Some snails can sleep for months during dry weather, sealing their shells with a layer of dried mucus."]],
sp:["The giant clam","Giant clams live on coral reefs in the Indian and Pacific Oceans and can grow over a meter long. Tiny algae living in their colorful mantles make food from sunlight and share it with the clam. Despite stories, giant clams do not trap divers. They are protected in many countries because they were heavily harvested for food and shells."],
g:[["Mollusc","A soft-bodied invertebrate, often with a shell"],["Mantle","The fold of tissue that forms a mollusc’s shell"],["Radula","A ribbon of tiny teeth used for feeding"],["Nacre","Mother-of-pearl, the shiny material in shells and pearls"]],
f:["Snails, clams and squid are all molluscs.","A clam named Ming lived for 507 years.","Oysters make pearls by coating irritants in nacre."],
fun:"A clam found off Iceland was 507 years old, born around the time Columbus sailed."});

ADDART({id:"dragonfly",t:"Dragonflies",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪰",w:"Dragonfly",r:["insect","butterfly","mosquito","wetlands"],
p:[["Ancient fliers","Dragonflies and their relatives have existed for more than 300 million years, since before the dinosaurs."],["Expert hunters","Adult dragonflies catch insects in midair and succeed in most of their hunts."],["Life in water","Young dragonflies, called nymphs, live underwater for months or years before becoming adults."]],
l:"Dragonflies are fast-flying insects with long bodies, two pairs of transparent wings and huge eyes. Together with the slimmer damselflies, they form the order Odonata, with about 6,000 species found on every continent except Antarctica. They begin life underwater as fierce nymphs, then climb out and transform into winged adults that hunt other insects, including mosquitoes, in flight. Dragonflies are among the most skilled fliers in the animal world and good indicators of healthy wetlands.",
s:[["Superb fliers","Dragonflies can move each of their four wings independently, letting them hover, fly backward, turn sharply and reach speeds of over 30 kilometers per hour. Engineers study their flight to design small flying robots and drones."],["Eyes that see almost everything","A dragonfly’s eyes cover most of its head and contain up to about 30,000 tiny lenses each, giving almost all-around vision. They can see colors and ultraviolet light and track moving prey precisely, predicting where it will go."],["Deadly hunters","Dragonflies catch prey with their legs, forming a basket to scoop insects out of the air. Studies have found they succeed in more than 90 percent of hunts, one of the highest rates of any predator. A single dragonfly can eat dozens to hundreds of mosquitoes a day."],["Underwater nymphs","Dragonfly eggs hatch in ponds, lakes and streams into nymphs. Nymphs breathe through gills inside their bodies and catch tadpoles, small fish and insect larvae with a hinged lower lip that shoots out like a spring. After months or years, a nymph climbs a plant stem, splits its skin and emerges as an adult."]],
k:[["Order","Odonata (dragonflies and damselflies)"],["Species","About 6,000"],["Speed","Over 30 km/h"],["Hunting success","Over 90% in some studies"],["Ancient relatives","Griffinflies, wingspan about 70 cm"]],
d:[["c. 320 million years ago","Early relatives of dragonflies take to the air"],["c. 300 million years ago","Giant griffinflies like Meganeuropsis live"],["1758","Linnaeus names dragonflies Libellula"],["1990s","Scientists track the migration of globe skimmers across the Indian Ocean"],["2016","Study confirms globe skimmers travel up to 18,000 km"]],
s2:[["Giants of the past","About 300 million years ago, relatives of dragonflies called griffinflies, such as Meganeuropsis, had wingspans of about 70 centimeters, the largest insects ever known. Higher oxygen levels in the air at that time may have allowed insects to grow so large."],["Dragonfly or damselfly?","Dragonflies are sturdier and usually rest with their wings spread flat. Damselflies are slender and usually fold their wings along their bodies when resting. Dragonflies’ eyes almost touch on top of the head, while damselflies’ eyes are separated."],["Long-distance travelers","The globe skimmer, also called the wandering glider, makes one of the longest insect migrations known, crossing the Indian Ocean between India and East Africa over several generations, a round trip of up to about 18,000 kilometers. It rides high winds of the monsoon."],["Signs of clean water","Because dragonfly nymphs need clean, unpolluted water, the presence of many dragonfly species is a sign of a healthy wetland. Their decline can warn of pollution or habitat loss. Building garden ponds helps dragonflies and many other animals."],["Dragonflies and people","In Japan, dragonflies, called tombo, are symbols of courage, strength and summer, and an old name for Japan, Akitsushima, means island of dragonflies. Many cultures admire them, though in parts of Europe old folk names, such as devil’s darning needle, reflect fear."],["Colors","Dragonflies come in brilliant blues, reds, greens and golds. Some colors come from pigments and some from tiny structures that reflect light. Many males are brightly colored to attract mates and warn rivals, while females are often duller."]],
sp:["A 300-million-year success","Dragonflies were among the first animals to fly, long before birds, bats or even dinosaurs existed. Their basic body plan has changed little in hundreds of millions of years, a sign of how well it works. They survived the great extinction that wiped out the dinosaurs and are still thriving today."],
g:[["Nymph","The young, water-living stage of a dragonfly"],["Odonata","The insect order of dragonflies and damselflies"],["Compound eye","An eye made of many tiny lenses"],["Migration","A seasonal journey from one region to another"]],
f:["Dragonflies have existed for over 300 million years.","They catch prey in more than 90 percent of their hunts.","Dragonfly nymphs live underwater."],
fun:"Some dragonflies migrate across the Indian Ocean, a round trip of up to 18,000 kilometers."});

ADDART({id:"termite",t:"Termites",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🏰",w:"Termite",r:["ants","insect","bee","soil"],
p:[["Insect builders","Termites are social insects that live in large colonies and build some of the most impressive structures in the animal world."],["Wood eaters","They feed on wood, dead plants and leaf litter, helping to recycle nutrients."],["Not ants","Though sometimes called white ants, termites are more closely related to cockroaches."]],
l:"Termites are social insects that live in colonies ranging from hundreds to millions of members, led by a king and queen. There are about 3,000 known species, most in tropical regions. Termites feed on cellulose, the tough material in wood and plants, with the help of microbes in their guts. In doing so, they break down dead plant matter and enrich soils, making them vital to many ecosystems. Some species, however, damage wooden buildings and furniture.",
s:[["The colony","A termite colony has castes. The king and queen reproduce; in some species the queen grows to many times the size of the workers and lays thousands of eggs a day. Workers gather food, build and care for the young. Soldiers, with large heads and powerful jaws, defend the colony; in some species they spray sticky chemicals."],["Eating wood","Few animals can digest cellulose, but termites rely on bacteria and other microbes in their guts to break it down. Some termites even farm fungus inside their mounds, feeding it chewed plant material and then eating the fungus."],["Mound builders","In the African savanna and Australian outback, some termites build mounds of soil, saliva and droppings that can tower over 8 meters. Inside, tunnels and chambers help control temperature and humidity. The magnetic termites of northern Australia build flat, wedge-shaped mounds aligned north to south."],["Termites and cockroaches","Genetic studies show that termites are a branch of the cockroach family tree that became social. Their resemblance to ants is due to their similar lifestyle; ants are actually closer relatives of bees and wasps."]],
k:[["Species","About 3,000"],["Colony size","Hundreds to millions"],["Closest relatives","Cockroaches"],["Tallest mounds","Over 8 m"],["Food","Cellulose from wood and plants"]],
d:[["c. 130 million years ago","Termites appear in the age of dinosaurs"],["1700s","Naturalists describe African termite mounds"],["2007","Genetic study confirms termites are social cockroaches"],["2018","Termite mounds in Brazil found to be about 4,000 years old"],["2015","Study shows termite mounds help dry grasslands resist drought"]],
s2:[["Natural air conditioning","Some termite mounds have networks of tunnels that let air circulate, keeping the nest at a steady temperature and humidity. Architects have studied them; the Eastgate Centre in Harare, Zimbabwe, opened in 1996, used ideas inspired by termite mounds to cool the building with far less energy."],["A city seen from space","In northeastern Brazil, termites have built about 200 million regularly spaced mounds covering an area about as large as Great Britain. Some mounds are around 4,000 years old. Together they contain enough soil to make thousands of Great Pyramids of Giza."],["Ecosystem engineers","Termites aerate soil, help rain soak into the ground and recycle nutrients from dead wood and leaves. Their mounds create rich patches where plants grow well, attracting other animals. Studies suggest that termite mounds help savannas survive droughts."],["Food for others","Termites are an important food for many animals, including aardvarks, pangolins, anteaters, chimpanzees and birds. Chimpanzees use sticks as tools to fish termites out of mounds. In parts of Africa and Asia, people eat termites too, as a rich source of protein."],["Pests and methane","A few termite species damage houses, bridges and furniture, causing large repair costs. Termites also release methane, a greenhouse gas, as their gut microbes digest wood, though they produce only a small share of global emissions."],["Long-lived queens","Termite queens can live for decades, far longer than most insects. In some species, a queen can lay tens of thousands of eggs a day for years. Scientists study termites to learn how they live so long and stay healthy."]],
sp:["Mounds older than the pyramids","In 2018 scientists reported that the mounds in northeastern Brazil, each about 2.5 meters tall and 9 meters wide, cover around 230,000 square kilometers. Dating of the soil showed that some were built about 4,000 years ago, around the time the pyramids of Egypt were built, making them among the oldest known structures built by insects."],
g:[["Colony","A group of insects living and working together"],["Caste","A group within a colony with a particular role"],["Cellulose","The tough material that makes up plant cell walls"],["Ecosystem engineer","An animal that changes its environment in ways that help others"]],
f:["Termites are closely related to cockroaches.","Some termite mounds are over 8 meters tall.","Termite mounds inspired a building design in Zimbabwe."],
fun:"Termites in Brazil built about 200 million mounds over an area as large as Great Britain."});

ADDART({id:"worms",t:"Earthworms and Other Worms",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪱",w:"Earthworm",r:["soil","eco","insect","darwin","centipedes"],
p:[["Soil builders","Earthworms burrow through soil, mixing it and turning dead leaves into rich earth."],["Many kinds of worm","Worms include earthworms, leeches, flatworms, roundworms and colorful sea worms."],["Darwin’s favorite","Charles Darwin studied earthworms for decades and wrote a book about them."]],
l:"Worms are soft-bodied animals with long, tube-shaped bodies and no legs. The best-known are earthworms, segmented worms that live in soil around the world, but the word worm covers several unrelated groups, including flatworms, roundworms and marine bristle worms. Earthworms are vital for healthy soil: by burrowing and eating dead plant matter, they mix and aerate the soil, help water drain and produce nutrient-rich castings. Farmers and gardeners value them highly.",
s:[["How earthworms live","An earthworm’s body is made of many ring-like segments, each with tiny bristles that grip the soil. It moves by squeezing muscles that make segments longer and thinner, then shorter and fatter. Earthworms have no lungs and breathe through their moist skin, which is why they come to the surface in heavy rain."],["Soil engineers","Earthworms swallow soil as they burrow, digesting bits of dead leaves and microbes and leaving behind castings rich in nutrients. Their tunnels let air and water reach plant roots. A healthy field can contain more than a million earthworms per hectare."],["Worms of many kinds","Flatworms, such as planarians, can regrow a whole body from a small piece. Roundworms, or nematodes, are among the most numerous animals on Earth, living in soil, water and inside other animals. Leeches, relatives of earthworms, include species that suck blood and others that hunt small animals."],["Sea worms","The oceans are home to many colorful worms. Feather duster and Christmas tree worms on coral reefs spread fan-like tentacles to catch food. Giant tube worms near deep-sea hot springs grow over 2 meters long and get their food from bacteria living inside them."]],
k:[["Earthworm species","About 7,000"],["Longest earthworm","Giant Gippsland earthworm, up to about 3 m"],["Breathe through","Their skin"],["Main groups of worms","Segmented worms, flatworms, roundworms"],["Darwin’s book","The Formation of Vegetable Mould through the Action of Worms (1881)"]],
d:[["c. 550 million years ago","Worm-like animals leave burrows in ancient seafloors"],["1881","Darwin publishes his book on earthworms"],["1977","Giant tube worms are discovered at deep-sea vents"],["1998","The roundworm C. elegans becomes the first animal to have its genome sequenced"],["2002","Nobel Prize for research using C. elegans"]],
s2:[["Darwin and the worms","Charles Darwin studied earthworms for about 40 years. He measured how they slowly bury stones and ancient ruins by bringing soil to the surface, and even tested whether they could hear by playing a piano and a bassoon to them. His last book, published in 1881, showed how important worms are for shaping soil and landscapes."],["A worm that changed science","The tiny roundworm Caenorhabditis elegans, about a millimeter long, has helped scientists understand how genes control development, aging and cell death. In 1998 it became the first animal to have its complete genome sequenced. Research using it has led to several Nobel Prizes."],["Medicinal leeches","For centuries, doctors used leeches to drain blood, often harmfully. Today medicinal leeches are used carefully in some hospitals after surgery to reattach fingers or ears, because they help blood flow and release substances that stop clotting."],["Parasitic worms","Some worms live inside other animals as parasites, including tapeworms, hookworms and roundworms. Parasitic worm infections affect more than a billion people worldwide, especially children in places without clean water and sanitation. Medicines given in schools and better sanitation help control them."],["Giant earthworms","The giant Gippsland earthworm of southeastern Australia can grow up to about 3 meters long. It lives deep in moist soil and makes gurgling sounds as it moves through its burrows. It is rare and protected."],["Composting with worms","Many people use worms to turn food scraps and garden waste into compost, a method called vermicomposting. Red wiggler worms are often used because they eat quickly and thrive in compost bins, producing rich fertilizer for gardens."]],
sp:["Regrowing a body","Planarian flatworms are famous for their power of regeneration. If one is cut into pieces, each piece can grow into a complete new worm, including a new head and brain. Scientists study them to learn how cells rebuild lost body parts, hoping one day to apply such knowledge to medicine."],
g:[["Segmented worm","A worm with a body made of ring-like sections"],["Castings","Nutrient-rich waste left by earthworms"],["Nematode","A roundworm"],["Regeneration","Regrowing lost body parts"]],
f:["Earthworms breathe through their skin.","Darwin wrote a book about earthworms in 1881.","A planarian flatworm can regrow from a small piece."],
fun:"Darwin played a piano and a bassoon to earthworms to test whether they could hear."});

;
/* data/living-4.js */
// Animal Kingdom (plants) articles, living part 4: bamboo, cacti, carnivorous plants, and mosses and ferns.
ADDART({id:"bamboo",t:"Bamboo",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🎋",w:"Bamboo",r:["panda","trees","photo","flower","sugarcane"],
p:[["A giant grass","Bamboo is not a tree but a giant grass, the tallest member of the grass family."],["Record grower","Some bamboos can grow nearly a meter in a single day, making them among the fastest-growing plants."],["Thousands of uses","People use bamboo for building, furniture, food, paper, musical instruments and much more."]],
l:"Bamboo is a group of more than 1,400 species of fast-growing grasses with woody, hollow stems called culms. Found naturally in Asia, Africa, the Americas and northern Australia, bamboo grows from cool mountain slopes to tropical forests. Some species reach over 30 meters tall. Strong, light and renewable, bamboo has been used by people for thousands of years for houses, scaffolding, tools, food and crafts, and it is the main food of the giant panda.",
s:[["Growing fast","Bamboo grows from underground stems called rhizomes. New shoots push up already as wide as they will ever be and stretch upward quickly; certain species have been measured growing about 90 centimeters in a day. A stem reaches its full height in a few months, then hardens over the next few years."],["Strong and light","Bamboo culms are hollow, divided into sections by solid nodes. This design makes them light but very strong for their weight. Some bamboo has a tensile strength comparable to steel, and in many parts of Asia it is used for scaffolding on tall buildings."],["Flowering all at once","Many bamboos flower only once every few decades, sometimes more than 100 years, and all plants of the same species flower at the same time, even in different countries, then die. The sudden supply of seeds can cause rat populations to explode, leading to famines in places such as northeastern India."],["Running and clumping","Running bamboos send rhizomes far underground and can spread quickly, sometimes becoming invasive. Clumping bamboos grow in tight groups. Gardeners plant clumping types or use barriers to keep running types from taking over."]],
k:[["Family","Grasses (Poaceae)"],["Species","More than 1,400"],["Fastest growth","About 90 cm in a day"],["Tallest","Over 30 m"],["Main users","People and giant pandas"]],
d:[["c. 500 BCE","Texts are written on bamboo strips in China (earliest surviving examples)"],["c. 105 CE","Paper is developed in China, later sometimes made from bamboo"],["1880","Thomas Edison uses carbonized bamboo in early light bulbs"],["1997","International Network for Bamboo and Rattan is founded"],["2009","First World Bamboo Day is held on 18 September"]],
s2:[["Bamboo in daily life","Bamboo is used for houses, bridges, floors, furniture, baskets, mats, chopsticks, fishing rods and musical instruments such as flutes and the angklung. Young bamboo shoots are eaten in many Asian dishes. Fibers from bamboo are made into paper and cloth."],["Writing on bamboo","Before paper became common, scribes in ancient China wrote on strips of bamboo tied together with cords to form books. Many important early texts survive on bamboo slips discovered by archaeologists in tombs."],["Light bulbs","When Thomas Edison was searching for a long-lasting filament for his electric light bulbs, his team tested thousands of materials. Carbonized bamboo from Japan worked well, and was used in his bulbs for several years in the 1880s."],["Green material?","Because it grows quickly and regrows from its roots after cutting, bamboo is promoted as a sustainable material that can replace some wood and plastics. Bamboo forests also absorb carbon dioxide and protect soil from erosion. However, processing and transporting bamboo products can use chemicals and energy."],["Bamboo forests","Famous bamboo forests include Arashiyama in Kyoto, Japan, where tall stems sway and creak in the wind, and the bamboo forests of Sichuan in China, home to giant pandas. Many animals besides pandas depend on bamboo, including red pandas, mountain gorillas and bamboo lemurs."],["The angklung","The angklung, a musical instrument from Indonesia made of bamboo tubes in a frame, produces a single note when shaken. A group of players, each with one or two angklungs, can play whole melodies together. UNESCO added it to its list of the world’s intangible cultural heritage in 2010."]],
sp:["Faster than you can watch","Some species of bamboo grow so fast that, on a warm, wet day, you could almost see them move. Growth of nearly a meter in 24 hours works out to about 4 centimeters an hour. Bamboo achieves this not by making many new cells quickly but by rapidly stretching cells that formed while the shoot was still underground."],
g:[["Culm","The woody, hollow stem of bamboo"],["Rhizome","An underground stem that sends up new shoots"],["Node","A solid joint dividing a bamboo stem into sections"],["Invasive","Spreading quickly into places where it is not wanted"]],
f:["Bamboo is a grass, not a tree.","Some bamboo grows nearly a meter in a day.","Many bamboos flower all at once only every few decades."],
fun:"Thomas Edison used bamboo from Japan to make the filaments of early light bulbs."});

ADDART({id:"cactus",t:"Cacti and Desert Plants",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌵",w:"Cactus",r:["desert","flower","mexicocountry","photo","plantdefenses","agave"],
p:[["Surviving drought","Cacti and other desert plants have special ways to survive with very little water."],["Spines, not leaves","Most cacti have spines instead of leaves, which protect them and reduce water loss."],["Native to the Americas","Almost all of the roughly 1,750 cactus species are native to the Americas."]],
l:"Cacti are a family of flowering plants adapted to dry environments, with about 1,750 species. Nearly all are native to the Americas, from Canada to Patagonia, though some have spread to other continents. Cacti store water in thick, fleshy stems, have spines instead of leaves and often have waxy skins that slow evaporation. They range from tiny button cacti to the giant saguaro, which can grow over 12 meters tall. Other desert plants, such as succulents and acacias, have their own clever ways to survive drought.",
s:[["Storing water","Cacti store water in spongy tissue inside their stems. Many have pleated or ribbed stems that can swell like an accordion after rain and shrink during drought. A large saguaro can hold several metric tons of water."],["Spines","A cactus’s spines are modified leaves. They protect the plant from thirsty animals, provide shade, trap a layer of still air that reduces water loss, and in some species collect dew. Some cacti are covered with soft white hairs that reflect sunlight."],["Saving water","Cacti open their breathing pores, called stomata, at night, when it is cooler, to take in carbon dioxide, storing it until daytime for photosynthesis. This special process, called CAM photosynthesis, greatly reduces water loss. Their wide, shallow roots quickly soak up rain."],["Flowers and fruit","Many cacti produce large, colorful flowers, often blooming for only a day or a night. They are pollinated by bees, birds, moths and bats. Cactus fruits, such as prickly pears and dragon fruit, are eaten by animals and people."]],
k:[["Family","Cactaceae"],["Species","About 1,750"],["Native to","The Americas"],["Tallest","Mexican giant cardon and saguaro, over 12 m"],["Special photosynthesis","CAM"]],
d:[["Thousands of years ago","People in Mexico begin using prickly pear cactus for food"],["1325","Legend says the Aztecs found Tenochtitlan where an eagle sat on a cactus"],["1500s","Spanish explorers carry prickly pears to Europe"],["1920s","Cactus moths are used to control invasive prickly pears in Australia"],["1994","Saguaro National Park is established in Arizona"]],
s2:[["The saguaro","The saguaro cactus of the Sonoran Desert in the southwestern United States and northwestern Mexico grows very slowly, taking about 10 years to reach a few centimeters and 50 to 70 years to grow its first arm. It can live for 150 years or more. Woodpeckers carve nest holes in it, later used by owls and other birds."],["Mexico’s cactus","The prickly pear, or nopal, is important in Mexican food and culture. Its pads and fruits are eaten, and it appears on Mexico’s flag, which shows an eagle perched on a cactus eating a snake, recalling the legend of the founding of the Aztec capital, Tenochtitlan."],["Other desert survivors","Plants in other deserts have evolved similar features independently. In Africa, euphorbias look remarkably like cacti, with spines and fleshy stems. Living stones, or lithops, look like pebbles to avoid being eaten. Welwitschia, in the Namib Desert, has just two long leaves and can live for over 1,000 years."],["Invasive cacti","Prickly pears brought to Australia in the 1800s spread over millions of hectares of farmland. In the 1920s scientists introduced the cactus moth from South America, whose caterpillars eat prickly pear, and it cleared vast areas, one of the most successful cases of biological control."],["Cacti in danger","Many cacti are threatened by collection for the plant trade, farming and building. A 2015 study found that about 31 percent of cactus species are threatened with extinction, making them one of the most threatened groups of living things assessed."],["Dragon fruit","Dragon fruit, or pitaya, comes from climbing cacti native to Central America. Its bright pink or yellow skin covers white or red flesh dotted with tiny black seeds. It is now grown widely in Vietnam, the Philippines, Thailand, China and other countries."]],
sp:["Night bloomers","Some cacti, such as the night-blooming cereus and queen of the night, open their large, fragrant white flowers for just one night, often only once a year. They are pollinated by moths and bats. In some places, people gather to watch them bloom, and botanical gardens hold special night openings."],
g:[["Succulent","A plant with thick, fleshy parts that store water"],["Spine","A sharp, modified leaf on a cactus"],["Stomata","Tiny pores in plants that let gases in and out"],["CAM photosynthesis","A way of photosynthesis that saves water by opening pores at night"]],
f:["A cactus’s spines are modified leaves.","A saguaro takes 50 to 70 years to grow its first arm.","About 31 percent of cactus species are threatened."],
fun:"A cactus appears on the national flag of Mexico."});

ADDART({id:"carnivplant",t:"Carnivorous Plants",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪴",w:"Carnivorous plant",r:["flower","insect","photo","wetlands","mimosa"],
p:[["Plants that eat animals","Carnivorous plants trap and digest insects and other small animals."],["Clever traps","They use snapping leaves, slippery pitchers, sticky hairs and underwater bladders."],["Living in poor soils","Most grow in bogs and other places where the soil lacks nutrients such as nitrogen."]],
l:"Carnivorous plants are plants that attract, trap and digest animals, mostly insects, to obtain nutrients. There are more than 800 species, including the Venus flytrap, pitcher plants, sundews and bladderworts. They still make their own food by photosynthesis, but catch prey to get nutrients such as nitrogen and phosphorus that are scarce in the wet, acidic soils where they usually grow. Charles Darwin was fascinated by them and wrote a book about them in 1875.",
s:[["Snap traps","The Venus flytrap, which grows wild only in a small area of North and South Carolina in the United States, has hinged leaves lined with trigger hairs. If an insect touches two hairs within about 20 seconds, the trap snaps shut in a fraction of a second. It then seals and digests the prey over several days."],["Pitfall traps","Pitcher plants have leaves shaped like jugs, filled with digestive fluid. Insects are lured by nectar and color to the slippery rim, fall in and cannot climb out. Asian Nepenthes pitchers, found from Southeast Asia to Australia, can be large enough to catch frogs and occasionally small mammals."],["Sticky traps","Sundews and butterworts cover their leaves with glistening drops of sticky glue that look like nectar or dew. Insects get stuck, and the leaves or tentacles slowly curl around them. Sundews are found on every continent except Antarctica."],["Suction traps","Bladderworts, which float in water or grow in wet soil, have tiny bladders that are kept under low pressure. When a small water animal brushes a trigger hair, the bladder’s door opens and sucks it in within a fraction of a millisecond, one of the fastest movements in the plant world."]],
k:[["Species","More than 800"],["Famous examples","Venus flytrap, pitcher plants, sundews, bladderworts"],["Why carnivorous","To get nitrogen and other nutrients"],["Habitat","Bogs, swamps and poor soils"],["Darwin’s book","Insectivorous Plants (1875)"]],
d:[["1760","The Venus flytrap is described by Arthur Dobbs"],["1875","Darwin publishes Insectivorous Plants"],["2007","A rat-trapping pitcher plant is discovered in the Philippines"],["2009","Nepenthes attenboroughii is named after David Attenborough"],["2016","Study shows Venus flytraps count touches"]],
s2:[["Darwin’s favorite plants","Charles Darwin experimented with sundews, feeding them meat, egg and even his own hair, to show that they digested animal matter. He called the Venus flytrap one of the most wonderful plants in the world. His 1875 book Insectivorous Plants helped prove that plants could be carnivorous."],["Counting plants","In 2016 scientists found that the Venus flytrap effectively counts. One touch does nothing, two close the trap, and after five or more the trap begins producing digestive enzymes. This helps the plant avoid wasting energy on raindrops or tiny insects that could escape."],["Pitcher plants of the Philippines","Many species of Nepenthes grow in the Philippines, Borneo and Sumatra. Nepenthes attenboroughii, discovered on Mount Victoria in Palawan and named after the naturalist David Attenborough in 2009, has pitchers large enough to trap rats. New species are still being found on remote mountains."],["Partners not prey","Some pitcher plants have unusual partnerships. In Borneo, one species provides a toilet for tree shrews, which feed on nectar and leave droppings in the pitcher, supplying nitrogen. Another hosts bats that roost inside it. Some insects and spiders live safely inside pitchers, eating trapped prey."],["Threats","Many carnivorous plants are rare and threatened by draining of wetlands, development, fire suppression and illegal collection. The wild Venus flytrap is protected, and digging it up in North Carolina is a felony."],["Growing them at home","Carnivorous plants are popular with gardeners. Most need rainwater or distilled water, lots of light, and poor, acidic soil such as peat moss. They should not be fed hamburger or fertilizer, which can kill them; they catch insects themselves."]],
sp:["Rat-eating plants","In 2007 scientists exploring Mount Victoria on the island of Palawan in the Philippines found a pitcher plant with pitchers up to about 30 centimeters tall. Named Nepenthes attenboroughii, it is one of the largest carnivorous plants and has been reported to trap rats, though it mostly catches insects."],
g:[["Carnivorous plant","A plant that traps and digests animals"],["Pitcher","A jug-shaped leaf that traps insects"],["Enzyme","A substance that speeds up chemical reactions, such as digestion"],["Bog","Wet, spongy ground with acidic soil"]],
f:["The Venus flytrap grows wild only in North and South Carolina.","Bladderworts have one of the fastest movements of any plant.","Some pitcher plants can trap rats."],
fun:"A Venus flytrap only shuts if an insect touches its trigger hairs twice within about 20 seconds."});

ADDART({id:"ferns",t:"Mosses and Ferns",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌿",w:"Fern",r:["trees","photo","flower","fungi","peatlands","cloudforests","plantevolution","airplants"],
p:[["Plants without flowers","Mosses and ferns are plants that reproduce by spores instead of seeds and never make flowers."],["Ancient plants","Their ancestors were among the first plants to grow on land, hundreds of millions of years ago."],["Coal from ferns","Giant ferns and their relatives formed the forests whose remains became coal."]],
l:"Mosses and ferns are non-flowering plants that reproduce by spores rather than seeds. Mosses are small, soft plants without true roots or veins that grow in dense carpets in damp places. Ferns are larger, with true roots, stems and veins, and feathery leaves called fronds. Both groups descend from some of the earliest land plants. Today there are about 12,000 species of moss and about 10,500 species of fern and their relatives, found from rainforests to the Arctic.",
s:[["Mosses","Mosses have no true roots, but tiny threads called rhizoids that anchor them. Without veins to carry water, they absorb it directly through their leaves, which is why they grow in damp, shady places and stay small. Many can dry out completely and revive when it rains. Peat moss, or sphagnum, forms vast bogs."],["Ferns","Ferns have veins that carry water and food, letting them grow larger. New fronds unroll from tight coils called fiddleheads. Ferns range from tiny floating water ferns to tree ferns over 10 meters tall, which grow in tropical and subtropical forests, from Australia and New Zealand to the Philippines."],["Spores","Instead of seeds, mosses and ferns release spores, tiny cells carried by wind. Ferns make spores in brown clusters on the undersides of their fronds. A spore grows into a small, separate plant that produces eggs and sperm; the sperm must swim through a film of water to reach an egg, so these plants need moisture to reproduce."],["Pioneers","Mosses are often among the first plants to grow on bare rock or soil, after lichens. They trap dust and water, slowly building soil in which other plants can grow. Mosses help prevent erosion and hold huge amounts of water, releasing it slowly to streams."]],
k:[["Moss species","About 12,000"],["Fern species","About 10,500"],["Reproduce by","Spores"],["Tallest ferns","Tree ferns, over 10 m"],["First land plants","Over 470 million years ago"]],
d:[["c. 470 million years ago","The first plants colonize land"],["c. 360 million years ago","Fern-like plants form vast forests"],["c. 300 million years ago","Coal swamp forests flourish"],["1840s–1890s","Fern craze, called Pteridomania, sweeps Victorian Britain"],["2018","Fern genomes are sequenced for the first time"]],
s2:[["Forests that became coal","About 300 million years ago, in the Carboniferous period, swampy forests of giant club mosses, horsetails, tree ferns and early seed plants covered much of the land. When they died, they were buried and slowly turned into coal. The name Carboniferous means coal-bearing."],["Peat bogs","Sphagnum moss forms peat bogs, where dead moss builds up over thousands of years without fully rotting. Peatlands cover only about 3 percent of Earth’s land but store about twice as much carbon as all the world’s forests. Draining or burning them releases this carbon, adding to climate change."],["Bog bodies","Because peat bogs are acidic, cold and low in oxygen, they can preserve things for thousands of years. Bodies of people from the Iron Age, such as Tollund Man in Denmark, have been found in bogs with skin, hair and even their last meals preserved."],["Fern fever","In the mid-1800s, a craze for ferns, called Pteridomania, swept Britain. People collected ferns from the countryside, grew them in glass cases and decorated pottery, fabric and gravestones with fern patterns. Some wild ferns became rare because of overcollecting."],["Ferns we eat and use","Young fiddleheads of some ferns are eaten as vegetables in parts of Asia and North America, such as pako in the Philippines. Water ferns called Azolla live with bacteria that capture nitrogen and are used in rice paddies as natural fertilizer. Some ferns, however, are poisonous."],["Mosses everywhere","Mosses grow on every continent, including Antarctica, where they are among the few plants that survive. Some mosses can survive being frozen for centuries: in 2014 scientists revived moss that had been buried under Antarctic ice for about 1,500 years."]],
sp:["Reviving ancient moss","In 2014 British scientists took a core of moss from deep in an Antarctic moss bank, where it had been frozen for about 1,500 years. Placed in a warm, lit incubator, the moss began to grow again within weeks. The experiment showed that some plants can survive very long periods of freezing, which may help them recolonize land after ice ages."],
g:[["Spore","A tiny reproductive cell that can grow into a new plant"],["Frond","The leaf of a fern"],["Fiddlehead","The coiled young frond of a fern"],["Peat","Partly decayed plant matter, mostly moss, that builds up in bogs"]],
f:["Mosses and ferns reproduce by spores, not seeds.","Coal formed from ancient swamp forests of fern-like plants.","Moss frozen for about 1,500 years has been brought back to life."],
fun:"Peatlands store about twice as much carbon as all of the world’s forests."});

;
/* data/war-1.js */
// History articles, war part 1: the Western Front, the Treaty of Versailles and the rise of fascism.
ADDART({id:"westernfront",t:"The Western Front",c:"History",i:"🪖",w:"Western Front (World War I)",r:["wwi","francecountry","germanycountry","ukcountry"],
p:[["A line of trenches","The Western Front was a line of trenches stretching about 700 kilometers from the Belgian coast to Switzerland during World War I."],["Stalemate","For almost four years, neither side could break through, and millions of soldiers died for small gains."],["Industrial warfare","Machine guns, heavy artillery, poison gas, tanks and aircraft changed the nature of war."]],
l:"The Western Front was the main theater of fighting in World War I in western Europe, where the armies of Germany faced those of France, Britain, Belgium and, from 1917, the United States. After Germany’s rapid invasion of Belgium and France in 1914 was halted at the Battle of the Marne, both sides dug in. A line of trenches ran from the North Sea to the Swiss border, and for nearly four years huge battles such as Verdun and the Somme cost millions of lives for little ground. The front finally broke in 1918, leading to Germany’s defeat.",
s:[["The race to the sea","In August 1914 Germany invaded Belgium and northern France, hoping to defeat France quickly before turning on Russia. In September, French and British forces stopped the advance at the Marne, near Paris. Each side then tried to outflank the other northward in a race to the sea, until trenches stretched all the way to the coast."],["Life in the trenches","Soldiers lived in muddy, flooded ditches, protected by sandbags and barbed wire, with rats, lice and the constant danger of shellfire and snipers. Between the opposing trenches lay no man’s land, a stretch of mud and craters. Units rotated between the front line, support trenches and rest areas behind the lines."],["Verdun","In February 1916, Germany attacked the French fortress city of Verdun, hoping to wear down the French army. The battle lasted about ten months and caused around 700,000 casualties on both sides. The French rallying cry They shall not pass became famous, and Verdun became a symbol of endurance and slaughter in France."],["The Somme","On 1 July 1916, British and French forces attacked along the River Somme. On the first day alone, the British army suffered about 57,000 casualties, including about 19,000 dead, the worst day in its history. The battle lasted until November, with more than a million casualties in total, and the Allies advanced only about 10 kilometers."]],
k:[["Length","About 700 km, from the North Sea to Switzerland"],["Duration","1914–1918"],["Main opponents","Germany against France, Britain, Belgium and later the United States"],["Bloodiest battles","Verdun, the Somme, Passchendaele"],["Ended","Armistice, 11 November 1918"]],
d:[["August 1914","Germany invades Belgium and France"],["September 1914","Battle of the Marne halts the German advance"],["December 1914","Christmas Truce along parts of the front"],["February 1916","Battle of Verdun begins"],["July 1916","Battle of the Somme begins"],["11 November 1918","Armistice ends the fighting"]],
s2:[["The Christmas Truce","At Christmas 1914, along parts of the front, British and German soldiers stopped fighting on their own initiative. They sang carols, met in no man’s land, exchanged gifts and buried their dead; in a few places they even played football. Commanders on both sides disapproved, and such truces were not repeated on the same scale."],["New weapons","Machine guns and artillery made attacking across open ground deadly. In 1915 Germany used poison chlorine gas at Ypres, and soon both sides used gas, which caused terrible suffering. The British introduced tanks at the Somme in 1916, and aircraft were used first for spotting and later for fighting and bombing."],["Soldiers from around the world","Millions of soldiers from the British and French empires fought on the Western Front, including troops from India, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa, Senegal, Morocco and Vietnam. Chinese and Egyptian laborers dug trenches and built roads behind the lines."],["Passchendaele","In 1917, British and Commonwealth forces attacked near Ypres in Belgium in the Third Battle of Ypres, known as Passchendaele. Heavy rain turned the battlefield into a sea of mud in which men, horses and guns sank. After three months, the Allies had advanced only a few kilometers at the cost of about half a million casualties on both sides."],["The breakthrough","In spring 1918, Germany launched a series of huge offensives, gaining ground but exhausting its army. From August, Allied forces, now joined by large numbers of American troops and using tanks, aircraft and artillery together, pushed the Germans back in the Hundred Days Offensive. Germany asked for an armistice, which took effect at 11 a.m. on 11 November 1918."],["Remembering","The landscapes of the Western Front are scattered with war cemeteries and memorials, such as the Menin Gate at Ypres, where buglers have played the Last Post every evening since 1928, and the memorials at Thiepval and Verdun. Poppies, which bloomed in the churned fields, became a symbol of remembrance in many countries."]],
sp:["In Flanders Fields","In May 1915, after the death of a friend, the Canadian doctor John McCrae wrote the poem In Flanders Fields, describing poppies blowing between the crosses of soldiers’ graves. The poem became one of the most famous of the war and inspired the use of red poppies to remember those who died in war, a tradition still followed on Remembrance Day in many countries."],
g:[["Trench","A long, narrow ditch dug to protect soldiers"],["No man’s land","The ground between opposing trenches"],["Stalemate","A situation in which neither side can win"],["Armistice","An agreement to stop fighting"]],
f:["The Western Front stretched about 700 kilometers.","The first day of the Somme was the bloodiest day in British military history.","Soldiers held a Christmas Truce in 1914."],
fun:"Buglers have played the Last Post at the Menin Gate in Ypres almost every evening since 1928."});

ADDART({id:"versailles",t:"The Treaty of Versailles",c:"History",i:"📜",w:"Treaty of Versailles",r:["wwi","ww2","germanycountry","fascism","un"],
p:[["Ending World War I","The Treaty of Versailles, signed on 28 June 1919, formally ended the war between Germany and the Allied Powers."],["Harsh terms","Germany lost territory, its colonies and most of its military, accepted blame for the war and was ordered to pay reparations."],["A troubled peace","Many historians see the treaty’s terms and their effects as one of the causes of World War II."]],
l:"The Treaty of Versailles was the most important of the peace treaties that ended World War I. Negotiated at the Paris Peace Conference and signed in the Hall of Mirrors at the Palace of Versailles on 28 June 1919, five years to the day after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, it set out the terms imposed on Germany. It also created the League of Nations. The treaty was bitterly resented in Germany, and its legacy remains much debated by historians.",
s:[["The peacemakers","The conference was dominated by the leaders of the victorious powers: President Woodrow Wilson of the United States, Prime Minister David Lloyd George of Britain, Prime Minister Georges Clemenceau of France and Prime Minister Vittorio Orlando of Italy. Germany was not allowed to negotiate and was presented with the terms."],["Territory and colonies","Germany returned Alsace-Lorraine to France, lost land to the new Poland, including a corridor to the sea that split East Prussia from the rest of Germany, and gave smaller areas to Belgium, Denmark and Czechoslovakia. All its colonies in Africa and the Pacific were handed to other powers as mandates."],["Military limits","The German army was limited to 100,000 men, and Germany was banned from having an air force, submarines, tanks or conscription. The Rhineland, the German region bordering France, was to be demilitarized and occupied by Allied troops for 15 years."],["War guilt and reparations","Article 231, often called the war guilt clause, held Germany and its allies responsible for the losses caused by the war. Germany was required to pay reparations; in 1921 the amount was set at 132 billion gold marks, though in practice only a fraction was ever paid."]],
k:[["Signed","28 June 1919"],["Place","Hall of Mirrors, Palace of Versailles, France"],["Main leaders","Wilson, Lloyd George, Clemenceau, Orlando"],["Created","The League of Nations"],["Reparations set","132 billion gold marks (1921)"]],
d:[["January 1919","Paris Peace Conference opens"],["28 June 1919","Treaty of Versailles is signed"],["January 1920","Treaty takes effect; League of Nations is founded"],["1921","Reparations are set at 132 billion gold marks"],["1923","France and Belgium occupy the Ruhr when Germany falls behind on payments"],["1936","Germany remilitarizes the Rhineland in breach of the treaty"]],
s2:[["Wilson’s Fourteen Points","In January 1918, President Wilson set out Fourteen Points for peace, including open diplomacy, free trade, arms reduction, the right of peoples to determine their own government and an association of nations. Many Germans expected a peace based on them and felt betrayed by the final terms. Self-determination was applied mainly in Europe, not to colonies."],["The League of Nations","The treaty created the League of Nations, the first worldwide organization meant to keep peace through cooperation. However, the United States Senate refused to ratify the treaty, so the United States never joined. The League settled some disputes but failed to stop aggression in the 1930s and was replaced by the United Nations in 1945."],["Voices from the colonies","Many people from colonized lands went to Paris hoping to be heard. The young Vietnamese activist who later became known as Ho Chi Minh petitioned for rights for his people, and Japan’s proposal to include a statement of racial equality was rejected. China was angered when German rights in Shandong were given to Japan, sparking the May Fourth Movement."],["A Carthaginian peace?","The British economist John Maynard Keynes, who attended the conference, wrote in 1919 that the treaty was too harsh and would damage Europe’s economy. Others argued it was not harsh enough to keep Germany weak, or that it was enforced too inconsistently. Historians still debate whether the treaty itself, or the failure to uphold it, contributed more to the next war."],["Resentment in Germany","Many Germans called the treaty a Diktat, a dictated peace, and blamed the politicians of the new Weimar Republic who signed it. Nationalists spread the false stab-in-the-back myth that Germany had not been defeated on the battlefield but betrayed at home. Adolf Hitler exploited this anger as he rose to power."],["Other treaties","Separate treaties were signed with Germany’s allies: Saint-Germain with Austria, Trianon with Hungary, Neuilly with Bulgaria and Sèvres with the Ottoman Empire, later replaced by the Treaty of Lausanne with Türkiye in 1923. Together they redrew the map of Europe and the Middle East, creating new states such as Poland, Czechoslovakia and Yugoslavia."]],
sp:["The Hall of Mirrors","The treaty was signed in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, the same room where, in 1871, the German Empire had been proclaimed after France’s defeat in the Franco-Prussian War. The French deliberately chose the location to reverse that humiliation. In 1940, Hitler in turn insisted that France sign its surrender in the same railway carriage used for the 1918 armistice."],
g:[["Reparations","Payments for damage caused by war"],["Mandate","A territory placed under another country’s administration by the League of Nations"],["Self-determination","The right of a people to choose their own government"],["Demilitarized","Without military forces or fortifications"]],
f:["The treaty was signed five years to the day after Franz Ferdinand’s assassination.","The United States never joined the League of Nations.","Germany’s army was limited to 100,000 men."],
fun:"Germany made its final payment on bonds connected with World War I reparations in 2010."});

ADDART({id:"fascism",t:"The Rise of Fascism",c:"History",i:"⚠️",w:"Fascism",r:["ww2","holocaust","versailles","depression","democ"],
p:[["A dangerous ideology","Fascism is a far-right political movement that glorifies the nation, rejects democracy and demands obedience to a single leader."],["Italy and Germany","Benito Mussolini took power in Italy in 1922, and Adolf Hitler’s Nazi Party in Germany in 1933."],["Road to war","Fascist expansion and aggression in the 1930s led to World War II and the Holocaust."]],
l:"Fascism is an authoritarian, ultranationalist political movement that arose in Europe after World War I. Fascist movements glorified the nation and often a supposed racial community, rejected democracy, liberalism and communism, used violence against opponents and demanded total loyalty to a leader. Benito Mussolini founded the first fascist movement in Italy and came to power in 1922. In Germany, Adolf Hitler’s National Socialist, or Nazi, Party took power in 1933 and established a dictatorship whose aggression led to World War II and the genocide of the Holocaust.",
s:[["Why fascism grew","After World War I, many Europeans faced economic hardship, unemployment, fear of communist revolution and anger at the peace treaties. Some felt democratic governments were weak and divided. Fascist movements promised national greatness, order and strong leadership, and blamed scapegoats, such as minorities and political opponents, for the nation’s problems."],["Mussolini’s Italy","Benito Mussolini, a former socialist journalist, founded the Fascist movement in 1919. Its blackshirted squads attacked socialists and trade unionists. In October 1922, after the March on Rome, the king appointed Mussolini prime minister. By 1926 he had banned other parties and ruled as a dictator, calling himself Il Duce, the leader."],["Hitler and the Nazis","Adolf Hitler, an Austrian-born veteran of World War I, became leader of the Nazi Party, which combined extreme nationalism with violent antisemitism. After the Great Depression hit Germany, the party became the largest in parliament. Hitler was appointed chancellor in January 1933 and within months had banned other parties and taken dictatorial power."],["Life under fascist rule","Fascist states controlled the press, schools and youth groups, used propaganda and mass rallies, and imprisoned or killed opponents. Secret police, such as the Gestapo in Germany, spied on citizens. In Germany, Jews were stripped of their rights by the Nuremberg Laws of 1935 and faced growing persecution."]],
k:[["Began","Italy, 1919"],["Key leaders","Benito Mussolini (Italy), Adolf Hitler (Germany)"],["Mussolini in power","1922–1943"],["Nazis in power","1933–1945"],["Main features","Ultranationalism, dictatorship, suppression of opposition, militarism"]],
d:[["1919","Mussolini founds the Fascist movement"],["October 1922","March on Rome; Mussolini becomes prime minister"],["30 January 1933","Hitler becomes chancellor of Germany"],["1935","Nuremberg Laws strip German Jews of citizenship; Italy invades Ethiopia"],["1936–1939","Spanish Civil War; Franco’s forces win with fascist support"],["1938","Germany annexes Austria; Kristallnacht attacks on Jews"]],
s2:[["Aggression in the 1930s","Fascist states pursued expansion by force. Italy invaded Ethiopia in 1935, using poison gas. Germany rearmed, remilitarized the Rhineland in 1936, annexed Austria in March 1938 and took the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia after the Munich Agreement. Japan, ruled by militarists, invaded Manchuria in 1931 and China in 1937."],["Appeasement","Britain and France, eager to avoid another war, often tried to satisfy Hitler’s demands, a policy called appeasement. At Munich in September 1938, they agreed to let Germany take the Sudetenland. British Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain claimed it meant peace for our time, but in March 1939 Germany seized the rest of Czechoslovakia."],["The Spanish Civil War","From 1936 to 1939, Spain was torn by civil war between the elected Republican government and nationalist rebels led by General Francisco Franco. Germany and Italy sent troops and aircraft to help Franco, while the Soviet Union aided the Republic and volunteers came from many countries. In 1937, German and Italian planes bombed the town of Guernica, inspiring Picasso’s famous painting."],["Resistance","Many people opposed fascism at great risk, including socialists, communists, liberals, religious believers and students. In Germany, the White Rose student group distributed anti-Nazi leaflets and its members, including Sophie and Hans Scholl, were executed in 1943. During World War II, resistance movements fought occupation across Europe."],["The end of fascist rule","Fascist Italy and Nazi Germany were defeated in World War II. Mussolini was overthrown in 1943 and killed by Italian partisans in April 1945. Hitler took his own life in Berlin on 30 April 1945, days before Germany surrendered. At the Nuremberg trials, surviving Nazi leaders were tried for war crimes and crimes against humanity."],["Learning from history","Historians study how fascism rose to understand how democracies can fail: through economic crisis, political violence, propaganda, the targeting of minorities and the weakening of courts, the press and elections. Remembering the victims and the consequences of fascism is an important part of education in many countries."]],
sp:["The word fascism","The word fascism comes from the Italian fascio, meaning a bundle, and from the ancient Roman fasces, a bundle of rods tied around an axe carried before Roman officials as a symbol of authority. Mussolini adopted the fasces as the symbol of his movement to suggest strength through unity and to link his rule to the glory of ancient Rome."],
g:[["Fascism","A far-right movement that rejects democracy and demands obedience to a leader"],["Dictatorship","Rule by one person or group with unlimited power"],["Appeasement","Giving in to an aggressor’s demands to avoid conflict"],["Propaganda","Information used to spread a particular political message"]],
f:["Mussolini came to power in Italy in 1922.","Hitler became chancellor of Germany on 30 January 1933.","The word fascism comes from the Roman fasces."],
fun:"The word fascism comes from the fasces, a bundle of rods carried by officials in ancient Rome."});

;
