// History articles, war part 2: Stalingrad, D-Day and the Pacific War.
ADDART({id:"stalingrad",t:"The Battle of Stalingrad",c:"History",i:"❄️",w:"Battle of Stalingrad",r:["ww2","russiacountry","germanycountry","russrev"],
p:[["A turning point","The Battle of Stalingrad, from 1942 to 1943, was a turning point of World War II in Europe."],["Street by street","German and Soviet soldiers fought house by house through the ruined city on the Volga River."],["Huge losses","It was one of the bloodiest battles in history, with well over a million soldiers killed, wounded or captured."]],
l:"The Battle of Stalingrad was fought between Nazi Germany and its allies and the Soviet Union for control of the city of Stalingrad, now Volgograd, in southern Russia, from August 1942 to February 1943. The German Sixth Army fought its way into the city, but in November 1942 the Soviet Red Army surrounded it. After months of fighting in freezing conditions, the surviving German troops surrendered on 2 February 1943. The battle marked the end of Germany’s advance in the east and the beginning of its retreat.",
s:[["Why Stalingrad?","In June 1941, Germany invaded the Soviet Union. In 1942 Hitler sent his armies south toward the oil fields of the Caucasus. Stalingrad, an industrial city on the Volga River named after Soviet leader Joseph Stalin, protected their flank and controlled river traffic. Its name made it a symbol for both leaders."],["The attack","On 23 August 1942, German bombers struck Stalingrad in massive raids that killed many civilians and turned much of the city to rubble. The German Sixth Army, under General Friedrich Paulus, pushed into the city. Soviet forces under General Vasily Chuikov held on to a narrow strip along the river, ordered not to retreat."],["Rattenkrieg","Fighting raged through ruined factories, apartment blocks and sewers, often hand to hand. German soldiers called it Rattenkrieg, war of the rats. Snipers on both sides were feared. Soviet troops stayed close to German lines so that German aircraft and artillery could not attack without hitting their own men."],["Operation Uranus","On 19 November 1942, the Red Army launched Operation Uranus, attacking weaker Romanian forces on both sides of the city. Within days the Soviet pincers met, trapping about 300,000 German and allied soldiers. Hitler forbade a breakout, and an attempt to relieve them failed. Supplies dropped by air were far too few."]],
k:[["Dates","23 August 1942 – 2 February 1943"],["Place","Stalingrad (now Volgograd), Soviet Union"],["Commanders","Paulus (Germany); Chuikov, Zhukov (Soviet Union)"],["Casualties","Estimates range from about 1.2 to 2 million in total"],["Result","Soviet victory; German Sixth Army destroyed"]],
d:[["22 June 1941","Germany invades the Soviet Union"],["23 August 1942","Massive German air raids on Stalingrad"],["19 November 1942","Soviet counterattack, Operation Uranus, begins"],["23 November 1942","German Sixth Army is surrounded"],["31 January 1943","Paulus surrenders"],["2 February 1943","Last German troops in the city surrender"]],
s2:[["Surrender","In freezing temperatures, starving and short of ammunition, the trapped army was gradually crushed. On 31 January 1943, Paulus, just promoted to field marshal by Hitler, who expected him to fight to the death, surrendered. The last pocket gave up on 2 February. About 91,000 German prisoners were taken; only about 5,000 ever returned home."],["Civilians","Many civilians remained trapped in the city throughout the battle, living in cellars and ruins. Tens of thousands were killed. Children ran messages and gathered food in dangerous conditions. When the battle ended, the city was almost completely destroyed."],["Why it mattered","Stalingrad was Germany’s greatest defeat so far and broke the myth that its army could not be beaten. It boosted the morale of the Soviet Union and the Allies. In July 1943, Germany’s last major offensive in the east failed at Kursk, and from then on the Red Army advanced steadily toward Berlin."],["The Eastern Front","The war between Germany and the Soviet Union was the largest and deadliest theater of World War II. The Soviet Union lost an estimated 24 to 27 million people, soldiers and civilians, more than any other country. Most German military deaths in the war occurred on the Eastern Front."],["Pavlov’s House","A four-story apartment building in central Stalingrad was defended for about two months by a small group of Soviet soldiers, led for part of the time by Sergeant Yakov Pavlov. Surrounded by mines and barbed wire, they held off repeated attacks. Pavlov’s House became a famous symbol of Soviet resistance."],["Remembering Stalingrad","The city was renamed Volgograd in 1961. On the hill of Mamayev Kurgan, where some of the fiercest fighting took place, stands The Motherland Calls, a statue about 85 meters tall, among the tallest statues in the world. Streets and stations in cities such as Paris and London are named after Stalingrad."]],
sp:["The Motherland Calls","The statue The Motherland Calls, completed in 1967, shows a woman striding forward with a raised sword, calling her people to defend their country. At about 85 meters tall including the sword, it was the tallest statue in the world when built. Around it, a memorial complex holds the graves of thousands of soldiers who died in the battle."],
g:[["Encirclement","Surrounding an enemy force so it cannot escape or be supplied"],["Eastern Front","The war between Germany and its allies and the Soviet Union in World War II"],["Field marshal","The highest rank in many armies"],["Morale","The confidence and spirit of a group"]],
f:["Stalingrad is now called Volgograd.","About 91,000 German soldiers were captured at Stalingrad.","The Soviet Union lost more people in World War II than any other country."],
fun:"Only about 5,000 of the 91,000 German prisoners taken at Stalingrad ever returned home."});

ADDART({id:"dday",t:"D-Day and the Normandy Landings",c:"History",i:"⚓",w:"Normandy landings",r:["ww2","francecountry","ukcountry","usacountry","canadacountry"],
p:[["The largest seaborne invasion","On 6 June 1944, Allied forces landed on the beaches of Normandy in northern France."],["Five beaches","American, British and Canadian troops came ashore on beaches code-named Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword."],["Liberating Europe","The landings opened a second front in western Europe and led to the liberation of France."]],
l:"D-Day, 6 June 1944, was the day Allied forces launched the invasion of German-occupied France in World War II, known as Operation Overlord. In the largest seaborne invasion in history, about 156,000 soldiers from the United States, Britain, Canada and other Allied nations landed on five beaches in Normandy or dropped by parachute and glider behind them, supported by thousands of ships and aircraft. Despite heavy losses, especially at Omaha Beach, the Allies established a foothold, and Paris was liberated in August 1944.",
s:[["Planning","For years the Soviet Union had urged Britain and the United States to open a second front against Germany in the west. The invasion was planned in great secrecy under the overall command of US General Dwight D. Eisenhower. The Allies gathered millions of soldiers and huge amounts of equipment in southern England."],["Deception","To confuse the Germans about where the landings would come, the Allies ran Operation Fortitude, creating a fake army in southeastern England with inflatable tanks, dummy landing craft and false radio messages, pointing to the Pas-de-Calais, the shortest crossing. Double agents fed false information to German intelligence."],["The landings","After midnight, paratroopers and gliders landed inland to seize bridges and roads. At dawn, after bombing and naval bombardment, troops waded ashore. At Omaha Beach, American soldiers faced steep bluffs and strong German defenses and suffered heavy casualties. On the other beaches, troops advanced inland more quickly."],["The weather gamble","The landings depended on calm seas, a late-rising moon and low tide at dawn. Storms forced Eisenhower to postpone the invasion by a day. Meteorologists predicted a brief break in the weather on 6 June, and Eisenhower gave the order to go. Many German commanders, believing the weather too bad, were away from their posts."]],
k:[["Date","6 June 1944"],["Code name","Operation Overlord (landings: Operation Neptune)"],["Beaches","Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno, Sword"],["Troops landed on D-Day","About 156,000"],["Supreme commander","General Dwight D. Eisenhower"]],
d:[["November 1943","Allied leaders at Tehran agree on the invasion"],["5 June 1944","Eisenhower decides to launch the invasion"],["6 June 1944","D-Day landings in Normandy"],["June–August 1944","Battle of Normandy"],["25 August 1944","Paris is liberated"],["8 May 1945","Germany surrenders"]],
s2:[["Casualties","Allied casualties on D-Day itself are estimated at over 10,000, including more than 4,400 confirmed dead. German losses are uncertain. During the whole Battle of Normandy, which lasted until late August, hundreds of thousands of soldiers on both sides were killed, wounded or captured, and about 20,000 French civilians died."],["Many nations","Most of the soldiers were American, British and Canadian, but Free French, Polish, Norwegian, Dutch, Belgian, Czechoslovak, Australian, New Zealand and other forces also took part. The French Resistance sabotaged railways and telephone lines to slow German reinforcements."],["Mulberry harbors","To unload supplies before they could capture a port, the Allies towed huge prefabricated concrete harbors, called Mulberries, across the Channel and assembled them off the beaches. A pipeline under the sea, PLUTO, later carried fuel. Within weeks, hundreds of thousands of vehicles and soldiers had come ashore."],["The breakout","German forces fought hard in the hedgerow country of Normandy, where small fields bordered by thick hedges made attacking difficult. In late July, the Allies broke out, and in August they trapped many German troops near Falaise. Paris rose up and was liberated on 25 August 1944 by French and American forces."],["The Eastern and Western Fronts","D-Day was one part of the Allied strategy. At the same time, in June 1944, the Soviet Union launched Operation Bagration, a massive offensive that destroyed a large German army group in Belarus. Squeezed from both east and west, Germany surrendered less than a year later."],["Remembering D-Day","The Normandy beaches are lined with memorials, museums and cemeteries, including the American Cemetery at Colleville-sur-Mer above Omaha Beach and Commonwealth and German cemeteries nearby. Each year on 6 June, veterans, leaders and visitors gather for ceremonies; the 80th anniversary in 2024 was attended by some of the last surviving veterans."]],
sp:["What does the D mean?","The D in D-Day simply stands for day. Military planners used D-Day for the date of any planned operation, and H-Hour for its starting time, so that plans could be made before the exact date was fixed. Days before or after were written as D-1 or D+1. Because of the Normandy landings, D-Day is now almost always used to mean 6 June 1944."],
g:[["Invasion","An armed attack into another country’s territory"],["Second front","A new area of fighting that divides an enemy’s forces"],["Paratrooper","A soldier trained to drop by parachute"],["Liberation","Freeing a place from occupation"]],
f:["About 156,000 Allied troops landed on D-Day.","The five beaches were code-named Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword.","Paris was liberated on 25 August 1944."],
fun:"The Allies used inflatable tanks to trick the Germans about where the invasion would land."});

ADDART({id:"pacificwar",t:"The Pacific War",c:"History",i:"🌊",w:"Pacific War",r:["ww2","japocc","atomicbomb","japancountry","pacific","palaucountry"],
p:[["War in Asia and the Pacific","The Pacific War was the part of World War II fought in East Asia, Southeast Asia and the Pacific Ocean."],["Japan’s expansion","Japan invaded China in 1937 and, after attacking Pearl Harbor in 1941, conquered much of Southeast Asia."],["Island battles","The Allies fought back across the Pacific, island by island, until Japan surrendered in 1945."]],
l:"The Pacific War was fought between Japan and the Allies, chiefly the United States, China, Britain and its empire, Australia, New Zealand, the Netherlands, the Philippines and, at the very end, the Soviet Union. It grew out of Japan’s war in China, which began in full in 1937. After Japan attacked the US naval base at Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941, it swept across Southeast Asia and the western Pacific. The tide turned at Midway in 1942, and after years of brutal fighting and the atomic bombings, Japan surrendered in August 1945.",
s:[["War in China","Japan seized Manchuria in 1931 and launched a full-scale invasion of China in July 1937. Japanese forces captured Shanghai and the capital, Nanjing, where they massacred tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands of civilians and prisoners. Chinese Nationalist and Communist forces kept fighting, and the war tied down huge numbers of Japanese troops."],["Pearl Harbor and expansion","On 7 December 1941, Japanese aircraft attacked the US Pacific Fleet at Pearl Harbor in Hawaii, killing about 2,400 Americans and bringing the United States into the war. Within hours Japan attacked the Philippines, Malaya and Hong Kong. By mid-1942 it had conquered Malaya, Singapore, the Dutch East Indies, the Philippines, Burma and many Pacific islands."],["Midway","In June 1942, near Midway Atoll, US code breakers who had learned Japan’s plans helped the US Navy ambush a Japanese fleet. American aircraft sank four Japanese aircraft carriers, a blow from which the Japanese navy never fully recovered. Midway is often called the turning point of the Pacific War."],["Island hopping","From 1942 the Allies advanced on two main paths: under General Douglas MacArthur through New Guinea toward the Philippines, and under Admiral Chester Nimitz across the central Pacific. They captured some islands and bypassed others, leaving Japanese garrisons cut off. Battles such as Guadalcanal, Tarawa, Saipan, Iwo Jima and Okinawa were fought with great ferocity."]],
k:[["Period","1937 (in China) / 1941 (Pearl Harbor) – 1945"],["Main powers","Japan against the United States, China, Britain, Australia and others"],["Turning point","Battle of Midway, June 1942"],["Largest naval battle","Leyte Gulf, October 1944"],["Ended","Japan’s surrender, 2 September 1945"]],
d:[["7 July 1937","Marco Polo Bridge incident begins full-scale war in China"],["7 December 1941","Japan attacks Pearl Harbor"],["15 February 1942","Singapore falls to Japan"],["4–7 June 1942","Battle of Midway"],["23–26 October 1944","Battle of Leyte Gulf"],["2 September 1945","Japan formally surrenders"]],
s2:[["Occupation and suffering","Japanese occupation brought great suffering to Asia. Forced labor, massacres and famine killed millions of people. Hundreds of thousands of laborers from Asia and Allied prisoners of war were forced to build the Burma Railway, where many died. Many women and girls from Korea, China, the Philippines and elsewhere were forced into sexual slavery by the Japanese military."],["Resistance movements","Across occupied Asia, people resisted. In the Philippines, guerrilla fighters supported by the United States harassed Japanese forces. Communist and nationalist movements in China, Vietnam, Malaya and Burma fought the occupation, and some later fought for independence from European colonial rule."],["Leyte Gulf","In October 1944, as MacArthur returned to the Philippines at Leyte, the Japanese navy made a desperate attempt to destroy the landing fleet. The Battle of Leyte Gulf, among the largest naval battles in history, ended in a decisive Allied victory. Japan first used kamikaze suicide aircraft in this battle."],["Iwo Jima and Okinawa","In 1945, US forces captured Iwo Jima, where a famous photograph showed marines raising the flag on Mount Suribachi, and then Okinawa, the largest battle of the Pacific War, lasting nearly three months. More than 100,000 Okinawan civilians, perhaps a quarter of the population, died. The heavy losses shaped US planning for an invasion of Japan’s main islands."],["The end of the war","In August 1945, the United States dropped atomic bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki, and the Soviet Union declared war on Japan and invaded Manchuria. On 15 August, Emperor Hirohito announced Japan’s surrender by radio. The formal surrender was signed aboard the battleship USS Missouri in Tokyo Bay on 2 September 1945."],["After the war","Japan was occupied by Allied forces led by the United States until 1952 and adopted a democratic, pacifist constitution. Leaders were tried for war crimes in Tokyo. Korea was freed from Japanese rule but divided. Across Southeast Asia, the war weakened European colonial powers, speeding movements for independence in Indonesia, Vietnam, Burma, Malaya and elsewhere."]],
sp:["Code breakers","Before Midway, US Navy cryptanalysts in Hawaii had partly broken Japan’s naval code. They knew an attack was coming on a target called AF but not where. To check, they sent a fake message saying Midway was short of fresh water; soon Japanese messages reported that AF was short of water. Knowing the target, the US Navy prepared its ambush."],
g:[["Island hopping","Capturing key islands while bypassing others"],["Kamikaze","Japanese suicide attacks by aircraft"],["Guerrilla","A fighter in a small, irregular force"],["Cryptanalyst","A person who breaks codes"]],
f:["The Battle of Midway was a turning point of the Pacific War.","Japan signed its surrender aboard the USS Missouri.","The Battle of Okinawa lasted nearly three months."],
fun:"US code breakers tricked Japan into revealing that its target was Midway with a fake message about a water shortage."});

