/* data/war-3.js */
// History articles, war part 3: Hiroshima and Nagasaki, the Berlin Wall and the Cuban Missile Crisis.
ADDART({id:"atomicbomb",t:"Hiroshima and Nagasaki",c:"History",i:"🕊️",w:"Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki",r:["ww2","pacificwar","nuclear","japancountry","coldwar","marshallcountry"],
p:[["The only nuclear attacks","In August 1945, the United States dropped atomic bombs on the Japanese cities of Hiroshima and Nagasaki."],["Terrible destruction","The bombs killed an estimated 150,000 to 246,000 people by the end of 1945, most of them civilians."],["A new age","Japan surrendered days later, and the bombings began an age of nuclear weapons that still shapes the world."]],
l:"On 6 August 1945, the United States dropped an atomic bomb on Hiroshima, Japan, and on 9 August a second on Nagasaki. They remain the only uses of nuclear weapons in war. The explosions and the fires and radiation that followed killed tens of thousands instantly and many more in the following months; by the end of 1945, estimates of the dead range from about 150,000 to 246,000. Japan announced its surrender on 15 August. The bombings remain the subject of moral and historical debate.",
s:[["The Manhattan Project","Fearing that Nazi Germany might build an atomic bomb, the United States, with Britain and Canada, launched the secret Manhattan Project in 1942. Scientists led by J. Robert Oppenheimer at Los Alamos in New Mexico designed the weapons. On 16 July 1945, the first atomic bomb was tested in the desert at Trinity."],["Hiroshima","At 8:15 in the morning on 6 August 1945, the US bomber Enola Gay dropped a uranium bomb called Little Boy on Hiroshima, a city of about 350,000 people. It exploded with the force of about 15,000 metric tons of TNT, destroying most of the city in a fireball and blast. Tens of thousands died instantly."],["Nagasaki","On 9 August, a plutonium bomb called Fat Man was dropped on Nagasaki, a port city, after clouds covered the primary target, Kokura. Hills limited the damage to part of the city, but tens of thousands were killed. The same day, the Soviet Union invaded Japanese-held Manchuria."],["Surrender","On 15 August 1945, Emperor Hirohito announced Japan’s surrender in a radio broadcast, the first time most Japanese had heard his voice. He referred to a new and most cruel bomb. The formal surrender was signed on 2 September, ending World War II."]],
k:[["Hiroshima","6 August 1945, bomb named Little Boy (uranium)"],["Nagasaki","9 August 1945, bomb named Fat Man (plutonium)"],["Deaths by end of 1945","Estimates range from about 150,000 to 246,000"],["Japan’s surrender announced","15 August 1945"],["Survivors","Called hibakusha"]],
d:[["1939","Einstein signs a letter warning President Roosevelt about atomic bombs"],["1942","The Manhattan Project begins"],["16 July 1945","First atomic test at Trinity, New Mexico"],["6 August 1945","Hiroshima is bombed"],["9 August 1945","Nagasaki is bombed"],["2024","Nihon Hidankyo, an organization of survivors, wins the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s2:[["The survivors","Survivors, called hibakusha in Japanese, suffered burns and injuries, and many later developed radiation sickness, leukemia and other cancers. They also faced discrimination. For decades, hibakusha have shared their stories to warn against nuclear weapons. In 2024 their organization, Nihon Hidankyo, won the Nobel Peace Prize."],["Debate","Historians and the public continue to debate the bombings. Supporters argue that they ended the war quickly and avoided a costly invasion of Japan that could have killed even more people, both Allied and Japanese. Critics argue that Japan was already close to surrender, that the Soviet declaration of war was decisive, or that deliberately killing civilians on such a scale was morally wrong."],["Sadako and the cranes","Sadako Sasaki was two years old when the bomb fell on Hiroshima. At 12 she developed leukemia. Following a Japanese tradition that folding 1,000 paper cranes grants a wish, she folded cranes in the hospital before she died in 1955. Her story made the paper crane a worldwide symbol of peace."],["Rebuilding","Both cities were rebuilt and are now thriving. In Hiroshima, the ruined dome of an exhibition hall, close to where the bomb exploded, was preserved as the Atomic Bomb Dome, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The Peace Memorial Park and Museum attract visitors from around the world."],["The nuclear age","Within a few years, the Soviet Union, Britain, France and China had built nuclear weapons, and the far more powerful hydrogen bomb was developed. During the Cold War, the United States and the Soviet Union built tens of thousands of warheads. Treaties since the 1960s have limited testing and spread, but nine countries are believed to have nuclear weapons today."],["Peace Day","Every 6 August, Hiroshima holds a peace ceremony at 8:15 a.m., the moment the bomb exploded, and the mayor reads a peace declaration calling for the abolition of nuclear weapons. Nagasaki holds a similar ceremony on 9 August. Lanterns are floated on Hiroshima’s rivers in memory of the dead."]],
sp:["The Atomic Bomb Dome","The Hiroshima Prefectural Industrial Promotion Hall, built in 1915, stood about 160 meters from the point below the explosion. Because the blast came from almost directly above, part of its walls and the steel frame of its dome survived while everything around was flattened. Preserved as it was, the Genbaku Dome became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1996, a stark reminder of the bombing."],
g:[["Atomic bomb","A weapon whose explosive power comes from splitting atoms"],["Hibakusha","Survivors of the atomic bombings"],["Radiation sickness","Illness caused by a high dose of radiation"],["Manhattan Project","The secret wartime program that built the first atomic bombs"]],
f:["Hiroshima and Nagasaki are the only cities attacked with nuclear weapons.","Survivors of the bombings are called hibakusha.","The paper crane is a symbol of peace because of Sadako Sasaki."],
fun:"The Atomic Bomb Dome in Hiroshima survived because the explosion happened almost directly above it."});

ADDART({id:"berlinwall",t:"The Berlin Wall",c:"History",i:"🧱",w:"Berlin Wall",r:["coldwar","germanycountry","ussrfall","eu"],
p:[["A divided city","From 1961 to 1989, the Berlin Wall divided the German city of Berlin into East and West."],["Symbol of the Cold War","The wall became the most famous symbol of the division between communist and democratic Europe."],["Its fall","On 9 November 1989, the wall was opened, and Germany was reunited less than a year later."]],
l:"The Berlin Wall was a guarded concrete barrier that divided Berlin from 1961 to 1989. Built by communist East Germany, officially the German Democratic Republic, it encircled West Berlin, cutting it off from East Berlin and the surrounding East German countryside. East Germany called it a protection against fascism, but its real purpose was to stop people fleeing to the West. At least 140 people died at the wall. Its sudden opening on 9 November 1989 became a symbol of the end of the Cold War.",
s:[["A divided Germany","After World War II, Germany and its capital, Berlin, were divided into four zones controlled by the United States, Britain, France and the Soviet Union. In 1949 the Western zones became West Germany and the Soviet zone became communist East Germany. Berlin, deep inside East Germany, was also split, with West Berlin linked to the West."],["Why it was built","In the 1950s, millions of East Germans, many of them young and skilled, fled to the West, often simply by crossing in Berlin. By 1961 about 3 million had left. To stop this, on the night of 12–13 August 1961, East German soldiers and workers sealed the border with barbed wire, soon replaced by concrete walls."],["The wall","Over time, the barrier became a system of two walls, with a death strip between them, watchtowers, floodlights, dogs, trenches and guards ordered to shoot people trying to escape. It ran about 155 kilometers around West Berlin, including about 43 kilometers through the city itself."],["Escapes","About 5,000 people escaped over, under or through the wall, using tunnels, homemade balloons, cars with hidden compartments and even a zip line. At least 140 people were killed trying to cross or in incidents at the wall. Peter Fechter, an 18-year-old shot in 1962 and left to bleed to death in view of Western onlookers, became a symbol of its brutality."]],
k:[["Built","13 August 1961"],["Opened","9 November 1989"],["Length around West Berlin","About 155 km"],["Deaths at the wall","At least 140"],["German reunification","3 October 1990"]],
d:[["1949","Germany is divided into West and East Germany"],["13 August 1961","Construction of the Berlin Wall begins"],["26 June 1963","Kennedy declares Ich bin ein Berliner in West Berlin"],["12 June 1987","Reagan urges Gorbachev to tear down this wall"],["9 November 1989","The wall is opened"],["3 October 1990","Germany is reunified"]],
s2:[["Famous speeches","In 1963 US President John F. Kennedy visited West Berlin and told a huge crowd Ich bin ein Berliner, I am a Berliner, expressing solidarity with the city. In 1987 President Ronald Reagan, speaking at the Brandenburg Gate, called on Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev: Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall!"],["Checkpoint Charlie","Checkpoint Charlie was the best-known crossing point between East and West Berlin, used by Allied military personnel and foreigners. In October 1961, American and Soviet tanks faced each other there for 16 hours in a tense standoff. A replica guardhouse now stands at the site, and a museum tells the story of escapes."],["Revolutions of 1989","In 1989, communist governments across Eastern Europe faced rising protests. Hungary opened its border with Austria, and thousands of East Germans fled through it. Huge peaceful demonstrations, such as those in Leipzig, demanded freedom. Soviet leader Gorbachev made clear he would not send troops to prop up East Germany’s government."],["The night the wall opened","On 9 November 1989, an East German official, Günter Schabowski, announced at a press conference that citizens could travel freely and, when asked when, said it took effect immediately. Thousands rushed to the border crossings, and overwhelmed guards opened the gates. Berliners climbed on the wall, celebrated and began chipping pieces off it."],["Reunification","After East Germany’s first free elections in March 1990, the two Germanys negotiated reunification with the four former occupying powers. On 3 October 1990, East Germany joined West Germany, forming one country with Berlin as its capital. The day is celebrated as German Unity Day."],["Remembering the wall","Today only a few stretches of the wall remain. The Berlin Wall Memorial on Bernauer Strasse preserves a section of the death strip. The East Side Gallery, a 1.3-kilometer stretch covered in murals by artists from around the world, is one of Berlin’s most visited sites. Pieces of the wall stand in cities worldwide."]],
sp:["A mistaken announcement","The opening of the Berlin Wall happened partly by accident. Günter Schabowski had been handed a note about new, looser travel rules shortly before a live press conference and had not been told they were meant to start the next day. When a journalist asked when they took effect, he looked at his papers and said, as far as I know, immediately. Within hours, crowds had forced the border open."],
g:[["Cold War","The rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union and their allies, 1947–1991"],["Death strip","The guarded zone between the two walls"],["Checkpoint","A place where travelers are stopped and inspected"],["Reunification","Joining together again after being divided"]],
f:["The Berlin Wall stood from 1961 to 1989.","About 5,000 people escaped across it.","Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990."],
fun:"The Berlin Wall was opened partly because an official misread when new travel rules would begin."});

ADDART({id:"cubancrisis",t:"The Cuban Missile Crisis",c:"History",i:"🚀",w:"Cuban Missile Crisis",r:["coldwar","usacountry","russiacountry","nuclear","atomicbomb"],
p:[["Thirteen days","In October 1962, the United States and the Soviet Union came closer to nuclear war than at any other time."],["Missiles in Cuba","The crisis began when the United States discovered Soviet nuclear missiles being installed in Cuba, just 145 kilometers from Florida."],["A peaceful end","After tense negotiations, the Soviet Union agreed to remove the missiles, and the United States secretly agreed to remove its missiles from Türkiye."]],
l:"The Cuban Missile Crisis was a 13-day confrontation in October 1962 between the United States and the Soviet Union over Soviet nuclear missiles placed in Cuba. President John F. Kennedy announced a naval quarantine of the island, and for days the world feared a nuclear war. The crisis ended when Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev agreed to withdraw the missiles in exchange for a US pledge not to invade Cuba and a secret agreement to remove US missiles from Türkiye. It led both sides to take steps to reduce the risk of nuclear war.",
s:[["Background","In 1959 Fidel Castro’s revolution took power in Cuba and soon allied with the Soviet Union. In April 1961, a US-backed invasion by Cuban exiles at the Bay of Pigs failed. Meanwhile, the United States had placed nuclear missiles in Italy and Türkiye, within range of the Soviet Union. Khrushchev decided to place Soviet missiles secretly in Cuba."],["Discovery","On 14 October 1962, a US U-2 spy plane photographed launch sites for Soviet medium-range missiles under construction in Cuba. On 16 October, President Kennedy was shown the photographs. He gathered his closest advisers, a group called ExComm, to decide how to respond."],["The quarantine","Some advisers urged air strikes or an invasion. Kennedy chose a naval quarantine, a blockade to stop more weapons reaching Cuba, while demanding the removal of the missiles. On 22 October, he announced the discovery and the quarantine in a televised address. US forces were put on high alert."],["The most dangerous days","On 24 October, Soviet ships approaching the quarantine line stopped or turned back. On 27 October, known as Black Saturday, a US U-2 was shot down over Cuba, killing its pilot, and US warships dropped practice depth charges to force a Soviet submarine to surface, unaware that it carried a nuclear torpedo. Both sides came very close to war."]],
k:[["Dates","16–28 October 1962"],["Leaders","John F. Kennedy (USA), Nikita Khrushchev (USSR), Fidel Castro (Cuba)"],["Distance from Cuba to Florida","About 145 km"],["Outcome","Soviet missiles removed; US pledged not to invade Cuba"],["Secret deal","US missiles removed from Türkiye"]],
d:[["April 1961","Bay of Pigs invasion fails"],["14 October 1962","U-2 photographs missile sites in Cuba"],["22 October 1962","Kennedy announces the quarantine"],["27 October 1962","Black Saturday: a U-2 is shot down"],["28 October 1962","Khrushchev agrees to remove the missiles"],["1963","Moscow–Washington hotline is set up; Limited Test Ban Treaty signed"]],
s2:[["The deal","On 26 and 27 October, Khrushchev sent letters offering to remove the missiles if the United States promised not to invade Cuba, and later also asked for the removal of US missiles from Türkiye. Kennedy publicly accepted the first offer, while his brother Robert Kennedy privately assured the Soviet ambassador that the missiles in Türkiye would be removed later. On 28 October, Khrushchev announced the withdrawal."],["Vasily Arkhipov","On 27 October, the Soviet submarine B-59, harassed by US ships and out of contact with Moscow, had a nuclear torpedo on board. Its captain reportedly wanted to launch it, but the decision needed the agreement of three officers. One of them, Vasily Arkhipov, refused. Many historians believe his decision may have prevented a nuclear war."],["Cuba’s view","Cuba’s leaders, who had been preparing for a US invasion, were angry that the Soviet Union negotiated the withdrawal without consulting them. Fidel Castro had even urged Khrushchev to use nuclear weapons if the United States invaded. The crisis left Cuba under a long US trade embargo that continues in part today."],["Lessons learned","Shaken by how close they had come, the two superpowers set up a direct communication link, the Moscow–Washington hotline, in 1963, so that leaders could talk quickly in a crisis. That year they also signed the Limited Test Ban Treaty, banning nuclear tests in the atmosphere, in space and underwater."],["Close calls","The crisis showed how mistakes and misunderstandings could lead to disaster. Historians have since learned of several close calls during the crisis, including a U-2 that strayed into Soviet airspace and the submarine incident. These lessons have shaped later efforts to manage nuclear risks."],["Thirteen Days","The crisis has been the subject of many books and films. Robert Kennedy’s memoir Thirteen Days, published in 1969, described the discussions inside the White House. Recordings of ExComm meetings, released decades later, have given historians a detailed view of how decisions were made."]],
sp:["A hotline for peace","During the crisis, messages between Kennedy and Khrushchev took hours to deliver, sometimes passing through embassies and telegraph offices. To prevent such delays, the two countries set up a direct link in 1963. Contrary to popular belief, it was not a red telephone at first but a teletype machine sending written messages, which were less likely to be misunderstood."],
g:[["Quarantine","Here, a naval blockade to stop weapons reaching Cuba"],["Superpower","An extremely powerful country, such as the United States or Soviet Union in the Cold War"],["Embargo","An official ban on trade with a country"],["Hotline","A direct communication link for emergencies"]],
f:["The Cuban Missile Crisis lasted 13 days in October 1962.","A Soviet officer, Vasily Arkhipov, refused to approve launching a nuclear torpedo.","The Moscow–Washington hotline was set up in 1963."],
fun:"The famous Moscow–Washington hotline was first a teletype machine, not a red telephone."});

