// The Human Body articles, part 2: cancer, diabetes, the microbiome and ageing.
ADDART({id:"cancer",t:"Cancer",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🎗️",w:"Cancer",r:["cell","dna","immune","radioact","skinmarks"],
p:[["Cells out of control","Cancer is a group of diseases in which body cells grow and divide uncontrollably and can spread to other parts of the body."],["A leading cause of death","Cancer is one of the leading causes of death worldwide, but many cancers can be prevented or treated."],["Better treatments","Surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, immunotherapy and targeted drugs help millions of people survive cancer."]],
l:"Cancer is the name for more than 100 diseases in which abnormal cells divide without control and can invade nearby tissues and spread to other parts of the body. It begins when changes, called mutations, build up in a cell’s DNA. Cancer can affect almost any part of the body; common types include lung, breast, bowel, prostate and skin cancer. Around 20 million new cases are diagnosed each year worldwide. Avoiding tobacco, staying active, vaccination and screening can prevent many cancers, and treatments have greatly improved survival.",
s:[["How cancer starts","Normally, cells grow, divide and die in an orderly way, controlled by genes. Mutations in these genes, caused by aging, inherited faults or damage from things such as tobacco smoke, ultraviolet light or some viruses, can let a cell divide uncontrollably. Over time, it may form a lump called a tumor."],["Benign and malignant","Not all tumors are cancer. Benign tumors grow slowly and do not spread. Malignant tumors are cancerous: they can invade nearby tissue and break away, traveling through the blood or lymph system to form new tumors elsewhere, a process called metastasis."],["Risk factors","Tobacco is the single largest preventable cause of cancer. Other risks include alcohol, obesity, lack of exercise, too much sun and certain infections, such as human papillomavirus, HPV, and hepatitis B. The risk of most cancers rises with age. Some people inherit genes, such as BRCA1 and BRCA2, that raise their risk."],["Detection","Screening tests can find some cancers early, when they are easier to treat, such as mammograms for breast cancer, Pap or HPV tests for cervical cancer and tests for bowel cancer. Warning signs to check with a doctor include a new lump, unexplained weight loss, a changing mole or a persistent cough."]],
k:[["Types","More than 100"],["New cases worldwide","About 20 million a year (2022)"],["Largest preventable cause","Tobacco"],["Main treatments","Surgery, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, immunotherapy, targeted therapy"],["Preventable by vaccine","Cervical cancer (HPV) and some liver cancers (hepatitis B)"]],
d:[["c. 1600 BCE","An Egyptian papyrus describes breast tumors"],["c. 400 BCE","Hippocrates uses the word karkinos, crab"],["1896","X-rays are first used to treat cancer"],["1940s","The first chemotherapy drugs are developed"],["2006","First HPV vaccine is approved"],["2018","Nobel Prize for cancer immunotherapy"]],
s2:[["Treatments","Surgery removes tumors. Radiotherapy uses high-energy rays to kill cancer cells. Chemotherapy uses drugs that attack fast-dividing cells. Targeted therapies block specific molecules that cancer cells need to grow, and hormone therapies treat cancers such as some breast and prostate cancers. Patients often receive a combination."],["Immunotherapy","Immunotherapy helps the body’s own immune system fight cancer. Checkpoint inhibitor drugs release brakes that cancer uses to hide from immune cells; James Allison and Tasuku Honjo won the 2018 Nobel Prize for this work. CAR-T therapy reprograms a patient’s immune cells to attack certain blood cancers."],["Preventing cancer","Experts estimate that 30 to 50 percent of cancers could be prevented by avoiding tobacco, limiting alcohol, keeping a healthy weight, eating plenty of fruit and vegetables, being active, protecting skin from the sun and getting vaccinated against HPV and hepatitis B."],["Childhood cancer","Cancer in children is rare, and the most common types, such as leukemia and brain tumors, differ from adult cancers. In wealthy countries more than 80 percent of children with cancer survive, but in many low-income countries survival is much lower. The World Health Organization aims to raise global survival to at least 60 percent by 2030."],["Living with cancer","Thanks to better treatments and early detection, many more people survive cancer than in the past, and some cancers are now managed as long-term conditions. Support from family, friends, doctors, nurses and support groups helps patients and survivors cope with the physical and emotional effects."],["Why the name cancer?","The ancient Greek physician Hippocrates used the words karkinos and karkinoma, meaning crab, to describe tumors, perhaps because their swollen veins reminded him of a crab’s legs. The Roman doctor Celsus translated it into Latin as cancer. The study of cancer, oncology, comes from the Greek onkos, meaning mass or swelling."]],
sp:["Henrietta Lacks","In 1951, cells were taken from a tumor of Henrietta Lacks, a young African American woman with cervical cancer, without her knowledge. Her cells, called HeLa cells, were the first human cells to keep growing in a laboratory and have been used in research on polio vaccines, cancer, HIV and more. Her story has raised important questions about consent and fairness in medical research."],
g:[["Tumor","An abnormal lump of cells"],["Malignant","Cancerous; able to invade and spread"],["Metastasis","The spread of cancer to other parts of the body"],["Chemotherapy","Treatment with drugs that kill cancer cells"]],
f:["Tobacco is the largest preventable cause of cancer.","Vaccines can prevent cervical cancer.","Cancer immunotherapy won the 2018 Nobel Prize."],
fun:"The word cancer comes from the Greek and Latin words for crab."});

ADDART({id:"diabetes",t:"Diabetes",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩸",w:"Diabetes",r:["hormone","nutri","exercise","blood","stemcells","pancreas","ultraprocessed"],
p:[["Too much sugar in the blood","Diabetes is a long-term condition in which the body cannot control the level of sugar, or glucose, in the blood."],["Insulin","It happens when the body does not make enough insulin, a hormone, or cannot use it properly."],["Very common","About 590 million adults worldwide live with diabetes, according to the International Diabetes Federation."]],
l:"Diabetes is a chronic disease in which blood sugar levels are too high because the body does not produce enough insulin or does not respond to it properly. Insulin, a hormone made by the pancreas, lets cells take in glucose from the blood for energy. There are two main types: type 1, in which the immune system destroys the cells that make insulin, and type 2, much more common, linked to genetics, excess weight and lack of exercise. Without good control, diabetes can damage the heart, kidneys, eyes and nerves.",
s:[["How blood sugar works","When we eat, carbohydrates are broken down into glucose, which enters the blood. The pancreas releases insulin, which acts like a key, letting glucose into cells for energy and storing extra in the liver and muscles. Between meals, another hormone, glucagon, releases stored glucose so blood sugar stays steady."],["Type 1 diabetes","In type 1 diabetes, the immune system mistakenly destroys the insulin-making beta cells in the pancreas. It often starts in childhood or young adulthood. People with type 1 need insulin every day, by injection or pump, and must monitor their blood sugar carefully. Its exact cause is not known, and it cannot currently be prevented."],["Type 2 diabetes","In type 2 diabetes, the body’s cells become resistant to insulin, and over time the pancreas cannot keep up. It is linked to genes, age, excess body fat, especially around the waist, and lack of physical activity. It is becoming more common worldwide, including among younger people, and can often be prevented or delayed by healthy living."],["Symptoms","Common signs include being very thirsty, urinating often, feeling tired, losing weight without trying, blurred vision and slow-healing cuts. Type 2 may develop slowly with few symptoms, so many people do not know they have it. A simple blood test can diagnose diabetes."]],
k:[["Adults with diabetes","About 590 million worldwide (IDF, 2024)"],["Main types","Type 1 and type 2"],["Key hormone","Insulin, made by the pancreas"],["Insulin discovered","1921, in Toronto"],["World Diabetes Day","14 November"]],
d:[["c. 1550 BCE","An Egyptian papyrus describes frequent urination"],["1889","Scientists show the pancreas is linked to diabetes"],["1921","Banting and Best discover insulin"],["1922","First patient is treated with insulin"],["1982","First human insulin made by genetic engineering"],["2006","UN recognizes World Diabetes Day"]],
s2:[["The discovery of insulin","In 1921, at the University of Toronto, Frederick Banting and Charles Best, working in John Macleod’s laboratory, extracted insulin from the pancreas of dogs. With chemist James Collip’s help in purifying it, in January 1922 they treated 14-year-old Leonard Thompson, who was dying of diabetes, and he recovered. Banting and Macleod won the 1923 Nobel Prize."],["Managing diabetes","People with diabetes manage it through healthy eating, physical activity, medicines and regular checks. Many with type 2 take tablets such as metformin, and some need insulin. Glucose meters and continuous glucose monitors, sensors worn on the skin, help people track their levels. Insulin pumps can deliver insulin automatically."],["Complications","Over many years, high blood sugar can damage blood vessels and nerves, increasing the risk of heart attacks, strokes, kidney failure, blindness and foot problems that may lead to amputation. Good control of blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol greatly lowers these risks."],["A global challenge","The number of adults with diabetes has risen sharply since 1990, especially in low- and middle-income countries in Asia, the Middle East, Africa and the Pacific. Many people cannot afford insulin or medicines. The World Health Organization has made access to insulin and diabetes care a priority."],["Preventing type 2","Studies have shown that people at high risk can greatly reduce their chances of developing type 2 diabetes by losing a modest amount of weight, eating more vegetables, whole grains and fiber, cutting down on sugary drinks and being active for at least 150 minutes a week."],["Hypoglycemia","If blood sugar falls too low, called hypoglycemia, a person may feel shaky, sweaty, confused or dizzy. It can happen to people taking insulin or some medicines. It is treated quickly with sugar, such as fruit juice or glucose tablets. Friends and teachers of people with diabetes can learn the signs."]],
sp:["The miracle of insulin","Before 1922, a diagnosis of type 1 diabetes was almost always a death sentence, especially for children, who were kept on starvation diets that only delayed death. When insulin became available, children who had been near death recovered within days. Banting and his colleagues sold the patent for insulin to the University of Toronto for just one dollar each, so that it could be widely available."],
g:[["Glucose","A sugar that is the body’s main source of energy"],["Insulin","A hormone that lets cells take in glucose"],["Pancreas","An organ that makes insulin and digestive juices"],["Chronic","Lasting a long time"]],
f:["Insulin was discovered in Toronto in 1921.","Type 2 diabetes is the most common type.","World Diabetes Day is 14 November, Frederick Banting’s birthday."],
fun:"The discoverers of insulin sold its patent for one dollar each."});

ADDART({id:"microbiome",t:"The Microbiome",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦠",w:"Human microbiome",r:["microbe","digest","immune","antibiotics","appendix"],
p:[["Trillions of tiny partners","The human microbiome is the community of trillions of bacteria, fungi and viruses that live on and inside our bodies."],["Mostly in the gut","Most live in the large intestine, where they help digest food and make vitamins."],["Important for health","Scientists are discovering that the microbiome affects digestion, immunity and possibly even mood."]],
l:"The human microbiome is the vast community of microorganisms, mostly bacteria but also fungi, viruses and other microbes, that live on the skin, in the mouth, nose and gut, and elsewhere in the body. The number of bacterial cells is about the same as the number of human cells. Far from being harmful, most of these microbes are helpful: they digest fiber, produce vitamins, train the immune system and protect against disease-causing germs. Research on the microbiome has grown rapidly since the 2000s.",
s:[["How many microbes?","An adult body hosts roughly 38 trillion bacterial cells, similar to its roughly 30 trillion human cells. Together the microbes weigh only about 200 grams, because bacterial cells are tiny. They carry far more genes than our own cells, adding many abilities our bodies lack."],["The gut microbiome","Most microbes live in the large intestine, where hundreds of species break down fiber we cannot digest, producing short-chain fatty acids that nourish the gut lining. They also make vitamins such as vitamin K and some B vitamins. Each person’s mix of gut microbes is unique, like a fingerprint."],["Where we get them","Babies get their first microbes from their mothers during birth and breastfeeding and from their surroundings. The microbiome changes quickly in early childhood and becomes more stable by about age three. Diet, medicines, pets, siblings and where we live all shape it."],["Protecting us","Helpful microbes take up space and nutrients, making it harder for harmful germs to settle. They help train the immune system to tell friend from foe. Skin microbes help protect against infections, and microbes in the mouth and vagina help keep harmful organisms in check."]],
k:[["Bacterial cells in the body","About 38 trillion"],["Human cells","About 30 trillion"],["Total weight of microbes","About 200 grams"],["Main location","The large intestine"],["Big research project","Human Microbiome Project (2007–2016)"]],
d:[["1683","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek sees bacteria from his own mouth"],["1880s","Scientists begin identifying gut bacteria"],["2007","US launches the Human Microbiome Project"],["2012","First map of the healthy human microbiome is published"],["2013","Fecal transplants are shown to cure severe gut infections"],["2016","New estimate shows bacteria and human cells are about equal in number"]],
s2:[["Fiber feeds microbes","Gut microbes thrive on fiber from vegetables, fruits, beans and whole grains. Diets rich in a variety of plant foods tend to support a more diverse microbiome, which is linked with better health. Fermented foods, such as yogurt, kimchi and kefir, contain live microbes."],["Antibiotics and the microbiome","Antibiotics kill harmful bacteria but can also wipe out helpful ones, upsetting the balance of the microbiome. This can cause diarrhea and sometimes allow dangerous bacteria, such as Clostridioides difficile, to take over. The microbiome usually recovers, but not always completely."],["Fecal transplants","For people with severe, repeated C. difficile infections, doctors can transplant stool from a healthy donor, restoring a healthy microbiome. In trials since 2013, this has cured most patients. Scientists are developing capsules of selected bacteria as cleaner alternatives."],["Microbes and the brain","Researchers have found that gut microbes communicate with the brain through nerves, hormones and the immune system, sometimes called the gut-brain axis. Studies in mice suggest microbes can affect behavior and stress. Whether and how this matters for people’s mood and mental health is an active area of research."],["Changing microbiomes","People in industrialized countries tend to have less diverse gut microbiomes than people living traditional lifestyles, such as the Hadza hunter-gatherers of Tanzania. Scientists think processed diets, antibiotics and very clean environments may contribute, and that this may be linked to the rise of allergies and some autoimmune diseases."],["Probiotics","Probiotics are live microbes sold in foods and supplements that are meant to benefit health. Some probiotics help with specific problems, such as certain kinds of diarrhea, but for many other claims the evidence is weak. Scientists are still learning which microbes help which people."]],
sp:["Leeuwenhoek’s animalcules","In 1683 the Dutch cloth merchant and lens maker Antonie van Leeuwenhoek scraped plaque from his own teeth, mixed it with water and looked at it through one of his homemade microscopes. He saw tiny moving creatures he called animalcules: bacteria. He was the first person to describe the microbes that live in the human body."],
g:[["Microbiome","The community of microbes living in a particular place, such as the body"],["Probiotic","Live microbes taken to benefit health"],["Fiber","Plant material that humans cannot digest but gut microbes can"],["Gut-brain axis","The links between the digestive system and the brain"]],
f:["The body contains roughly as many bacterial cells as human cells.","Most of the body’s microbes live in the large intestine.","Each person’s gut microbiome is unique."],
fun:"Your body carries about 200 grams of microbes, roughly the weight of a mango."});

ADDART({id:"aging",t:"Ageing and Lifespan",c:"Health and the Body",i:"⏳",w:"Ageing",r:["cell","dna","exercise","brain","retirement"],
p:[["Growing older","Ageing is the gradual change in the body and mind that happens over a lifetime."],["Living longer","Average life expectancy worldwide has more than doubled since 1900, to about 73 years."],["Healthy ageing","Staying active, eating well, staying social and avoiding tobacco help people stay healthy as they age."]],
l:"Ageing is the process of growing older, in which the body’s cells, tissues and organs gradually change and become less able to repair themselves. It affects everyone differently, depending on genes, lifestyle and environment. Thanks to better nutrition, clean water, vaccines and medicine, people today live far longer than in the past: global life expectancy has risen from about 32 years in 1900 to around 73 today. Scientists study ageing to help people live not only longer but healthier lives.",
s:[["What happens with age","With age, skin loses elasticity and wrinkles, hair turns gray, muscles and bones tend to weaken, and eyesight and hearing may decline. The heart, lungs and kidneys work less efficiently. These changes happen gradually and at different rates in different people."],["Why do we age?","Scientists think ageing results from the slow build-up of damage to cells and molecules over time. DNA collects mutations, the protective ends of chromosomes, called telomeres, shorten with each cell division, worn-out cells stop dividing, and cell repair systems become less effective."],["Life expectancy","Life expectancy is the average number of years a newborn can expect to live. It was low in the past mainly because many children died young. Today it is highest in places such as Japan, Hong Kong, Switzerland and South Korea, over 83 years, and lowest in some countries affected by poverty and conflict."],["The brain and ageing","Some memory and thinking skills, such as recalling names, may slow with age, while knowledge and vocabulary often keep growing. Diseases such as Alzheimer’s are not a normal part of ageing. Staying mentally, physically and socially active is linked to better brain health."]],
k:[["Global life expectancy","About 73 years (2023)"],["Life expectancy in 1900","About 32 years"],["Longest verified life","Jeanne Calment, 122 years (1875–1997)"],["Highest life expectancy","Over 83 years in Japan, Hong Kong, Switzerland and others"],["Key cell feature","Shortening telomeres"]],
d:[["1900","Global life expectancy is about 32 years"],["1961","Leonard Hayflick shows that human cells divide only a limited number of times"],["1997","Jeanne Calment dies at 122"],["2009","Nobel Prize for discoveries about telomeres"],["2021–2030","UN Decade of Healthy Ageing"]],
s2:[["Blue zones","Researchers have studied places where many people live to 100 or more, sometimes called blue zones, such as Okinawa in Japan, Sardinia in Italy and the Nicoya Peninsula in Costa Rica. Common features include plant-rich diets, daily physical activity, strong family and community ties and a sense of purpose, though the idea and some data have been debated."],["The Hayflick limit","In 1961 Leonard Hayflick discovered that normal human cells grown in a lab divide only about 40 to 60 times before stopping. This limit is linked to telomeres, which shorten each time a cell divides. Elizabeth Blackburn, Carol Greider and Jack Szostak won the 2009 Nobel Prize for discovering how telomeres and the enzyme telomerase protect chromosomes."],["The oldest people","The longest confirmed human lifespan belongs to Jeanne Calment of France, who lived to 122 years and 164 days, dying in 1997. People who live to 110 or more are called supercentenarians. The number of people over 100 is growing fast worldwide."],["An ageing world","Because people live longer and have fewer children, the world’s population is getting older. By 2030 one in six people will be 60 or over. Countries such as Japan, Italy and South Korea already have large older populations, which brings challenges for health care and pensions, and opportunities as older people continue to work and volunteer."],["Healthy habits","Regular exercise, a balanced diet, enough sleep, not smoking, limiting alcohol, keeping a healthy weight, staying connected with others and continuing to learn are all linked to healthier ageing. It is never too late to benefit: people who start exercising in later life still gain strength and balance."],["Animals and ageing","Lifespans vary enormously in nature. Some mayflies live as adults for only a day, while Greenland sharks may live for over 250 years, and a tiny jellyfish, Turritopsis dohrnii, can revert to an earlier life stage, making it potentially immortal. Scientists study long-lived animals to learn about ageing."]],
sp:["Jeanne Calment","Jeanne Calment was born in Arles, France, in 1875, before the Eiffel Tower was built, and said she had met the painter Vincent van Gogh as a girl. She took up fencing at 85, rode a bicycle until 100 and lived to 122 years and 164 days, dying in 1997. Her age was carefully verified with records, making her the oldest person ever confirmed."],
g:[["Life expectancy","The average number of years a person can expect to live"],["Telomere","A protective cap at the end of a chromosome"],["Supercentenarian","A person aged 110 or older"],["Alzheimer’s disease","A brain disease that causes memory loss and confusion"]],
f:["Global life expectancy has more than doubled since 1900.","Jeanne Calment lived to 122, the oldest person ever confirmed.","Greenland sharks may live for over 250 years."],
fun:"Jeanne Calment, who lived to 122, took up fencing at age 85."});

;
/* data/arts2.js */
// Arts and Culture articles, part 2: cave art, architecture, photography, and comics and manga.
ADDART({id:"caveart",t:"Cave Art and Prehistoric Art",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🦬",w:"Cave painting",r:["davinci","indonesiacountry","francecountry","caves","cavebear","cavelion","megaloceros"],
p:[["The oldest art","Prehistoric people painted and carved images of animals, people and signs on cave walls tens of thousands of years ago."],["Around the world","Cave and rock art has been found on every inhabited continent, from Indonesia and Australia to Europe and Africa."],["Mysterious meanings","No one knows for sure why it was made, but it may have been linked to hunting, beliefs or storytelling."]],
l:"Prehistoric art includes paintings, engravings and carvings made by people before the invention of writing. The most famous are cave paintings, such as those at Lascaux and Chauvet in France and Altamira in Spain, which show bison, horses, deer and other animals in vivid detail. Even older art has been found in Indonesia, where a painted scene on Sulawesi is at least 51,200 years old. Prehistoric people also carved figurines, decorated tools and made jewelry from shells and beads, showing that art is one of the oldest human activities.",
s:[["How it was made","Painters used natural pigments: red and yellow ocher from iron-rich earth, black from charcoal or manganese, and white from chalk or clay. They applied them with fingers, brushes made of fur or plant fibers, or by blowing paint through hollow bones to spray outlines of their hands. They worked by the light of fat-burning lamps and torches."],["Hand stencils","Some of the oldest and most widespread images are hand stencils, made by placing a hand on the rock and blowing paint around it. They appear in caves in Indonesia, Argentina’s Cueva de las Manos, Spain, France and Australia. Studies of their size suggest that many were made by women and children as well as men."],["Animals in motion","European cave painters often showed large animals, such as bison, horses, mammoths, aurochs, lions and rhinos, sometimes using the bumps of the cave wall to give them shape and drawing extra legs to suggest movement. People are shown less often and usually more simply."],["Carvings and figurines","Prehistoric people also carved small figures from ivory, bone and stone. The Lion Man of Hohlenstein-Stadel in Germany, carved from mammoth ivory about 40,000 years ago, has a human body and a lion’s head. Small female figures, such as the Venus of Willendorf, are found across Europe."]],
k:[["Oldest known painting","Sulawesi, Indonesia, at least 51,200 years old"],["Famous caves","Lascaux, Chauvet (France); Altamira (Spain)"],["Main pigments","Ocher, charcoal, manganese"],["Oldest figurative carving","Lion Man, about 40,000 years old"],["Found on","Every inhabited continent"]],
d:[["c. 51,200 years ago","Painted scene with a pig and human figures in Sulawesi"],["c. 40,000 years ago","Lion Man figurine is carved"],["c. 36,000 years ago","Chauvet Cave paintings"],["c. 17,000 years ago","Lascaux Cave paintings"],["1879","Altamira paintings are discovered in Spain"],["1940","Teenagers discover Lascaux"]],
s2:[["Lascaux","In September 1940, four teenagers in southwestern France followed their dog, according to one account, into a hole and discovered Lascaux Cave, covered with about 600 painted animals and many engravings, made about 17,000 years ago. Visitors’ breath and lights damaged the paintings, so the cave was closed in 1963. Visitors now see detailed replicas."],["Chauvet","Chauvet Cave, discovered in 1994, contains some of the oldest and finest paintings in Europe, about 36,000 years old, including lions, mammoths, rhinos and bears. Because the entrance collapsed long ago, the cave was sealed and its art preserved almost untouched. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["Altamira","The paintings at Altamira in northern Spain were discovered in 1879 by Maria, the young daughter of an amateur archaeologist, Marcelino Sanz de Sautuola, who looked up and saw bulls on the ceiling. Experts at first refused to believe prehistoric people could paint so well, and accused Sautuola of forgery. They later admitted they were wrong."],["Indonesia’s ancient art","In the caves of Sulawesi and Borneo, scientists have dated paintings using mineral crusts that formed on top of them. In 2024 researchers reported that a scene on Sulawesi showing human-like figures and a wild pig is at least 51,200 years old, the oldest known figurative art and the oldest known painted story."],["Rock art of Africa and Australia","Africa has hundreds of thousands of rock art sites, such as the paintings of the San people in southern Africa and the engravings of the Sahara, some showing a time when the desert was green with giraffes and cattle. Aboriginal Australians have one of the world’s longest continuous traditions of rock art, still connected to living cultures."],["Why did they make it?","Many ideas have been suggested: hunting magic to bring success, religious or spiritual rituals, records of important events, teaching, storytelling or simply the human love of creating images. Since caves were often dark and hard to reach, many researchers think the art had a special meaning, but the full answer may never be known."]],
sp:["The oldest story","The painting on Sulawesi dated in 2024 shows three human-like figures interacting with a wild pig. Scientists think it may be the oldest known depiction of a story, more than 51,000 years old. Some of the figures appear to have animal features, suggesting that even at this early date, people imagined beings that were part human and part animal."],
g:[["Prehistoric","From the time before written records"],["Ocher","A natural earth pigment, red or yellow"],["Stencil","An image made by spraying paint around an object"],["Figurative art","Art that represents real things, such as animals or people"]],
f:["The oldest known painting is at least 51,200 years old, in Indonesia.","Lascaux Cave was closed to visitors in 1963 to protect its paintings.","Altamira was discovered by a young girl in 1879."],
fun:"The Altamira cave paintings were discovered by a young girl who looked up at the ceiling."});

ADDART({id:"architecture",t:"Architecture",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"🏛️",w:"Architecture",r:["gothic","pyramids","tajmahal","bridges","urban","cubism","artnouveau","artdeco","bauhaus","fresco","tinyhomes"],
p:[["The art of building","Architecture is the art and science of designing buildings and other structures."],["Styles through time","Each era and culture has created its own styles, from ancient temples to modern skyscrapers."],["Useful and beautiful","Good architecture combines strength, usefulness and beauty."]],
l:"Architecture is the art and science of designing and building structures, especially buildings that people live, work and worship in. Architects consider how a building will be used, how it will stand up, what materials to use and how it will look and feel. Architecture reflects the climate, technology, beliefs and values of each society, from the pyramids of Egypt, Greek temples and Chinese palaces to Gothic cathedrals, Islamic mosques and the glass skyscrapers of modern cities.",
s:[["Strength, usefulness, beauty","The Roman architect Vitruvius, writing in the 1st century BCE, said that good buildings need firmitas, utilitas and venustas: strength, usefulness and beauty. These three ideas still guide architects today. A building must stand safely, serve its purpose well and please the people who use and see it."],["Ancient architecture","Ancient Egyptians built pyramids and temples of stone. The Greeks developed the column orders, Doric, Ionic and Corinthian, seen in temples such as the Parthenon. The Romans used arches, vaults, domes and concrete to build aqueducts, amphitheaters and the Pantheon, whose dome remains the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world."],["Architecture around the world","Chinese builders created wooden palaces and temples with curved tile roofs, such as Beijing’s Forbidden City. Islamic architecture is known for domes, arches, minarets and geometric decoration, as in the Alhambra in Spain. Hindu and Buddhist temples, such as Angkor Wat and Borobudur, represent sacred mountains."],["Medieval to modern","Gothic cathedrals rose with pointed arches and stained glass. The Renaissance revived classical forms. In the 1800s, iron and glass made new buildings possible, such as London’s Crystal Palace. In the 1900s, steel frames, reinforced concrete and elevators led to skyscrapers and modernist styles that rejected decoration."]],
k:[["Classical principles","Strength, usefulness, beauty (Vitruvius)"],["Greek column orders","Doric, Ionic, Corinthian"],["Tallest building","Burj Khalifa, Dubai, 828 m"],["Top award","Pritzker Architecture Prize, since 1979"],["Key modern materials","Steel, reinforced concrete, glass"]],
d:[["c. 2560 BCE","Great Pyramid of Giza is completed"],["447–432 BCE","The Parthenon is built in Athens"],["c. 125 CE","The Pantheon in Rome is rebuilt with its great dome"],["1420s–1436","Brunelleschi builds the dome of Florence Cathedral"],["1885","First skyscraper, the Home Insurance Building, is completed in Chicago"],["2010","Burj Khalifa opens in Dubai"]],
s2:[["Domes","Domes have long been used to create vast, impressive spaces. Rome’s Pantheon, Istanbul’s Hagia Sophia, built in the 530s, and Florence Cathedral, whose dome was engineered by Filippo Brunelleschi without a full wooden support frame, were wonders of their time. The Taj Mahal and St. Peter’s Basilica are other famous examples."],["Skyscrapers","The first skyscrapers were built in Chicago and New York in the late 1800s, made possible by steel frames and safe elevators. Since 2010 the tallest building has been the Burj Khalifa in Dubai, 828 meters tall. Many of the world’s tallest buildings are now in Asia and the Middle East."],["Modern architecture","In the early 1900s, architects such as Le Corbusier, Walter Gropius of the Bauhaus school and Ludwig Mies van der Rohe championed simple forms, open plans and new materials, summed up in the phrase form follows function, coined by Louis Sullivan. Frank Lloyd Wright designed buildings in harmony with nature, such as Fallingwater."],["Architecture and climate","Traditional buildings are often well suited to their climates: thick mud walls keep homes cool in deserts, steep roofs shed snow, and houses on stilts with open walls, such as the bahay kubo of the Philippines, stay cool and dry in tropical rain. Today architects design green buildings that use less energy and water."],["Famous architects","Notable architects include Sinan, who designed great mosques for the Ottoman Empire; Antoni Gaudí, whose Sagrada Família in Barcelona has been under construction since 1882; Zaha Hadid, the first woman to win the Pritzker Prize in 2004; Tadao Ando of Japan; and Balkrishna Doshi of India."],["Building for people","Architecture shapes how people live and feel. Good design can make homes healthier, schools better for learning and cities more pleasant to walk in. Architects also design for disasters, such as earthquake-resistant buildings in Japan and typhoon-resistant shelters in the Philippines and Bangladesh."]],
sp:["Form follows function","The American architect Louis Sullivan, who designed early skyscrapers in Chicago, wrote in 1896 that form ever follows function, meaning that a building’s shape should grow from its purpose. The phrase became a motto of modern architecture. Critics later argued that buildings also need beauty, character and a sense of place, not just efficiency."],
g:[["Architect","A person who designs buildings"],["Column","An upright pillar that supports a structure"],["Dome","A rounded roof shaped like half a sphere"],["Modernism","A style that favors simple forms and new materials"]],
f:["The Pantheon’s dome is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world.","The Burj Khalifa is 828 meters tall.","Zaha Hadid was the first woman to win the Pritzker Prize."],
fun:"Gaudí’s Sagrada Família in Barcelona has been under construction since 1882."});

ADDART({id:"photography",t:"Photography",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"📷",w:"Photography",r:["camera","light","cinema","smartphone","popart"],
p:[["Writing with light","Photography means writing with light: making images by capturing light on a sensitive surface."],["Invented in the 1820s and 1830s","The first lasting photographs were made in France, and photography spread around the world within years."],["Everyone a photographer","Today, with smartphones, people take well over a trillion photos every year."]],
l:"Photography is the art and science of creating images by recording light, either chemically on film or electronically with a digital sensor. Since the first permanent photograph was made in the 1820s, photography has transformed how people record history, share news, make art, do science and remember their lives. It moved from slow, expensive processes to snapshots with portable cameras and, in the 21st century, to digital cameras built into almost every phone.",
s:[["The first photographs","Around 1826, the French inventor Nicéphore Niépce made the oldest surviving photograph from nature, a view from his window, which needed an exposure of hours or even days. In 1839 Louis Daguerre announced the daguerreotype, which produced sharp images on silver-coated copper plates. That same year, William Henry Fox Talbot in Britain revealed a process using paper negatives, from which many prints could be made."],["Photography spreads","Photography quickly became popular for portraits, replacing painted miniatures. Exposure times shortened from minutes to seconds. In 1888 George Eastman’s Kodak camera, sold with the slogan You press the button, we do the rest, made photography easy for ordinary people. Roll film, color film and small cameras followed."],["How a camera works","A camera is a light-tight box with a lens that focuses light onto film or a sensor. The aperture, an adjustable opening, controls how much light enters, and the shutter controls how long. In digital cameras, millions of tiny light-sensitive cells, or pixels, record the image as data."],["Photography as art","At first, many debated whether photography could be art. Photographers such as Alfred Stieglitz, Ansel Adams and Henri Cartier-Bresson showed its creative power through landscapes, portraits and street scenes. Today photographs hang in major museums, and photography is studied as an art form."]],
k:[["Oldest surviving photograph","Niépce, about 1826–1827"],["Daguerreotype announced","1839"],["First Kodak camera","1888"],["First digital camera prototype","1975"],["Word origin","Greek for writing with light"]],
d:[["c. 1826","Niépce makes the oldest surviving photograph"],["1839","Daguerre announces the daguerreotype; Talbot reveals his paper process"],["1861","First color photograph, by James Clerk Maxwell"],["1888","The Kodak camera is introduced"],["1975","Steven Sasson builds the first digital camera at Kodak"],["2000","First camera phones go on sale"]],
s2:[["Recording history","Photographs have recorded history since the 1850s. Mathew Brady’s team photographed the American Civil War, and later photographers documented both world wars, the struggle for civil rights and the Moon landings. Photojournalists risk their lives in war zones to show the world what is happening."],["Photos that changed opinion","Some photographs have changed the world. Images of child laborers by Lewis Hine helped bring in child labor laws in the United States. Dorothea Lange’s Migrant Mother showed the hardships of the Great Depression. Pictures of Earth from space, such as Earthrise, helped inspire the environmental movement."],["The digital revolution","In 1975 Kodak engineer Steven Sasson built the first digital camera, which weighed about 4 kilograms and took 23 seconds to record a black-and-white image. Digital cameras became common in the 2000s, and camera phones, first sold around 2000, made photography part of everyday life. Ironically, Kodak struggled to adapt and filed for bankruptcy in 2012."],["Photography in science","Photography lets scientists see what the eye cannot: high-speed photos freeze a bullet or a hummingbird’s wings, microscopes photograph cells, and telescopes capture faint galaxies. In 1878 Eadweard Muybridge’s photographs of a galloping horse proved that all four hooves leave the ground at once."],["Truth and manipulation","Photographs have been altered almost since photography began, from removing people in old political photos to editing images today with software. Now artificial intelligence can create realistic images of events that never happened. Learning to question where an image comes from has become an important skill."],["Selfies","The word selfie was named word of the year by Oxford Dictionaries in 2013. People have made self-portraits since the early days of photography; in 1839 Robert Cornelius took what is often called the first photographic self-portrait. Today billions of selfies are shared on social media."]],
sp:["Muybridge’s galloping horse","In the 1870s, people argued about whether a galloping horse ever has all four hooves off the ground. In 1878, Eadweard Muybridge set up a row of 12 cameras along a racetrack in California, each triggered by a thread as the horse passed. His photographs showed that all four hooves do leave the ground, with the legs tucked underneath. His work led toward motion pictures."],
g:[["Exposure","The amount of light that reaches film or a sensor"],["Aperture","The adjustable opening that lets light into a camera"],["Shutter","The part of a camera that opens and closes to let in light"],["Negative","An image with reversed light and dark, used to make prints"]],
f:["Photography means writing with light in Greek.","The first digital camera took 23 seconds to record an image.","Muybridge proved that a galloping horse lifts all four hooves at once."],
fun:"The first digital camera, built in 1975, weighed about 4 kilograms."});

ADDART({id:"comics",t:"Comics and Manga",c:"Arts and Literature",i:"💭",w:"Comics",r:["anim","novel","japancountry","fairy","manga","popart"],
p:[["Stories in pictures","Comics tell stories through sequences of drawings, usually with speech bubbles and captions."],["Many traditions","Comic traditions include American comic books, Japanese manga, Franco-Belgian bandes dessinées and the komiks of the Philippines."],["Read by millions","Comics and manga are among the world’s most popular forms of reading and inspire films, cartoons and games."]],
l:"Comics are a medium that combines images and words, arranged in panels, to tell stories or convey ideas. They range from short newspaper strips and superhero comic books to long graphic novels and Japanese manga series running for decades. Comics have rich traditions around the world, including manga in Japan, manhwa in Korea, bandes dessinées in France and Belgium, fumetti in Italy and komiks in the Philippines. Today digital comics, read on phones, are reaching new audiences worldwide.",
s:[["The language of comics","Comics use panels, boxes that show moments in a story, arranged in order. Speech balloons show what characters say, thought bubbles what they think, and captions add narration. The spaces between panels, called gutters, let readers imagine what happens in between. Lines and symbols show motion, sound and emotion."],["Early comics","People have told stories in pictures for thousands of years, from Egyptian wall paintings to medieval tapestries. Modern comics grew in the 1800s with illustrated stories, such as those by the Swiss artist Rodolphe Töpffer. Newspaper comic strips became popular in the United States in the 1890s."],["Comic books and superheroes","American comic books took off in the 1930s. Superman, created by Jerry Siegel and Joe Shuster, appeared in 1938, followed by Batman, Wonder Woman, Spider-Man and many more. Superhero stories became hugely popular and later inspired some of the highest-earning films ever made."],["Manga","Manga, Japanese comics, are read by people of all ages and cover every subject, from sports and romance to history and science fiction. Osamu Tezuka, creator of Astro Boy in the 1950s, is called the god of manga. Manga is usually printed in black and white and read from right to left. Series such as One Piece have sold hundreds of millions of copies."]],
k:[["Key elements","Panels, speech balloons, captions, gutters"],["Superman’s debut","1938"],["God of manga","Osamu Tezuka"],["Best-selling manga","One Piece, over 500 million copies"],["Philippine tradition","Komiks"]],
d:[["1830s","Rodolphe Töpffer creates early comic stories"],["1895","The Yellow Kid newspaper strip appears in New York"],["1929","Tintin first appears in Belgium"],["1938","Superman debuts in Action Comics #1"],["1952","Osamu Tezuka’s Astro Boy begins"],["1992","Maus becomes the first graphic novel to win a Pulitzer Prize"]],
s2:[["Franco-Belgian comics","In France and Belgium, comics are called bandes dessinées and are considered the ninth art. Hergé’s Tintin, first published in 1929, and René Goscinny and Albert Uderzo’s Asterix, from 1959, are loved worldwide. The Angoulême International Comics Festival in France is one of the largest in the world."],["Komiks of the Philippines","The Philippines has a strong komiks tradition. Artists such as Francisco V. Coching and Mars Ravelo created popular characters, including Ravelo’s Darna, a heroine who first appeared in 1947 under the name Varga and as Darna from 1950, and who became the star of many films and television series. Komiks magazines were widely read from the 1940s to the 1990s."],["Graphic novels","Graphic novels are long, book-length comics, often with serious themes. Art Spiegelman’s Maus, which tells of his father’s survival of the Holocaust with Jews drawn as mice and Nazis as cats, won a special Pulitzer Prize in 1992. Marjane Satrapi’s Persepolis described growing up in Iran during the Islamic Revolution."],["Webtoons","In the 2000s, Korean artists developed webtoons, digital comics designed to be read by scrolling down on a phone. They spread worldwide through apps and have been adapted into popular television dramas and films. Digital platforms now let artists from any country publish their work."],["Comics and education","Comics are used in schools to encourage reluctant readers, teach history and science and explain complex topics simply. Research suggests that combining words and pictures can help people understand and remember information. Many libraries now have large graphic novel collections."],["From page to screen","Comics and manga have inspired countless films, animated series and games. Anime adaptations of manga, such as Dragon Ball, Naruto and Demon Slayer, are watched worldwide, and films based on superhero comics are among the highest-grossing ever. Many comic artists now design with adaptation in mind."]],
sp:["Reading right to left","Traditional Japanese books are read from right to left, so most manga printed in English and other languages keeps this order: readers start at what Westerners would call the back of the book and read the panels from right to left. Early translations flipped the pages into a mirror image, but fans preferred the original layout, which keeps the artist’s design."],
g:[["Panel","A single frame in a comic"],["Speech balloon","A shape containing a character’s words"],["Manga","Japanese comics"],["Graphic novel","A long, book-length comic"]],
f:["Superman first appeared in 1938.","Manga is usually read from right to left.","Maus was the first graphic novel to win a Pulitzer Prize."],
fun:"In France and Belgium, comics are called the ninth art."});

;
/* data/earth2.js */
// Planet Earth articles, part 2: ice ages and glaciers, caves, and wetlands and mangroves.
ADDART({id:"iceage",t:"Ice Ages and Glaciers",c:"Geography",i:"🏔️",w:"Ice age",r:["antarc","arctic","climchange","greenland","alps","glaciers","snowballearth","fjords"],
p:[["Rivers of ice","Glaciers are huge masses of ice that slowly flow downhill or spread outward under their own weight."],["Frozen worlds","During ice ages, vast ice sheets covered much of North America, Europe and Asia."],["Shaping the land","Glaciers carved valleys, lakes and fjords and left behind boulders and hills."]],
l:"A glacier is a large, long-lasting body of ice formed from compacted snow that slowly moves under its own weight. Glaciers today cover about 10 percent of Earth’s land, mostly in Antarctica and Greenland, and hold about 69 percent of the world’s fresh water. During ice ages, the most recent of which peaked about 20,000 years ago, ice sheets several kilometers thick covered large parts of North America, Europe and Asia. Today most glaciers are shrinking as the climate warms.",
s:[["How glaciers form","Where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer, snow builds up year after year. The weight of new snow squeezes the layers below into dense ice. When the ice becomes thick enough, usually tens of meters, it begins to flow slowly downhill or outward, like very thick syrup."],["Types of glaciers","Mountain or valley glaciers flow down valleys from high mountains, as in the Alps, Himalaya and Andes. Ice sheets are enormous glaciers covering whole landmasses; only two exist today, in Antarctica and Greenland. Where glaciers reach the sea, they break off icebergs in a process called calving."],["Carving the land","As glaciers move, they scrape the ground with rocks frozen into their base, carving U-shaped valleys, sharp peaks such as the Matterhorn, and fjords where the sea fills deep glacial valleys, as in Norway, Chile and New Zealand. They carry rocks long distances and leave them behind as moraines and giant boulders called erratics."],["Ice ages","Earth has had several major ice ages over its history. The current one began about 2.6 million years ago and includes colder periods, called glacials, and warmer ones, called interglacials. We are living in an interglacial that began about 11,700 years ago. In the last glacial maximum, sea levels were about 120 meters lower than today."]],
k:[["Land covered by glaciers","About 10%"],["Share of fresh water in ice","About 69%"],["Last glacial maximum","About 20,000 years ago"],["Sea level then","About 120 m lower"],["Ice sheets today","Antarctica and Greenland"]],
d:[["c. 2.6 million years ago","Current ice age begins"],["c. 20,000 years ago","Last glacial maximum"],["c. 11,700 years ago","Current warm interglacial begins"],["1837","Louis Agassiz proposes past ice ages"],["1920s","Milutin Milanković explains ice age cycles"],["2019","Okjökull in Iceland becomes the first glacier lost to warming to get a memorial"]],
s2:[["What causes ice ages?","Ice age cycles are triggered mainly by slow changes in Earth’s orbit and tilt, which alter how much sunlight reaches different parts of the planet over tens of thousands of years. The Serbian scientist Milutin Milanković worked this out in the early 1900s. Changes in greenhouse gases, ice cover and ocean currents amplify the effects."],["Life in the Ice Age","During the last glacial period, woolly mammoths, woolly rhinos, cave lions, giant deer and saber-toothed cats roamed. Early humans adapted to the cold with clothing, fire and shelters. Lower sea levels created land bridges, such as Beringia between Asia and North America, which people crossed into the Americas."],["Ice cores","Scientists drill deep into ice sheets to extract ice cores. Bubbles of ancient air trapped in the ice show the levels of carbon dioxide and temperature going back about 800,000 years in Antarctica. They show that carbon dioxide levels today are far higher than at any time in that period."],["Shrinking glaciers","Nearly all of the world’s mountain glaciers are shrinking because of global warming, and their melting adds to sea level rise. In 2019 Icelanders held a funeral for Okjökull, which had lost its status as a glacier, and placed a plaque addressed to the future. The United Nations declared 2025 the International Year of Glaciers’ Preservation."],["Water towers","Mountain glaciers act as water towers, storing water in winter and releasing it in summer. Rivers fed by Himalayan glaciers, such as the Indus, Ganges and Brahmaputra, supply water to hundreds of millions of people. As glaciers shrink, rivers may at first carry more meltwater, then less, threatening water supplies."],["Snowball Earth","Some scientists think that hundreds of millions of years ago Earth may have frozen over almost completely, from the poles to near the equator, a state called Snowball Earth. Volcanoes slowly built up carbon dioxide until the climate warmed and the ice melted. Soon afterward, complex life began to flourish."]],
sp:["A letter to the future","In August 2019, scientists and residents climbed a mountain in western Iceland to mark the loss of Okjökull, a glacier that had melted so much it no longer moved. They placed a plaque reading, in part: In the next 200 years all our glaciers are expected to follow the same path. This monument is to acknowledge that we know what is happening and what needs to be done. Only you know if we did it."],
g:[["Glacier","A large, slow-moving mass of ice"],["Ice sheet","A glacier covering a huge area of land"],["Fjord","A deep inlet of the sea carved by a glacier"],["Moraine","Rocks and soil deposited by a glacier"]],
f:["Glaciers hold about 69 percent of the world’s fresh water.","During the last ice age, sea levels were about 120 meters lower.","Iceland held a funeral for a glacier in 2019."],
fun:"Antarctic ice cores contain air bubbles trapped up to about 800,000 years ago."});

ADDART({id:"caves",t:"Caves",c:"Geography",i:"🕳️",w:"Cave",r:["caveart","volc","bat","vietnamcountry"],
p:[["Hidden worlds","Caves are natural underground spaces large enough for a person to enter."],["Carved by water","Most large caves form when slightly acidic water slowly dissolves limestone rock over thousands of years."],["Home to life and history","Caves shelter unique animals and hold clues to Earth’s past and early human history."]],
l:"A cave is a natural hollow in the ground large enough for a person to enter. Most caves form in limestone, as rainwater, made slightly acidic by carbon dioxide, seeps through cracks and slowly dissolves the rock, creating passages, chambers and underground rivers over hundreds of thousands of years. Other caves form in lava, ice or sea cliffs. Caves contain beautiful formations such as stalactites and stalagmites, are home to specially adapted animals and have preserved fossils and ancient art.",
s:[["How limestone caves form","Rainwater absorbs carbon dioxide from the air and soil, forming a weak acid. As it trickles through cracks in limestone, it dissolves the rock, widening the cracks into tunnels and caverns. Landscapes shaped this way, with caves, sinkholes and underground streams, are called karst."],["Stalactites and stalagmites","When water dripping into a cave loses carbon dioxide, it leaves behind tiny amounts of the mineral calcite. Over thousands of years, these build up into stalactites hanging from the ceiling and stalagmites rising from the floor. When they meet, they form columns. They usually grow only a few centimeters every thousand years."],["Other kinds of caves","Lava tubes form when the surface of a lava flow cools and hardens while molten lava keeps flowing beneath, leaving a tunnel. Sea caves are carved by waves pounding cliffs. Glacier caves form inside ice. Some caves form when acidic water rises from below, as at Carlsbad Caverns in the United States."],["Cave life","Caves are home to animals adapted to darkness. Bats and some birds roost in caves and fly out to feed. Deep inside, true cave animals, such as blind cave fish, salamanders like the olm of Slovenia, and pale insects and spiders, have lost their eyes and color but have heightened senses of touch and smell."]],
k:[["Largest cave passage","Hang Sơn Đoòng, Vietnam"],["Longest cave system","Mammoth Cave, USA, over 680 km mapped"],["Deepest known cave","Veryovkina Cave, Georgia, about 2,200 m"],["Main rock","Limestone"],["Study of caves","Speleology"]],
d:[["c. 1 million years ago","Early humans use fire in Wonderwerk Cave, South Africa"],["1790s","Settlers begin exploring Mammoth Cave in Kentucky"],["1994","Chauvet Cave is discovered in France"],["2009","Hang Sơn Đoòng is explored by a British-Vietnamese team"],["2018","Twelve boys and their coach are rescued from Tham Luang cave, Thailand"],["2018","Veryovkina becomes the deepest known cave"]],
s2:[["Hang Sơn Đoòng","Hang Sơn Đoòng, in Phong Nha-Kẻ Bàng National Park in Vietnam, is the largest known cave passage in the world. Discovered by a local man, Hồ Khanh, around 1990 and explored in 2009, some chambers are more than 200 meters high, large enough to hold a skyscraper. Parts of the roof have collapsed, letting in sunlight so that a jungle grows inside."],["The Tham Luang rescue","In June 2018, twelve boys from a youth football team and their coach became trapped by floodwater in Tham Luang cave in northern Thailand. After they were found by British divers nine days later, an international team of divers and rescuers brought all 13 out safely in a daring operation. A Thai former navy diver, Saman Kunan, died during the effort."],["Caves and early humans","Caves have preserved evidence of early humans, including fossils, tools and art. In South Africa’s Rising Star cave, scientists found remains of Homo naledi. In Callao Cave in the Philippines, fossils of a small human species named Homo luzonensis, described in 2019, are at least 50,000 years old."],["Mammoth Cave","Mammoth Cave in Kentucky, in the United States, is the longest known cave system in the world, with more than 680 kilometers of mapped passages. Enslaved African Americans, such as Stephen Bishop in the 1830s and 1840s, explored and mapped much of it and guided early visitors."],["Caves and climate","Stalagmites grow in layers, like tree rings, and their chemistry records changes in rainfall and temperature over hundreds of thousands of years. Scientists use them to study ancient climates, such as monsoon patterns in Asia."],["Exploring caves safely","Caving, or spelunking, is exciting but dangerous: caves can flood quickly, be very cold and confusing to navigate. Cavers use helmets, lights, ropes and maps, travel in groups and tell others where they are going. Many caves are protected to preserve fragile formations and wildlife."]],
sp:["A jungle inside a cave","In Hang Sơn Đoòng, two places where the cave roof collapsed long ago, called dolines, let sunlight pour in. Beneath them, trees up to 30 meters tall grow on the cave floor, forming jungles with their own plants and animals. The cave also has clouds that form inside it and a river that runs through it."],
g:[["Karst","A landscape of caves and sinkholes formed by dissolving rock"],["Stalactite","A mineral formation hanging from a cave ceiling"],["Stalagmite","A mineral formation rising from a cave floor"],["Speleology","The scientific study of caves"]],
f:["Hang Sơn Đoòng in Vietnam is the largest known cave passage.","Mammoth Cave in the USA has over 680 km of mapped passages.","Stalactites hang down; stalagmites rise up."],
fun:"Hang Sơn Đoòng in Vietnam is so big that it has its own jungle and weather inside."});

ADDART({id:"wetlands",t:"Wetlands and Mangroves",c:"Geography",i:"🌾",w:"Wetland",r:["eco","climchange","frog","reef","borneo","herons","lotus","peatlands","willows","estuaries","waterhyacinth"],
p:[["Where land meets water","Wetlands are areas where water covers the soil or is near the surface for much of the year, such as marshes, swamps, bogs and mangroves."],["Rich in life","Wetlands support huge numbers of birds, fish, insects, amphibians and plants."],["Natural protectors","They filter water, prevent floods, protect coasts from storms and store large amounts of carbon."]],
l:"Wetlands are areas where water covers the soil, or is present at or near the surface, for all or part of the year. They include marshes, swamps, bogs, fens, floodplains, river deltas, lagoons and mangrove forests. Wetlands are among the most productive ecosystems on Earth, supporting a great variety of life. They also provide people with clean water, food, flood protection and protection from storms. Yet the world has lost a large share of its wetlands to drainage, farming and development.",
s:[["Kinds of wetlands","Marshes are dominated by grasses and reeds. Swamps have trees and shrubs. Bogs are spongy, acidic wetlands covered with peat moss. Mangroves are coastal forests of salt-tolerant trees that grow in tropical and subtropical tidal zones. Wetlands can be freshwater, salty or brackish, a mix of both."],["Mangrove forests","Mangroves grow along tropical coasts in Asia, Africa, the Americas and Oceania. Their tangled roots, some sticking up out of the mud to breathe, hold soil in place, slow waves and shelter young fish, crabs and shrimp. Indonesia has the largest area of mangroves of any country, followed by Brazil and Australia."],["Nature’s sponges and filters","Wetlands soak up rainwater and release it slowly, reducing floods and keeping rivers flowing in dry seasons. Their plants and soils trap sediments and absorb pollutants, cleaning the water. Coastal wetlands and mangroves act as buffers against storm surges and tsunamis."],["Wildlife","Wetlands are vital for migrating birds, which stop to rest and feed on long journeys. They are nurseries for many fish species, including those caught commercially. Frogs, turtles, crocodiles, otters, dragonflies and countless plants depend on them."]],
k:[["Types","Marshes, swamps, bogs, fens, mangroves, deltas"],["Wetland loss since 1970","About 35% (estimate)"],["Global treaty","Ramsar Convention (1971)"],["Largest tropical wetland","The Pantanal, South America"],["World Wetlands Day","2 February"]],
d:[["1971","Ramsar Convention on Wetlands is signed in Iran"],["1975","Ramsar Convention comes into force"],["1997","First World Wetlands Day is celebrated"],["2004","Indian Ocean tsunami shows the protective value of mangroves"],["2021","UN declares 2 February an official international day"]],
s2:[["The Ramsar Convention","In 1971, countries meeting in the Iranian city of Ramsar signed a treaty to protect wetlands, one of the first modern global environmental agreements. Today more than 170 countries have joined, and over 2,500 Wetlands of International Importance, called Ramsar Sites, have been designated, covering an area larger than Mexico."],["Famous wetlands","The Pantanal, mostly in Brazil, is the world’s largest tropical wetland and home to jaguars, capybaras and caimans. The Okavango Delta in Botswana floods each year in the middle of the Kalahari Desert, attracting elephants and other wildlife. The Sundarbans, shared by India and Bangladesh, is the largest mangrove forest and home to Bengal tigers."],["Disappearing wetlands","Wetlands have been drained for farming, cities, ports and aquaculture, such as shrimp ponds, or polluted. Studies suggest that about 35 percent of wetlands have been lost since 1970, faster than forests. Mangroves have also declined, though the rate of loss has slowed in recent years as countries protect and replant them."],["Blue carbon","Mangroves, salt marshes and seagrasses store large amounts of carbon in their soils, sometimes several times more per hectare than tropical forests. This is called blue carbon. When these ecosystems are destroyed, the stored carbon can be released, adding to climate change, so protecting them helps fight global warming."],["Protection from storms","After the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami, studies found that some villages behind healthy mangroves suffered less damage. In the Philippines, Bangladesh, Vietnam and other countries, communities are replanting mangroves to protect coasts from typhoons and rising seas, and to restore fisheries."],["Peatlands","Peatlands, such as bogs, cover only about 3 percent of Earth’s land but store about a third of all soil carbon, twice as much as all the world’s forests. When drained or burned, as in parts of Indonesia during dry years, they release huge amounts of carbon dioxide and smoke."]],
sp:["The Sundarbans","The Sundarbans, meaning beautiful forest in Bengali, covers about 10,000 square kilometers of mangrove forest in the delta of the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rivers in Bangladesh and India. It is the largest mangrove forest in the world and one of the last strongholds of the Bengal tiger, which swims between islands. Both countries’ parts are UNESCO World Heritage Sites."],
g:[["Wetland","Land covered by or saturated with water for much of the year"],["Mangrove","A salt-tolerant tree or forest growing on tropical coasts"],["Brackish","Water that is a mix of fresh and salt water"],["Blue carbon","Carbon stored in coastal and marine ecosystems"]],
f:["The Ramsar Convention to protect wetlands was signed in 1971.","The Sundarbans is the world’s largest mangrove forest.","Wetlands help prevent floods and clean water."],
fun:"Bengal tigers in the Sundarbans mangrove forest are strong swimmers, crossing rivers between islands."});

;
