// Technology articles, part 2.1: the wheel, the light bulb and 3D printing.
ADDART({id:"wheel",t:"The Wheel",c:"Technology",i:"⚙️",w:"Wheel",r:["cars","rail","mesop","bridges"],
p:[["An ancient invention","The wheel was invented about 5,500 years ago and changed transport, farming and industry."],["More than transport","Wheels are used in carts, pottery, mills, gears, clocks and countless machines."],["Wheel and axle","Joined to an axle, a wheel reduces friction and makes heavy loads easier to move."]],
l:"The wheel is a circular object that turns on an axle, one of the most important inventions in history. Potter’s wheels appeared in Mesopotamia around 4500 to 3500 BCE, and wheeled vehicles soon after, around 3500 BCE, in Mesopotamia, the Caucasus and central Europe. Wheels made it easier to carry heavy loads, led to chariots and carts and, through waterwheels, gears and pulleys, powered machines from mills to clocks. Today wheels turn in everything from bicycles and cars to turbines and hard drives.",
s:[["Why the wheel was hard to invent","A wheel on its own is not enough; it needs an axle, and the two must fit precisely: too tight and they jam, too loose and they wobble. Making smooth round wheels and axles required good tools and skill. That is one reason the wheel appeared thousands of years after farming began."],["Early wheels","The earliest vehicle wheels were solid disks made from planks of wood fastened together. One of the oldest surviving wheels, found in the Ljubljana Marshes in Slovenia, is about 5,150 years old. Pictures of wheeled wagons appear on clay tablets from the Sumerian city of Uruk and on a pot from Poland of similar age."],["Spokes and chariots","Around 2000 BCE, people in the steppes of Central Asia developed light wheels with spokes, much lighter than solid wheels. Spoked wheels made fast horse-drawn chariots possible, which spread across Asia, the Middle East, Egypt and Europe and changed warfare."],["Wheels at work","Wheels are not only for vehicles. Potter’s wheels let craftspeople shape round pots quickly. Waterwheels and windmills, using wheels with blades, ground grain and powered machines. Gears, toothed wheels that mesh together, transmit power and change speed in clocks, bicycles and engines."]],
k:[["Potter’s wheel","Mesopotamia, c. 4500–3500 BCE"],["First wheeled vehicles","c. 3500 BCE"],["Oldest surviving wheel","Ljubljana Marshes, Slovenia, about 5,150 years old"],["Spoked wheels","c. 2000 BCE"],["Key partner","The axle"]],
d:[["c. 4000 BCE","Potter’s wheels are used in Mesopotamia"],["c. 3500 BCE","Wheeled vehicles appear"],["c. 3150 BCE","The Ljubljana Marshes wheel is made"],["c. 2000 BCE","Spoked wheels and chariots develop"],["1845","Robert William Thomson patents the pneumatic tire"],["1888","John Boyd Dunlop develops a practical air-filled tire"]],
s2:[["The Americas","Peoples of ancient Mexico made small wheeled toys or figurines, but did not use wheels for transport. Without large animals to pull carts, and in many places with rugged land and dense forest, wheels were less useful than human porters, canoes and, in the Andes, llamas."],["Tires","Early wheels had rims of wood or iron. Air-filled, or pneumatic, rubber tires were patented by Robert William Thomson in 1845, and John Boyd Dunlop developed a practical version for bicycles in 1888. Pneumatic tires made rides smoother and helped bicycles and cars spread quickly."],["The wheel and the bicycle","The bicycle developed in the 1800s, from the wooden running machine of Karl Drais in 1817 to pedal-driven bicycles and the safety bicycle of the 1880s, with two equal wheels and a chain. It gave millions of people cheap personal transport and remains one of the most efficient ways to travel."],["Ball bearings","To reduce friction even more, engineers place ball or roller bearings between a wheel and its axle. Leonardo da Vinci sketched designs for bearings, and today they are used in everything from skateboards and fans to cars and jet engines."],["Giant wheels","Some wheels are enormous, such as Ferris wheels, first built for the World’s Columbian Exposition in Chicago in 1893, and today’s observation wheels like the London Eye and Ain Dubai. Huge wheels also turn in mining machines, ship propellers and wind turbines."],["Reinventing the wheel","The phrase reinventing the wheel means wasting time creating something that already exists. Yet engineers keep improving wheels, with lighter materials, airless tires, wheels for Mars rovers made of aluminum and titanium and wheels that can climb stairs."]],
sp:["The oldest wheel","In 2002, archaeologists in the Ljubljana Marshes in Slovenia found a wooden wheel and axle about 5,150 years old. The wheel, made of ash wood, is about 70 centimeters across, and the axle of oak. The axle turned together with the wheel, a design used on early two-wheeled carts. It is one of the oldest wooden wheels ever found."],
g:[["Axle","A rod on which a wheel turns"],["Spoke","A rod connecting the hub of a wheel to its rim"],["Friction","A force that resists movement between surfaces"],["Gear","A toothed wheel that meshes with another to transmit motion"]],
f:["The wheel was invented about 5,500 years ago.","One of the oldest surviving wheels is about 5,150 years old, from Slovenia.","Air-filled tires were patented in 1845."],
fun:"The potter’s wheel was probably invented before wheels were used on vehicles."});

ADDART({id:"lightbulb",t:"The Electric Light Bulb",c:"Technology",i:"💡",w:"Incandescent light bulb",r:["currents","light","bamboo","laser"],
p:[["Light at the flick of a switch","Electric light bulbs transformed homes, streets, factories and cities after the late 1800s."],["Many inventors","Thomas Edison and Joseph Swan made practical bulbs in 1879 and 1880, building on decades of work by others."],["From glow to LED","Today efficient LED lights have largely replaced old incandescent bulbs."]],
l:"The electric light bulb is a device that produces light from electricity. The first practical incandescent bulbs, in which a thin filament glows when heated by electric current, were developed around 1879 by Joseph Swan in Britain and Thomas Edison in the United States. Electric lighting changed daily life, letting people work, study and travel safely after dark. Over the next century, fluorescent lamps and then light-emitting diodes, or LEDs, made lighting far more efficient.",
s:[["Before electric light","For thousands of years, people lit the dark with fires, oil lamps and candles. In the 1800s, many cities installed gas lamps, burning gas piped through the streets. These sources were dim, smoky, smelly and a fire hazard."],["Early experiments","In 1802 Humphry Davy made platinum strips glow with electricity and later demonstrated a brilliant arc lamp. Arc lamps lit some streets and lighthouses, but were too bright and harsh for homes. Inventors spent decades trying to make a long-lasting incandescent bulb small and cheap enough for everyday use."],["Swan and Edison","In Britain, Joseph Swan demonstrated a carbon filament lamp in 1879, and his house was among the first lit by electric light. In the United States, Thomas Edison and his team tested thousands of materials and in 1879 produced a carbon filament bulb that lasted many hours; they later used carbonized bamboo. Edison and Swan eventually joined forces in Britain in a company called Ediswan."],["Lighting systems","Edison’s greatest achievement was creating a complete system: generators, wires, switches, meters and bulbs. In 1882 his Pearl Street Station in New York began supplying electricity to customers. Electric lighting spread quickly, though many rural areas and poorer countries waited decades for electricity."]],
k:[["Practical bulbs","1879–1880, Swan and Edison"],["Filament materials","Carbon, then tungsten from about 1910"],["First central power station","Pearl Street, New York, 1882"],["LED efficiency","Uses about 75–90% less energy than incandescent bulbs"],["Nobel Prize for blue LED","2014"]],
d:[["1802","Humphry Davy makes metal glow with electricity"],["1879","Swan and Edison demonstrate carbon filament bulbs"],["1882","Pearl Street power station opens in New York"],["c. 1910","Tungsten filaments become standard"],["1962","First visible red LED is developed"],["2014","Nobel Prize for inventing the efficient blue LED"]],
s2:[["Tungsten filaments","Carbon filaments were fragile and dim. From about 1910, bulbs used filaments of tungsten, a metal with a very high melting point, drawn into fine wires. Filling bulbs with inert gas instead of a vacuum made them last longer. Tungsten bulbs were standard for most of the 1900s."],["Fluorescent lamps","Fluorescent tubes, common from the 1930s, pass electricity through mercury vapor, producing ultraviolet light that makes a coating inside the tube glow. They use much less energy than incandescent bulbs. Compact fluorescent bulbs became popular in homes in the 1990s and 2000s."],["The LED revolution","Light-emitting diodes produce light when electricity passes through a semiconductor. Red and green LEDs existed for decades, but white LED lighting needed an efficient blue LED, achieved in the early 1990s by Isamu Akasaki, Hiroshi Amano and Shuji Nakamura, who won the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics. LEDs last many years and use far less energy."],["Banning inefficient bulbs","Because incandescent bulbs turn most of their electricity into heat rather than light, many countries, including the European Union, Australia, the Philippines and others, have phased them out in favor of efficient lighting, saving energy and reducing greenhouse gas emissions."],["Light pollution","Electric lighting has a downside: light pollution. Artificial light at night hides the stars, confuses migrating birds and newly hatched sea turtles, and may disturb human sleep. Shielded lights that point downward and warmer colors can reduce these effects."],["Light for everyone","Even today, hundreds of millions of people, mostly in sub-Saharan Africa and rural Asia, have no electricity at home. Cheap solar-powered LED lamps are replacing kerosene lamps in many homes, providing cleaner, safer light for studying and working."]],
sp:["The light that never goes out","In a fire station in Livermore, California, a hand-blown carbon filament bulb has been glowing almost continuously since 1901. Called the Centennial Light, it gives off only about 4 watts of dim light, but has been recognized as the longest-lasting light bulb. It even has its own webcam."],
g:[["Filament","A thin wire that glows when electric current flows through it"],["Incandescent","Giving off light because of heat"],["LED","Light-emitting diode, a semiconductor that gives off light"],["Light pollution","Excess artificial light at night"]],
f:["Swan and Edison both developed practical light bulbs around 1879.","LEDs use about 75 to 90 percent less energy than incandescent bulbs.","A light bulb in California has been glowing since 1901."],
fun:"A light bulb in a Californian fire station has been glowing since 1901."});

ADDART({id:"printing3d",t:"3D Printing",c:"Technology",i:"🖨️",w:"3D printing",r:["print","robot","comp","ai"],
p:[["Building layer by layer","3D printing makes solid objects by adding material one thin layer at a time, following a digital design."],["From toys to rockets","It is used to make prototypes, tools, medical implants, houses and even rocket engine parts."],["Making at home","Affordable desktop 3D printers let schools, hobbyists and small businesses make their own objects."]],
l:"3D printing, also called additive manufacturing, is a way of making three-dimensional objects by building them up layer by layer from a digital model. Unlike traditional methods that cut material away or pour it into molds, 3D printing adds material only where it is needed. Invented in the 1980s, it is now used in engineering, medicine, aerospace, architecture, fashion and education. Printers can work with plastics, metals, ceramics, concrete, resins and even living cells.",
s:[["How it works","First, a designer creates a 3D model on a computer or scans an existing object. Software slices the model into hundreds or thousands of thin horizontal layers. The printer then builds the object one layer at a time, each bonded to the layer below, until the whole object is complete."],["Kinds of 3D printing","The most common desktop printers melt plastic filament and squeeze it through a hot nozzle, like a precise glue gun. Stereolithography uses a laser or light to harden liquid resin. Other printers fuse powders of plastic or metal with lasers, or spray binding agents onto powder."],["Invention","In 1984 Chuck Hull in the United States invented stereolithography, patenting the first 3D printing process and founding the company 3D Systems. Similar ideas were developed by Hideo Kodama in Japan and others. Early machines were expensive and used mainly by industry to make prototypes."],["Desktop printers","When key patents expired in the late 2000s, open-source projects such as RepRap, a printer designed to print many of its own parts, helped launch cheap desktop printers. Schools, libraries and makerspaces now use them to teach design, engineering and creativity."]],
k:[["Also called","Additive manufacturing"],["Invented","1984, stereolithography by Chuck Hull"],["Common materials","Plastics, resins, metals, concrete"],["Desktop boom","Late 2000s onward"],["Uses","Prototypes, medicine, aerospace, construction, education"]],
d:[["1981","Hideo Kodama describes a method of building layers with light"],["1984","Chuck Hull files a patent for stereolithography"],["2005","The RepRap project begins"],["2009","Key patents expire, cheap desktop printers spread"],["2014","A 3D printer is used on the International Space Station"],["2020s","3D-printed houses and rocket engines become reality"]],
s2:[["Medicine","Doctors use 3D printing to make custom implants, such as hip joints and skull plates, shaped exactly to fit a patient. Surgeons print models of a patient’s heart or tumor to plan operations. Dental crowns, hearing aids and prosthetic limbs are often printed. Scientists are experimenting with printing living tissues, called bioprinting."],["Space","In 2014 a 3D printer was used on the International Space Station to make tools, so astronauts could make spare parts instead of waiting for a delivery from Earth. Rocket companies print complex engine parts in metal, which are lighter and made of fewer pieces."],["Houses","Large printers can squeeze out layers of concrete to build the walls of houses in days. Printed homes have been built in countries including Mexico, the Netherlands, the United States, the United Arab Emirates and India, and they are being tested as a way to provide affordable housing."],["Prototypes and spare parts","Companies use 3D printing to make prototypes quickly and cheaply, testing designs before mass production. It is also used to make spare parts for old machines, cars and aircraft that are no longer produced, and custom tools on factory floors."],["Limits","3D printing is usually slower than mass production for large numbers of identical items, and printed parts can be weaker than molded or forged ones. Many materials and machines are still expensive. For now, it is best for complex, custom or small-batch objects."],["Concerns","3D printing raises some concerns, such as the printing of untraceable weapons, the copying of copyrighted designs and plastic waste from failed prints. Governments and companies are discussing rules, and researchers are developing recyclable and plant-based printing materials."]],
sp:["Printing in space","In November 2014, astronauts on the International Space Station used a 3D printer to make a small plastic part, the first object ever 3D-printed in space. Weeks later, engineers on the ground emailed the design for a ratchet wrench to the station, and it was printed there. Future missions to the Moon and Mars may print tools, parts and even buildings from local materials."],
g:[["Additive manufacturing","Making objects by adding material layer by layer"],["Filament","A thin strand of plastic used in many 3D printers"],["Prototype","An early model used to test a design"],["Bioprinting","Printing structures made of living cells"]],
f:["3D printing was invented in the 1980s.","Astronauts have 3D-printed tools in space.","Whole houses have been built with 3D printers."],
fun:"In 2014 a wrench was designed on Earth, emailed to space and 3D-printed on the International Space Station."});

;
