// Technology articles, part 2.2: canals, video games and cybersecurity.
ADDART({id:"canals",t:"Canals",c:"Technology",i:"🚢",w:"Canal",r:["ships","bridges","trade","egyptcountry"],
p:[["Man-made waterways","Canals are artificial waterways built for transport, irrigation or drainage."],["Shortcuts for ships","The Suez and Panama canals cut thousands of kilometers off sea voyages between oceans."],["Ancient and modern","People have dug canals for thousands of years, from ancient Mesopotamia to China’s Grand Canal."]],
l:"A canal is an artificial waterway constructed to carry boats, supply water for farming or drain land. Canals have been built since ancient times: the Grand Canal of China, begun in the 5th century BCE, is the longest in the world, and canals carried much of the freight of the early Industrial Revolution in Britain. Great ship canals, such as the Suez Canal, opened in 1869, and the Panama Canal, opened in 1914, transformed world trade by linking seas and oceans.",
s:[["Early canals","Ancient Mesopotamians dug canals to carry river water to their fields, making farming and cities possible. Egyptians built a canal linking the Nile to the Red Sea. In China, canals carried grain and goods between regions; the Grand Canal eventually stretched about 1,800 kilometers from Beijing to Hangzhou."],["Locks","Canals often cross hills and valleys. Locks raise and lower boats between different water levels: a boat enters a chamber, gates close, and water is let in or out until it matches the next level. The pound lock, with gates at both ends, was used in China by the 10th century and later spread to Europe."],["Canals and industry","In the 1700s and early 1800s, canals were the highways of the Industrial Revolution in Britain, carrying coal, iron and goods far more cheaply than roads. The Erie Canal in the United States, opened in 1825, linked the Great Lakes to New York City and helped it become a great port."],["The Suez Canal","The Suez Canal in Egypt connects the Mediterranean Sea with the Red Sea, letting ships travel between Europe and Asia without sailing around Africa. Built by a French company led by Ferdinand de Lesseps with the forced and paid labor of many Egyptian workers, it opened in 1869. Egypt nationalized it in 1956, leading to the Suez Crisis."]],
k:[["Longest canal","Grand Canal of China, about 1,800 km"],["Suez Canal opened","1869"],["Panama Canal opened","1914"],["Key device","The lock"],["Busiest ship canals","Suez, Panama, Kiel"]],
d:[["c. 486 BCE","Work begins on what becomes China’s Grand Canal"],["1825","The Erie Canal opens in the United States"],["1869","The Suez Canal opens"],["1914","The Panama Canal opens"],["1956","Egypt nationalizes the Suez Canal"],["2021","The ship Ever Given blocks the Suez Canal for six days"]],
s2:[["The Panama Canal","The Panama Canal crosses the narrow Isthmus of Panama, linking the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans. A French attempt led by de Lesseps failed in the 1880s, defeated by disease and difficult terrain. The United States completed the canal in 1914; tens of thousands of workers, many from the Caribbean, built it, and thousands died. Panama took full control in 1999."],["How the Panama Canal works","The Panama Canal uses locks to lift ships about 26 meters to Gatun Lake, an artificial lake, and lower them again on the other side. Each crossing uses millions of liters of fresh water from the lake. A wider set of locks opened in 2016 for bigger ships, and droughts in 2023 and 2024 forced limits on the number of crossings."],["The Ever Given","In March 2021, the giant container ship Ever Given, about 400 meters long, ran aground and blocked the Suez Canal for six days, holding up hundreds of ships and disrupting global trade. Diggers, tugboats and high tides finally freed it. The event showed how much world trade depends on a few narrow waterways."],["City canals","Some cities are famous for their canals, which serve as streets. Venice in Italy has about 150 canals crossed by around 400 bridges. Amsterdam’s ring of canals dates from the 1600s. Bangkok, once called the Venice of the East, was crisscrossed by canals called khlongs, some still used by boats today."],["Irrigation canals","Many canals carry water for farming. Ancient irrigation canals helped civilizations grow in Mesopotamia, Egypt, Peru and the American Southwest. Today, huge irrigation canals in countries such as Pakistan, India, Egypt and the United States water millions of hectares of crops."],["Canals today","Many old transport canals in Britain and Europe were abandoned when railways and roads took over, but are now restored for leisure boating, walking and wildlife. New canals and the widening of old ones continue to be debated, balancing the benefits for trade against costs to the environment and local communities."]],
sp:["Falkirk Wheel","Some canals lift boats with ingenious machines instead of locks. The Falkirk Wheel in Scotland, opened in 2002, is the world’s only rotating boat lift. Two huge arms carry water-filled gondolas holding boats, turning to raise them about 24 meters between two canals in a few minutes, using about as much energy as boiling a few kettles."],
g:[["Canal","An artificial waterway"],["Lock","A chamber with gates that raises or lowers boats between water levels"],["Isthmus","A narrow strip of land joining two larger areas"],["Irrigation","Supplying water to land for crops"]],
f:["China’s Grand Canal is the longest canal in the world.","The Suez Canal opened in 1869 and the Panama Canal in 1914.","The Falkirk Wheel is the world’s only rotating boat lift."],
fun:"In 2021 a single ship blocked the Suez Canal for six days."});

ADDART({id:"videogames",t:"Video Games",c:"Technology",i:"🎮",w:"Video game",r:["comp","chip","ai","anim","escaperooms","lootboxes","emulators","speedrunning","pixelart"],
p:[["Play on a screen","Video games are interactive games played on computers, consoles, phones and arcade machines."],["A giant industry","Video games earn more money each year than films and recorded music combined."],["Billions of players","More than 3 billion people around the world play video games."]],
l:"A video game is an electronic game in which players interact with images on a screen using controllers, keyboards, touchscreens or motion sensors. Video games began as experiments in university and government laboratories in the 1950s and 1960s, became popular with arcade machines and home consoles in the 1970s and 1980s, and are now one of the world’s largest entertainment industries. They range from simple puzzles to vast online worlds, and are also used for education, training, art and competitive sport.",
s:[["Early games","In 1958 physicist William Higinbotham created Tennis for Two on an oscilloscope at a US laboratory. In 1962 students at MIT made Spacewar!, a space battle game for an early computer. In 1972 Atari released the arcade game Pong, a simple table tennis game that became a huge hit and launched the video game industry."],["The golden age of arcades","In the late 1970s and early 1980s, arcade games such as Space Invaders from Japan in 1978, Pac-Man in 1980 and Donkey Kong in 1981 drew crowds to arcades worldwide. Home consoles, such as the Atari 2600, brought games into living rooms."],["Consoles","After a market crash in 1983, Nintendo revived home gaming with the Nintendo Entertainment System and games such as Super Mario Bros. Rival companies such as Sega, Sony with the PlayStation and Microsoft with the Xbox followed. Handheld consoles, such as the Game Boy in 1989, let people play anywhere."],["Mobile and online games","Smartphones turned billions of people into gamers. Games can be downloaded instantly, and many are free to play. Online games let players around the world play together; some, such as Minecraft, are among the best-selling games of all time, with more than 300 million copies sold."]],
k:[["Players worldwide","More than 3 billion"],["First arcade hit","Pong (1972)"],["Best-selling game","Minecraft, over 300 million copies"],["Main platforms","Consoles, computers, phones"],["Esports","Professional competitive gaming"]],
d:[["1958","Tennis for Two is created"],["1962","Spacewar! is developed at MIT"],["1972","Pong and the first home console, the Magnavox Odyssey"],["1980","Pac-Man is released"],["1985","Super Mario Bros. is released in North America"],["2011","Minecraft is fully released"]],
s2:[["How games are made","Making a large game can involve hundreds of people over several years: designers, programmers, artists, animators, writers, musicians, sound engineers and testers. Game engines, software tools such as Unity and Unreal Engine, help developers build games. Small independent, or indie, studios also make many popular games."],["Esports","Esports are organized video game competitions, in which professional players and teams compete in games such as League of Legends, Dota 2 and Counter-Strike for prizes worth millions of dollars. Tournaments fill arenas and are watched online by millions. Esports were medal events at the 2022 Asian Games, held in Hangzhou, China, in 2023."],["Games and learning","Games can teach skills such as problem-solving, planning, teamwork and quick decision-making. Educational games teach math, languages and science, and simulations train pilots, surgeons and soldiers. Minecraft Education is used in classrooms worldwide to teach subjects from history to coding."],["Concerns","People worry about excessive gaming, violent content, spending money on in-game purchases and online harassment. The World Health Organization recognized gaming disorder as a condition in 2019, though it affects only a small share of players. Age ratings, parental controls and balanced habits help."],["Games as art","Many people consider video games an art form, combining storytelling, visual art, music and interaction. Museums such as the Smithsonian American Art Museum and the Museum of Modern Art in New York have held exhibitions of video games and added games to their collections."],["Technology pushed forward","Video games have driven advances in computer graphics, sound and chips. Graphics processing units, designed to render game graphics, later became the key hardware for training artificial intelligence. Game technology is also used in films, architecture and virtual reality."]],
sp:["Pong","In 1972, Atari placed its first Pong arcade machine in a bar in California. Within days, the owner called to complain that it had stopped working; when an engineer checked, he found the coin box so full of quarters that it had jammed. Pong’s simple game of bouncing a dot between two paddles started a worldwide industry."],
g:[["Console","A machine designed for playing video games"],["Arcade","A place with coin-operated game machines"],["Esports","Competitive professional video gaming"],["Game engine","Software used to build video games"]],
f:["More than 3 billion people play video games.","Pong, released in 1972, launched the video game industry.","Minecraft has sold more than 300 million copies."],
fun:"The first Pong machine stopped working because its coin box was too full."});

ADDART({id:"cybersecurity",t:"Cybersecurity",c:"Technology",i:"🔐",w:"Computer security",r:["comp","chip","crypto","ai","smartphone"],
p:[["Protecting the digital world","Cybersecurity is the protection of computers, networks, phones and data from attacks and theft."],["Many threats","Threats include viruses, scams, hackers, ransomware and data breaches."],["Everyone’s job","Strong passwords, software updates and caution online help keep people safe."]],
l:"Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting computer systems, networks, devices and data from unauthorized access, damage and theft. As more of life moves online, from banking and shopping to hospitals and power grids, cybersecurity has become vital for individuals, businesses and governments. Attackers range from individual criminals to organized gangs and state-backed groups. Defenders use technology, such as encryption and firewalls, as well as education, to stay one step ahead.",
s:[["Malware","Malware is malicious software designed to harm computers or steal information. Viruses spread by attaching to files; worms spread by themselves across networks; spyware secretly collects information; and trojans disguise themselves as useful programs. The Morris worm of 1988 was one of the first to spread widely across the early internet."],["Phishing and scams","Phishing tricks people into revealing passwords, bank details or personal information through fake emails, messages or websites that look real. Scammers may pretend to be a bank, a delivery company or a friend. Phishing is one of the most common ways attacks begin, because it targets people rather than machines."],["Ransomware","Ransomware locks or encrypts a victim’s files and demands payment to unlock them. Attacks have hit hospitals, schools, cities and companies. In 2017 the WannaCry ransomware spread to hundreds of thousands of computers in about 150 countries, disrupting hospitals in Britain and businesses worldwide."],["Encryption","Encryption scrambles data so that only someone with the right key can read it. It protects online banking, shopping, messages and passwords. Websites using HTTPS, shown by a padlock in the browser, encrypt the information sent between a user and the site."]],
k:[["Main threats","Malware, phishing, ransomware, data breaches"],["Key defenses","Encryption, updates, strong passwords, multi-factor authentication"],["Early internet worm","Morris worm, 1988"],["Major ransomware attack","WannaCry, 2017"],["Cybersecurity Awareness Month","October in many countries"]],
d:[["1971","Creeper, an early self-copying program, spreads on ARPANET"],["1988","The Morris worm infects thousands of computers"],["2000","The ILOVEYOU worm spreads worldwide by email"],["2010","Stuxnet, a cyberweapon targeting nuclear equipment, is discovered"],["2017","WannaCry ransomware attack"],["2024","A faulty security update causes worldwide computer outages"]],
s2:[["Staying safe online","Experts recommend using long, unique passwords or a password manager, turning on multi-factor authentication, which asks for a second proof of identity such as a code on your phone, keeping software updated, being cautious with links and attachments and backing up important files."],["Hackers","The word hacker originally meant a skilled programmer. Today it often refers to someone who breaks into computer systems. Ethical hackers, or white hats, are hired to find weaknesses before criminals do, and companies offer bug bounties, rewards for reporting security flaws."],["Attacks on infrastructure","Cyberattacks can target power grids, water systems, hospitals and transport. In 2010 Stuxnet, malicious code widely believed to have been created by state agencies, damaged centrifuges in Iran’s nuclear program. Protecting critical infrastructure is a major concern for governments."],["Data breaches","A data breach happens when attackers steal large amounts of personal information, such as names, passwords, card numbers or health records, from companies or governments. Some breaches have exposed the data of hundreds of millions or even billions of accounts. Laws in many countries require organizations to protect data and report breaches."],["AI and cybersecurity","Artificial intelligence is used on both sides: attackers use it to write convincing scam messages, create deepfake voices and find weaknesses, while defenders use it to detect unusual activity and respond to threats faster. Cybersecurity experts expect AI to change the field greatly."],["Careers in cybersecurity","There is a worldwide shortage of cybersecurity professionals. Jobs include security analysts, who watch for attacks, penetration testers, who try to break in to find weaknesses, cryptographers and digital forensics investigators. Many countries run competitions to find and train young talent."]],
sp:["The ILOVEYOU worm","In May 2000, millions of people received an email with the subject line ILOVEYOU and an attachment called LOVE-LETTER-FOR-YOU. Opening it released a worm that overwrote files and emailed itself to everyone in the victim’s address book. It spread around the world in hours, infecting tens of millions of computers and causing billions of dollars in damage. It was traced to a student in the Philippines; at the time the country had no law against it, and soon passed one."],
g:[["Malware","Software designed to harm or exploit computers"],["Phishing","Tricking people into revealing information through fake messages"],["Ransomware","Malware that locks files and demands payment"],["Multi-factor authentication","Logging in with more than one proof of identity"]],
f:["Phishing is one of the most common ways cyberattacks begin.","WannaCry hit computers in about 150 countries in 2017.","Encryption protects online banking and messages."],
fun:"The ILOVEYOU worm of 2000 led the Philippines to pass a law against computer crime."});

;
